window.MODULES.CT417 = {"name":"Software Engineering III","evidence":"past-papers","exam":{"summary":["Duration 2 hours. Answer any 3 of 4 questions (the lecturer says: attempt all four and the best three count). Each question is 20 marks, total 60 converted to a percentage.","No MCQs. Every question is scenario-based (a company with a problem: a veterinary clinic, a delivery firm, a bank) and mixes short explanations, code/command writing and calculations.","Reported question shapes for the current exam: Q1 has 4 parts, Q2 has 3 parts, Q3 has 3 parts, Q4 has 4 parts. About 3 of the 4 questions come from weeks 1-6; roughly half of one question comes from the last lecture (week 12: SLI/SLO/error budget).","The paper is brand new each year: same flow as before, different content and different scenarios. Do not memorise last year's answers; learn the methods."],"structure":[{"label":"Q1 (20)","detail":"Part (a) = something from the Agile Manifesto (week 1: the 4 values and 12 principles). Rest = requirements and change management. \"Where necessary, state any assumptions\". Attributes of good requirements are flagged as important."},{"label":"Q2 (20)","detail":"Maven directory structure / pom.xml / Spring Boot pom (the \"same question\" as the pom one), plus Git: list and explain Git commands (about 10 marks), or \"given this command, identify the problem and fix it\" (last year it was a merge issue and how to resolve it)."},{"label":"Q3 (20)","detail":"Docker: write/construct a Dockerfile (steps to construct) plus troubleshooting and Docker commands. GitHub Actions (workflow YAML) can also appear. Docker is a two-part question: part 1 Dockerfile, part 2 troubleshooting."},{"label":"Q4 (20)","detail":"(a) SCA / software security or JaCoCo; (b) API design / OpenAPI; (c) unit tests + TDD (you must write unit tests) and a \"what is wrong with this code\" critique worth about 5 marks; (d) calculations: SLI, SLO, error budget (heavily from week 12)."}],"freq":[{"topic":"CI/CD pipeline, GitHub Actions, pipeline improvements/pitfalls","count":6,"of":7},{"topic":"Security fundamentals (vulnerability, countermeasure, zero-day, buffer overflow)","count":5,"of":7},{"topic":"Design patterns / refactoring (factory, polymorphism, OCP)","count":4,"of":7},{"topic":"Git commands / merge conflicts","count":4,"of":7},{"topic":"SAST vs DAST, SonarQube, OWASP ZAP, code coverage","count":3,"of":7},{"topic":"Docker / virtualisation / troubleshooting a failed deployment","count":3,"of":7},{"topic":"Formal specification (Z notation) - older syllabus","count":3,"of":7},{"topic":"Agile Manifesto / principles","count":2,"of":7},{"topic":"MTTD / MTTR / MTBF calculation","count":2,"of":7},{"topic":"JUnit test cases (Calculator add)","count":1,"of":7},{"topic":"Maven / build process","count":1,"of":7},{"topic":"SCA / dependency scan prioritisation","count":1,"of":7}],"years":[{"year":"2019-20 (old syllabus)","themes":["Q1 git commit sequence (4), architectural styles (8), IaaS/PaaS/SaaS (3), style vs pattern (5)","Q2 design patterns + UML (8), component services, hypervisors/Docker (6), proxy errors","Q3 continuous software development system (6), kernels, plugin architecture","Q4 CAP/ACID/BASE, VirtualBox networking, layered architecture"]},{"year":"2021-22 (old syllabus)","themes":["Section A: Z formal specification (surjective function, train booking, supermarket, football)","Section B: Secure by Design (cookies, XSS, SQL injection, password hashing, dictionary attacks)"]},{"year":"2023-24 Semester 1 (answer all, 15 marks each)","themes":["Q1 Agile manifesto + 12 principles (Soccermatic)","Q2 Z spec of a terminal Cursor","Q3 RFC 2828 vulnerability/countermeasure, zero-day lifecycle, buffer overflow","Q4 CI advantages/pitfalls, JUnit tests for Calculator.add"]},{"year":"2023-24 Autumn (answer all, 15 marks each)","themes":["Q1 Agile principles for a CRM system","Q2 Z spec Cursor again","Q3 Maven vs Gradle benefits, features, build steps","Q4 CI/CD goals + challenges (Jenkins, Docker), buffer overflow"]},{"year":"2024-25 (any 3 of 4, 20 marks, CT417 lecturer)","themes":["Q1 FoodieGo CI/CD improvements (8), git commands for feature branch (5), explain a GitHub Actions YAML (7)","Q2 SAST vs DAST, why in pipeline, role of coverage, SonarQube slow, ZAP failing, low coverage","Q3 vulnerability, countermeasure, zero-day lifecycle, buffer overflow + countermeasures","Q4 design patterns, refactoring, polymorphism, Factory, Open/Closed"]},{"year":"2025-26 Semester 1","themes":["Q1 SkyBankAI: failed docker pull log (git+docker diagnose), design GitHub Actions pipeline, risky pipeline, merge conflicts","Q2 SAST/DAST, ZAP failures, SonarQube slow, coverage tool","Q3 definitions, MTTD/MTTR/MTBF average, buffer overflow","Q4 refactoring, design patterns, abstract User + Factory"]},{"year":"2025-26 Autumn","themes":["Q1 GitHub Actions YAML + failed manifest unknown: find cause, rewrite build step with sha + latest tags, docker commands on staging, 4 workflow improvements","Q2 SAST/DAST, ZAP, SonarQube, coverage (same shape)","Q3 SCA findings table (pick two to fix), remediation workflow, MTTD/MTTR from timestamps, C strcpy overflow","Q4 refactoring + patterns (same as Semester 1)"]}],"tips":["Spend about 36 minutes per question (120 min / 3) and about 1.8 minutes per mark. Attempt all four in the first hour if you can and finish the best three; the highest three count.","Always answer scenario questions in the scenario (name the clinic/bank, name its services). Generic answers lose the application marks.","Requirements/change questions: write \"Assumptions:\" first (2-3 bullets) then answer. This is explicitly rewarded.","When asked to write code (Dockerfile, pom, workflow, JUnit, git commands) write it line by line with a one-line reason per line. Marks go to each correct instruction and its explanation.","For \"identify the problem in this code\" give: the problem, why it is a problem (consequence), the fix, ideally a rewritten snippet. The lecturer says the flaws are design and intent flaws, not syntax errors.","For calculations show the formula, the numbers, the unit conversion, the result and a one-line verdict (SLO met or not). Method marks survive arithmetic slips.","Command questions: if you forget a command name, explain what it should do; \"explain git commands if you forget them\" earns partial credit."],"note":"Evidence is mixed. The three oldest papers (2019-22) and the two 2023-24 papers include topics no longer in the notes (architectural styles, Z notation, XSS and cookies, hypervisors). They are covered briefly in the low-priority \"legacy\" topic. The 2024-26 papers are the best guide to the the lecturer format, but the lecturer hints (below) override pure frequency: hinted topics are ordered first even where they never appeared in a past paper (requirements, Agile principles, Maven/pom, Git commands, API, TDD)."},"hints":{"source":"Reported by the student from the lecturer; unverified","likely":["Agile principles (Q1a: from the Agile Manifesto, week 1 slides)","Requirements and change management (Q1), \"where necessary, state any assumptions\", same as the checkpoint requirement exercise","Attributes of good requirements (Q1, flagged important)","Git commands (list and explain, 10 marks), merge conflicts, merge issues and how to resolve them","Given a command: identify the problem and how to fix it","GitHub Actions","Docker: write a Dockerfile (steps to construct), troubleshooting, Docker commands (this is the only Docker part)","Maven directory structure, pom.xml files, Spring Boot (same question as the pom question, Q2)","SCA security (Software Composition Analysis)","Unit tests (Q4c): you must write unit tests, JUnit","TDD (Q4c), JaCoCo, API / OpenAPI (Q4b)","SLI, SLO, error budget calculations (Q4d, lots of material from the last lecture)","Weeks 1-6 supply roughly 3 of the 4 questions; half of one question is from week 12"],"unlikely":["Agile vs DevOps comparison","What is a build","Monolithic architecture / microservices / software architecture in general","Monitoring, observability, Grafana, Prometheus (tools)","Definitions (pure definition questions)","Cyclomatic complexity formula","CI/CD as a concept","Week 7 content (architecture evolution)","Other security (no heartbeat etc.), DevSecOps (marked n?)","SDLC","Integration tests","Ticket lifecycles (Jira/GitHub issue lifecycle)","Questions exactly the same as last year: the paper is brand new, same flow, different content and scenarios"],"questionShapes":["Question formats: Q1 has 4 parts, Q2 has 3 parts, Q3 has 3 parts, Q4 has 4 parts; each question is 20 marks; answer 3 of 4 (answer all, best counts); no MCQ","Scenario 1 is about a veterinary clinic extending its existing software system","Q1: (a) Agile manifesto item, then requirements and change management with stated assumptions","Q2: Maven / pom / Spring Boot (the \"same\" question) and Git: last year was \"merge issue and how to resolve\"; this year expect \"given this command, identify the problem and fix it\"; one question asks you to list Git commands (10 marks)","Q3: Docker is two parts: part 1 Dockerfile (steps to construct), part 2 troubleshooting; remember the Docker commands","Q4: (a) SCA/security or JaCoCo, (b) API, (c) unit test + TDD (half a question) including a 5-mark \"what is wrong with this code\" (design/intent flaws, one-liners, functional Java), (d) SLI/SLO/error budget calculations","Everything on the exam has been covered in class except maybe one item; doing the checkpoints helps"],"notes":["CONFLICT FLAGGED: monitoring tools (Grafana, Prometheus) are reported unlikely, BUT SLI/SLO/SLA/error-budget calculations are reported very likely (Q4d, lots from the last lecture, week 12). Treat them as separate: skip tool detail, drill the calculations. This site has a dedicated high-priority topic for them.","Past papers 2023-26 asked vulnerability / countermeasure / zero-day / buffer overflow and SAST/DAST/ZAP/SonarQube almost every year, but the hints list \"other security\" and \"definitions\" as unlikely. Their topics are kept with full content but tiered low or possible; the SAST/DAST/coverage topics are kept as \"likely\" because three consecutive recent papers asked them and JaCoCo/SCA are hinted.","Code critique: the first sub-part is \"identify the problem in this implementation\", nothing syntactic. The lecturer writes many one-liners and uses functional Java (streams, lambdas, Optional) to trick you. Revise the code-critique topic and method.","Unit test question: you will have to write the tests yourself, so practise writing JUnit 5 tests by hand with assertions, Arrange-Act-Assert and a mock.","Topics flagged unlikely still have full content: the lecturer said everything was covered in class, so they can appear in short parts."]},"code":"CT417","topics":[{"id":"req-attributes","title":"Attributes of good requirements, user stories and FR/NFR","priority":1,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q1 (important); 0/7 past papers","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**A requirement says what the system must do, not how. A good one is clear, complete, needed, testable, doable, consistent and traceable.**","mnemonic":"U-C-N-V-F-C-T: Unambiguous, Complete, Necessary, Verifiable, Feasible, Consistent, Traceable (WHAT, not HOW)","source":["CT417_Notes_03_Requirements_Change_Management","CT417_Notes_01a_SE_Revision"],"sections":[{"heading":"What is a requirement","items":["A **requirement** is something the system must do or have, because someone needs it.","It can be a capability, a property or a limit.",{"type":"p","text":"Requests are easy to misread"},"\"Build a system to help TAs grade more efficiently\" hides many questions.","What counts as efficient? What does grading include?","Take it at face value and developers build the wrong thing.",{"type":"p","text":"Stakeholders"},"A **stakeholder** is anyone who affects the system or is affected by it: users, owners, regulators, support staff.","Missing one is like designing a house without asking who will live in it.","Each has different worries. Students fear unfair grades, TAs fear workload, instructors fear complaints.","The requirements must balance these."]},{"heading":"Attributes of good requirements (learn these for Q1)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Attribute","Meaning","Test question"],"rows":[["Unambiguous / clear","Only one possible interpretation; no vague words (fast, easy, user-friendly, efficient)","Could two developers read it differently?"],["Complete","Contains everything needed to design and test it (who, what, conditions, limits)","Is any information missing?"],["Concise","One requirement per statement, short","Does it say more than one thing?"],["Necessary","Removing it would leave a real need unmet; appropriate to the hierarchy level","What happens if we leave it out?"],["Verifiable (testable)","Can be proved by test, inspection, demonstration or analysis","How will we know it has been met?"],["Attainable / feasible","Can be achieved within technology, budget, time","Can it actually be built?"],["Consistent","Does not contradict other requirements (internally consistent)","Does it conflict with another?"],["Traceable","Linked up to a stakeholder need and down to design, code and tests","Where did it come from and where is it implemented/tested?"],["Solution-neutral (WHAT not HOW)","States the need, not the implementation, unless a constraint","Does it dictate a technology without reason?"]]},{"type":"note","text":"Wording from the slides, worth quoting: good requirements are concise, unambiguous, solution-neutral where appropriate, internally consistent, necessary, verifiable and attainable."},"They say **what**, not how.","They can be checked by a test or independent analysis.","They are necessary and pitched at the right level of the system.","They are feasible, consistent, and traceable to the requirements above and below."]},{"heading":"Functional vs non-functional requirements","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Functional (FR)","Non-functional (NFR)"],"rows":[["About","Product features: what the system does","Product properties: how well, the experience of the user"],["Examples","Receptionist can book an appointment","Booking page loads in under 2 seconds"],["Capture","Use cases, user stories, sequence diagrams, state machines","Global constraints, hard to capture in use cases"],["Traceability","Usually traced to one module/class/function","Usually cannot be implemented in a single module"],["Typical list","Create, read, update, delete, notify","Availability, maintainability, performance, privacy, reliability, scalability, security, usability"]]},"Turn a vague quality into something you can measure.","**Slide example**: \"With a 4-core server and 16 GB RAM the system should service at least 200 simultaneous clients with less than 300 ms latency.\"","**Vague**: \"The system should be fast.\"","**Measurable**: \"95% of search requests return in under 500 ms with 100 concurrent users.\""]},{"heading":"User stories","items":["A **user story** is a short sentence saying who wants what, and why.",{"type":"formula","label":"User story template","text":"As a <role> I want <capability> so that I can <get some benefit>"},"It fits on a small card. It may have prerequisites, and has a priority.","**Conditions of satisfaction** (acceptance criteria): testable behaviour, seen from the user's side.","They are numbered inside the story (1.1, 1.2) and each has a priority (E = essential, D = desirable).",{"type":"p","text":"Pothole example"},"Story 1: \"As a citizen I want to report potholes so that the town can do something about them.\" (E)","1.1: report a pothole (E).","1.2: see whether my pothole has been repaired (E).","1.3: see whether someone already reported it (D).","1.4: see the estimated repair time (D).","A good story is **INVEST**: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable."]},{"heading":"Worked example: rewriting a bad requirement (veterinary clinic)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Bad requirement: \"The clinic system shall be easy to use and quickly show a pet's history.\""},"\"Easy\" and \"quickly\" cannot be tested.","\"History\" is not defined.","It is two requirements stuck together.",{"type":"p","text":"Better"},"**Functional**: \"FR-12: A receptionist shall be able to retrieve all visits for a selected pet, ordered by date, from the pet record screen.\"","**Non-functional**: \"NFR-4: The visit list for a pet with up to 200 visits shall display within 2 seconds for 95% of requests with 20 concurrent users.\"","**Story**: \"As a vet I want to see a pet's previous visits so that I can avoid repeating treatments.\"","1.1: visits listed newest first.","1.2: an unknown pet gives a clear message.","1.3: the existing full visit list still works.",{"type":"note","text":"In the exam, state assumptions when the scenario is vague. For example: visits are stored with a pet id, reception uses the existing web screen, and 200 visits is a realistic maximum."}]},{"heading":"How to get complete requirements (elicitation)","items":["**Elicitation** means finding out what people need. One method is never enough.","**1.** Interview stakeholders (broad needs).","**2.** Watch real work (hidden complications).","**3.** Run workshops with several stakeholders (settle conflicts).","**4.** Build prototypes (check you understood).","**5.** Read policy documents (limits and rules).","**6.** Walk through detailed scenarios (find gaps).","**Warning signs**: only talking to managers, leading questions, using one method, missing quiet people."]}],"pitfalls":["Give at least six attributes, each with its meaning and a vet-clinic style example.","Say what, not how (\"use MySQL\" is how), unless it is a real constraint.","Avoid words you cannot measure, such as fast, secure or user-friendly.","State your assumptions. The lecturer asks for them."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Says WHAT, not HOW","items":["Unambiguous","Complete","Necessary","Verifiable"]},{"title":"And also","items":["Feasible","Consistent","Traceable"]},{"title":"Two types","items":["Functional","Non-functional"]}]}}},{"id":"agile-manifesto","title":"Agile Manifesto: 4 values and 12 principles","priority":2,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"2/7 papers (2023-24 x2) + hinted Q1a","marks":"4-9 marks","summary":"**The Agile Manifesto has 4 values and 12 principles. Question 1a asks you to describe it and apply 2 or 3 principles to a scenario.**","mnemonic":"Values: People, Software, Customer, Change (PSCC). Principles: Customer, People, Product, Reflect","source":["CT417_Notes_01b_Agile_DevOps"],"sections":[{"heading":"Agile in one paragraph","items":["**Agile** means building software in small working pieces.","You get feedback often, deliver often and adapt quickly.","The **Manifesto for Agile Software Development** was written in 2001.","It says: we are uncovering better ways of developing software by doing it and helping others do it.","Its key line: \"While there is value in the items on the right, we value the items on the left more.\""]},{"heading":"The four values","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Value (preferred)","Over","What it means in practice"],"rows":[["Individuals and interactions","processes and tools","Talk to people daily; tools support the team, they do not replace conversation"],["Working software","comprehensive documentation","A running increment is the measure of progress; document just enough"],["Customer collaboration","contract negotiation","Customer is part of the team, reviews each sprint; do not hide behind the contract"],["Responding to change","following a plan","Re-plan every sprint; changing requirements are welcome"]]},"**Slide quote**: \"The highest priority is to satisfy the customer through early and continuous delivery of valuable software.\""]},{"heading":"The twelve principles (learn all; the exam picks one or more)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["#","Principle (short)","Group"],"rows":[["1","Satisfy the customer through early and continuous delivery of valuable software","Customer"],["2","Welcome changing requirements, even late in development; harness change for competitive advantage","Customer"],["3","Deliver working software frequently, from a couple of weeks to a couple of months, preferring the shorter scale","Customer"],["4","Business people and developers work together daily throughout the project","Customer"],["5","Build projects around motivated individuals; give them the environment and support they need and trust them","People"],["6","Face-to-face conversation is the most efficient and effective way to convey information","People"],["7","Working software is the primary measure of progress","Product"],["8","Agile processes promote sustainable development: a constant pace indefinitely","People"],["9","Continuous attention to technical excellence and good design enhances agility","Product"],["10","Simplicity, the art of maximising the amount of work not done, is essential","Product"],["11","The best architectures, requirements and designs emerge from self-organising teams","People"],["12","At regular intervals the team reflects on how to become more effective, then tunes and adjusts its behaviour","Reflect"]]}]},{"heading":"Frameworks that implement Agile","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Scrum"},"Work in **sprints** of 2-4 weeks.","Meetings: sprint planning, daily stand-up, sprint review, retrospective.","**Product owner**: owns the to-do list (backlog) and priorities.","**Scrum master**: removes blockers and coaches the process.","**Development team**: builds it.",{"type":"p","text":"Others"},"**Kanban**: show work on a board and limit how much is in progress at once.","**XP (Extreme Programming)**: pair programming, test-driven development, continuous integration, small frequent releases.","**Lean**: cut waste, maximise value."]},{"heading":"Model answers to the past-paper shapes","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Shape 1: \"Describe the Manifesto and list TWO principles\" (4 marks)"},"One sentence on what it is.","The four value pairs.","Two principles, one line each. For example: welcome changing requirements (2) and deliver working software often (3).",{"type":"p","text":"Shape 2: \"Changing requirements cause delays. Pick one principle and give TWO solutions\" (6 marks)"},"Choose principle 2, welcome change.","**Solution 1**: keep a prioritised backlog and re-order it at each sprint planning, so new needs go into the next sprint.","**Solution 2**: short 2-week sprints with a demo, so wrong assumptions are caught early.","**Solution 3**: automated tests and continuous integration, so changes are cheap and safe.",{"type":"p","text":"Shape 3: \"Five steps using principle 4 to fix poor predictions\" (5 marks)"},"Principle 4: business people and developers work together daily.","**1.** Put a product owner in the team, available every day.","**2.** Agree together what \"accurate enough\" means, as a testable target (for example 70% match accuracy).","**3.** Stakeholders attend sprint reviews and try the model.","**4.** Add user concerns to the backlog as stories.","**5.** Review in a retrospective, adjust data and model, then measure again.",{"type":"p","text":"Shape 4: \"Three principles for XYZ, and how to apply them to its CRM project\" (6 + 9 marks)"},"**Customer collaboration**: the product owner reviews stories each sprint, and sales staff test each version.","**Responding to change**: re-prioritise each sprint. A new \"lead scoring\" request replaces a less valuable feature.","**Working software**: each sprint ends with something usable, such as contact search, shown at the review and shipped through CI/CD."]}],"pitfalls":["Apply each point to the scenario. Do not just recite.","Keep the 4 values (X over Y) separate from the 12 principles.","Do not write Agile vs Waterfall. The lecturer says 1a is about the Manifesto.","Agile does not mean no documentation. It values working software **over** heavy documentation."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"4 values (left over right)","items":["Individuals and interactions","Working software","Customer collaboration","Responding to change"]},{"title":"12 principles about","items":["Customer value","Working software","People","Continuous improvement"]}]}}},{"id":"req-change-management","title":"Requirements engineering, management and change control","priority":3,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q1 (checkpoint style); 0/7 papers","marks":"8-15 marks","summary":"**Requirements are gathered, written down, checked, then managed. Every change is recorded, assessed, approved, built, tested and traced. State your assumptions.**","mnemonic":"Capture - Clarify - Commit - Change-control - Trace (the 5 Cs of requirements management)","source":["CT417_Notes_03_Requirements_Change_Management"],"sections":[{"heading":"Requirements engineering process","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["Elicit (interviews, observation, workshops)","Analyse and negotiate conflicts","Specify (stories, criteria, NFRs)","Validate (prototypes, review)","Manage (change, trace, baseline)"],"direction":"row","loopBack":"requirements evolve, repeat"},"caption":"Requirements engineering is iterative, not a one-off phase."},"The first request is only a starting point, not the full requirement.","Keep the original request.","Then add the clarified requirement, the acceptance criteria and any open questions."]},{"heading":"Requirements management (what it is)","items":["Collect needs from the market, business strategy, customers and others.","Turn needs into requirements, and check the meaning with whoever asked.","Get management and the team to commit to them.","Keep a history of changes and **why** they were made.","Keep links both ways between requirements, plans and the things you build (**traceability**).","Find and fix any mismatches between them."]},{"heading":"Why we \"engineer\" requirements","items":["**Check they are met**: confirm early that the product covers the main requirements.","**Responsibility**: it is clear who builds each one.","**Visibility**: shows who has what, and where teams depend on each other.","**Assess change**: see quickly what a change affects.","**Support V&V** (verification and validation): gives data to check requirements and designs early."]},{"heading":"Traceability and change authority","items":["Requirements come in levels: stakeholder needs, then system requirements, then component requirements.","**Link upwards**: why does this exist?","**Link downwards**: where is it designed, coded and tested?","A **change authority** (a change board, or the product owner) decides if a change is accepted.","Developers do not just make changes informally.",{"type":"table","head":["Step","What happens","Output"],"rows":[["1 Raise","Stakeholder submits a change request (CR); original wording kept","CR recorded as an issue/work item"],["2 Clarify","Ask what, why, who, edge cases; write acceptance criteria and open questions","Clarified requirement"],["3 Impact analysis","Which requirements, components, tests, schedule, cost, risks are affected?","Impact report, estimate"],["4 Decide","Change authority approves, rejects, defers; set priority","Decision recorded with rationale"],["5 Implement","Branch, code, pull request with review","Change in code"],["6 Verify","Tests prove the criteria and existing behaviour still works (regression)","Test evidence"],["7 Update and trace","Update documents, link issue to PR and tests, release notes","Traceable history"]]}]},{"heading":"Readiness vs priority (lecture point)","items":["**Readiness** and **priority** are different things.","Readiness: raw request, then clarified, then ready to build.","Priority: high, medium or low.","A change can be urgent and still not ready.","**Ready** means: clear requirement, acceptance criteria, open questions solved or visible, an owner, and enough agreement to start.","Moving a card to \"Ready\" does not make the missing decisions. The tool only records the agreement."]},{"heading":"Tooling: Jira vs GitHub Issues + Projects","items":["**Jira**: for larger organisations with many teams, dependencies, roadmaps, permissions and reporting.","Its terms: project, epic, story/task, backlog, assignee, workflow, board, priority, acceptance criteria.","**GitHub Issues + Projects**: built around the code repository.","**Issue**: the detailed record (request, clarified requirement, acceptance criteria, discussion).","**Project**: the board or backlog, with status and priority.","**Pull request**: links the code to the issue.","The tool changes but the thinking does not. Pick a tool that fits the size of the job.","**Evidence chain**: Issue, then Pull Request, code, test, and proof (merge history, CI checks)."]},{"heading":"Worked example: PetClinic CR-02 (veterinary clinic)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"The request"},"\"Staff have to read the whole visit list by hand. They need one pet's visits, without breaking the current list.\"",{"type":"p","text":"Turn it into something buildable"},"**Requirement**: callers can get the visits for a chosen pet, and the full list still works as before.","**Criterion 1**: with a pet id, return only that pet's visits.","**Criterion 2**: with no pet id, return the full list, unchanged.","**Open question**: unknown pet, return 404 or an empty list?","**Open question**: badly formed pet id, return 400?","**Open question**: known pet with no visits, return an empty list?","This only adds an optional parameter, so it does not break existing users.",{"type":"note","text":"Assumptions to state: visit ids and pet ids are numbers, the existing endpoint must stay compatible, and the head vet approves the change."}]},{"heading":"Answer template for \"manage this change request\" (20-mark Q1 style)","items":["**1.** Assumptions (2-3 bullets).","**2.** Stakeholders and what each cares about.","**3.** The requirement as a user story, with acceptance criteria and non-functional requirements.","**4.** Check it is unambiguous, testable, consistent and traceable.","**5.** Change control: raise it, assess impact, authority decides, build on a branch with a PR, test, update the links.","**6.** Tools: issue, board, pull request, and Git history as the record."]}],"pitfalls":["Name who decides (change authority) and include an impact analysis step.","Do not forget traceability (issue, PR, tests) and the reasons for changes.","State assumptions when the question invites it.","Priority and readiness are not the same."]},{"id":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"SLI, SLO, SLA and error budget calculations","priority":4,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q4d (VERY likely, lots from last lecture)","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**SLI is what you measured. SLO is your target. SLA is the contract with penalties. The error budget is how much failure the target allows.**","mnemonic":"I measure, O targets, A contracts. Budget = (100 - SLO)% x window; SLI = (total - down) / total","source":["CT417_Notes_11a_Reliability_Monitoring"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions (the lecture)","items":["**SRE (Site Reliability Engineering)**: using software engineering to keep large systems reliable, fast and scalable.","It balances new features against stability.",{"type":"p","text":"The three letters"},"**SLI (Service Level Indicator)**: a measurement. For example uptime, response time, error rate or throughput.","**SLO (Service Level Objective)**: the target for that measurement, such as 99.95% uptime. It is your internal goal.","**SLA (Service Level Agreement)**: a contract with the customer. Miss it and you pay penalties, such as refunds.","The SLA is usually looser than the SLO, to give you a safety margin.",{"type":"p","text":"Error budget"},"How much failure is allowed in a time period.","Error budget = 100% - SLO.","Budget left: you can ship and take risks.","Budget used up: stop risky changes and work on stability.",{"type":"p","text":"Toil"},"**Toil** is boring, repeated manual work that could be automated.","It grows as the system grows, unless you cut it.","If toil takes all your time (\"engineering bankruptcy\"), nothing is left to improve things.","Less toil means more time for new ideas, better reliability and faster incident response."]},{"heading":"Formulas","items":[{"type":"p","text":"How reliable were we? (by time)"},{"type":"formula","label":"Availability SLI (time based)","text":"SLI % = (Total time - Downtime) / Total time x 100"},{"type":"p","text":"How reliable were we? (by requests)"},{"type":"formula","label":"Request based SLI","text":"SLI % = good requests / valid (total) requests x 100"},{"type":"p","text":"How much failure is allowed?"},{"type":"formula","label":"Error budget (percentage)","text":"Error budget % = 100% - SLO %"},{"type":"formula","label":"Allowed downtime","text":"Allowed downtime = (1 - SLO) x window length   (e.g. 0.001 x 43,200 min = 43.2 min)"},{"type":"p","text":"How much have we used?"},{"type":"formula","label":"Budget remaining / consumed","text":"Remaining = Allowed - Actual downtime;  Consumed % = Actual / Allowed x 100"},{"type":"formula","label":"Burn rate","text":"Burn rate = fraction of budget consumed / fraction of window elapsed = actual error rate / allowed error rate"},{"type":"formula","label":"Time until budget exhausted","text":"Time to exhaustion = window / burn rate (burn rate 1 = exactly on budget)"},{"type":"p","text":"Did we meet the target?"},{"type":"formula","label":"SLO met?","text":"Met if SLI >= SLO, equivalently Downtime <= Allowed downtime"},"**Units**: a 30-day month is 720 hours = 43,200 minutes = 2,592,000 seconds.","A year is 8,760 hours = 525,600 minutes."]},{"heading":"Reference table: allowed downtime (memorise the pattern)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["SLO","Error budget","Per 30 days (43,200 min)","Per 365 days (525,600 min)","Per 7 days (10,080 min)"],"rows":[["99%","1%","432 min = 7.2 h","5,256 min = 87.6 h","100.8 min"],["99.5%","0.5%","216 min = 3.6 h","2,628 min = 43.8 h","50.4 min"],["99.9% (\"three nines\")","0.1%","43.2 min","525.6 min = 8.76 h","10.08 min"],["99.95%","0.05%","21.6 min","262.8 min = 4.38 h","5.04 min"],["99.99% (\"four nines\")","0.01%","4.32 min","52.56 min","1.008 min"],["99.999% (\"five nines\")","0.001%","0.432 min = 25.9 s","5.256 min","6.05 s"]]},"**Shortcut**: each extra 9 in the target divides the allowed downtime by 10.","99.9% over 30 days is about 43 minutes."]},{"heading":"Worked example 1 (the lecture): uptime SLI","items":["Month: 30 × 24 = 720 hours. The service was down 1.44 hours.","SLI = (720 - 1.44) / 720 × 100 = 718.56 / 720 × 100 = **99.8%**.","Target (SLO) is 99.9%. Allowed downtime = 0.001 × 720 = 0.72 hours (43.2 minutes).","It was down 1.44 hours, more than allowed, so the **SLO is NOT met**.","Over budget by 0.72 hours (43.2 minutes). That is 200% of the budget used."]},{"heading":"Worked example 2: several incidents against a budget","items":["A vet clinic booking system. Target 99.9% over 30 days.","Three outages: 12, 18 and 9 minutes = 39 minutes.","Budget = 0.001 × 43,200 = 43.2 minutes.","Used = 39 / 43.2 = 90.3%. Left = 4.2 minutes.","SLI = (43,200 - 39) / 43,200 × 100 = 99.909%, which is at least 99.9%: **SLO met**.","But only 4.2 minutes remain, so pause risky releases."]},{"heading":"Worked example 3: stricter SLO","items":["Target 99.95% over 30 days. Downtime 25 minutes.","Budget = 0.0005 × 43,200 = 21.6 minutes.","SLI = (43,200 - 25) / 43,200 × 100 = 99.942%.","99.942 is below 99.95, so **not met**.","Over by 3.4 minutes (25 - 21.6). Used = 25 / 21.6 = 115.7%."]},{"heading":"Worked example 4: request-based SLI and error budget in requests","items":["10,000,000 requests in a month, 4,200 failed. Target 99.95%.","SLI = (10,000,000 - 4,200) / 10,000,000 = 99.958%: **met**.","Budget = 0.0005 × 10,000,000 = 5,000 allowed failures.","Used 4,200 = 84%. Left: 800 failures."]},{"heading":"Worked example 5: burn rate","items":["**Burn rate** is how fast you are spending the budget.","Target 99.9% over 30 days (budget 43.2 minutes). After 10 days you have had 30 minutes of downtime.","Budget used: 30 / 43.2 = 0.694.","Time passed: 10 / 30 = 0.333.","Burn rate = 0.694 / 0.333 = **2.08**, so about twice as fast as allowed.","The budget runs out at 30 / 2.08 = 14.4 days, about 4.4 days from now.","**Action**: pause new features and fix reliability."]},{"heading":"Worked example 6: working backwards and composite availability","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Working backwards"},"2 hours down in 30 days: SLI = (720 - 2) / 720 = 99.722%.","That meets a 99.5% target but misses 99.9%.","Most downtime allowed for 99.99% over a year = 0.0001 × 525,600 = 52.56 minutes.",{"type":"p","text":"Services in a chain (all must work)"},"Multiply them. 99.9% × 99.5% = **99.40%** (0.999 × 0.995 = 0.994005).","Three 99.9% parts in a row = 99.70%.","More links in the chain means lower availability.",{"type":"p","text":"Backup copies (either one works)"},"1 - (1 - 0.99)^2 = 99.99%.",{"type":"p","text":"Speed as the measurement"},"1,000,000 requests, 962,000 answered under 300 ms: SLI = 96.2%.","A target of \"95% under 300 ms\" is met, with 1.2 points to spare."]},{"heading":"Compliance checks and decisions","items":["**In your answer, state**: the SLI, the target, allowed vs actual downtime, met or not, % of budget used, and what to do.","**Budget healthy**: ship features and experiment.","**Budget gone**: freeze risky changes, fix reliability, hold a blameless review.","**SLA breach**: penalties apply. Keep the SLO stricter than the SLA (SLO 99.95%, SLA 99.9%).","Error budgets give developers and operations one shared view of risk.","Extra, not on the slides: alert on fast burn as well as slow. A burn rate of 14.4 spends 2% of a 30-day budget in 1 hour."]}],"pitfalls":["Do not mix up hours and minutes.","The budget is 0.1% of the time window, not 99.9% of the downtime.","If downtime is more than allowed, the SLO is **not** met.","SLI = measured, SLO = target, SLA = contract.","Always end with \"met\" or \"not met\" and what the team should do."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"SLI","sub":"what you measured","color":0},{"label":"SLO","sub":"the target for it","color":1},{"label":"SLA","sub":"the contract with penalties","color":2},{"label":"Error budget","sub":"100% minus SLO, turned into minutes or requests","color":3},{"label":"Decide","sub":"actual downtime within budget?","color":4}]}}},{"id":"git-commands","title":"Git and GitHub commands (list, explain, find the problem)","priority":5,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"4/7 papers (2019 Q1a, 2024-25 Q1b, 2025-26 Q1a); hinted: list commands 10 marks","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Know the three places a change lives, the everyday commands, branching, merging and undoing, and what each command does in one line.**","mnemonic":"Status, Add, Commit, Push (SACP) then Pull before Push; branch with checkout -b, merge with merge","source":["CT417_Notes_02_Git_GitHub","CT417_Exam_2019-2020_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5"],"sections":[{"heading":"The model: where changes live","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Working directory","sub":"files you edit"},{"label":"Staging area (index)","sub":"git add"},{"label":"Local repository","sub":"git commit"},{"label":"Remote (GitHub)","sub":"git push"}],"direction":"row","loopBack":"git pull / fetch brings remote changes back"},"caption":"A change travels left to right; pull travels back."},"**Git is distributed**: everyone has a full copy of the history, so committing needs no internet.","**GitHub** is a website that hosts Git repositories and adds teamwork tools such as pull requests.","A **commit** is a saved snapshot, with an id, author, time and message.","A **branch** is a movable bookmark pointing at a commit.","**HEAD** is where you are right now."]},{"heading":"Setup and starting","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command","What it does"],"rows":[["`git config --global user.name \"Name\"` and `user.email`","Set the identity recorded on commits"],["`git init`","Create a new empty local repository in the current folder"],["`git clone <url>`","Copy a remote repository (all history) to your machine and set `origin`"],["`git remote -v`","Show the remote repositories and URLs"],["`git remote add origin <url>`","Link a local repo to a remote called origin"]]}]},{"heading":"Everyday cycle","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command","What it does / why"],"rows":[["`git status`","Show which files are modified, staged or untracked and the current branch"],["`git diff`","Show unstaged changes line by line (`git diff --staged` for staged ones)"],["`git add <file>` / `git add .`","Stage changes: choose what goes into the next commit"],["`git commit -m \"message\"`","Record the staged snapshot in the local repository with a message"],["`git log` / `git log --oneline --graph`","Show commit history"],["`git pull`","fetch + merge: bring the latest remote commits into your branch"],["`git fetch`","Download remote changes without merging them"],["`git push`","Upload your local commits to the remote. First push of a branch: `git push -u origin <branch>`"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Past question (2019, 4 marks): get your work to the online repo"},"**1.** git status: see what changed.","**2.** git add: choose what to save.","**3.** git commit -m: save it locally.","**4.** git pull: bring in others' work and fix any conflicts.","**5.** git push: publish."]},{"heading":"Branching and merging","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command","What it does"],"rows":[["`git branch`","List local branches (`-a` includes remote)"],["`git branch <name>`","Create a branch"],["`git checkout <branch>` / `git switch <branch>`","Move to a branch"],["`git checkout -b <branch>` / `git switch -c <branch>`","Create and switch to a new branch"],["`git merge <branch>`","Merge the named branch INTO the branch you are currently on"],["`git branch -d <branch>`","Delete a merged branch (`-D` forces)"],["`git push origin --delete <branch>`","Delete a remote branch"],["`git rebase <branch>`","Replay your commits on top of another branch (linear history); do not rebase shared branches"]]}]},{"heading":"Undo and inspect","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command","What it does","Safe?"],"rows":[["`git restore <file>`","Discard unstaged edits in a file","Loses edits"],["`git restore --staged <file>` / `git reset <file>`","Unstage a file but keep the edits","Safe"],["`git revert <commit>`","Create a new commit that undoes an old one","Safe for shared history"],["`git reset --soft HEAD~1`","Undo last commit, keep changes staged","Local only"],["`git reset --hard <commit>`","Move branch and discard all working changes","Destructive"],["`git stash` / `git stash pop`","Shelve uncommitted work temporarily and restore it","Safe"],["`git tag v1.0`","Label a commit, e.g. a release","Safe"],["`git blame <file>`","Who last changed each line","Safe"],["`git commit --amend`","Edit the last commit message/content","Local only"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"`git push --force` overwrites the shared history and can destroy teammates' work. Use `git pull --rebase` then push. If you must force, use `--force-with-lease`."}]},{"heading":"GitHub concepts used with the commands","items":["**Pull request (PR)**: asking for your branch to be merged, usually into main. Others review it and automated checks run.","**Fork**: your own online copy of someone else's repository.","**Feature branch workflow**: each feature gets its own branch from main, and is merged back after review.","**.gitignore**: a list of files Git should not track (target/, *.class, .env, editor files).","**Do commit** everything needed to build from a fresh copy: source, settings, documents.","**Do not commit** built files (JARs, .pyc, .o), files with paths from your computer, or personal settings."]},{"heading":"Model answer: FoodieGo feature branch (5 marks, 2024-25 Q1b)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"git status\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Implemented new feature\"\ngit push origin feature-branch\ngit checkout main\ngit pull origin main\ngit merge feature-branch\ngit push origin main"},{"type":"p","text":"Why each line (this is where the marks are)"},"**status**: shows what changed.","**add**: stages the changes.","**commit**: saves them locally with a message.","**push**: publishes the feature branch.","**checkout main**: switch to the branch you are merging into.","**pull**: get the latest main first.","**merge**: bring the feature in.","**push**: publish main (or merge with a PR instead)."]},{"heading":"Given a command: identify the problem and fix it","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command / situation","Problem","Fix"],"rows":[["`git commit -m Implemented new feature`","Message not quoted: git treats words as pathspecs, so commit fails","`git commit -m \"Implemented new feature\"`"],["`git commit -m \"x\"` says \"nothing added to commit\"","Nothing staged","`git add <files>` first, then commit"],["`git merge feature-branch` run while ON feature-branch","Merges the branch into itself: \"Already up to date\". Wrong branch","`git checkout main` then `git merge feature-branch`"],["`git push` says \"no upstream branch\"","Branch has no remote counterpart yet","`git push -u origin feature-branch`"],["`git push` rejected: \"non-fast-forward / fetch first\"","Remote has commits you do not have","`git pull --rebase` (or `git pull`), resolve conflicts, push again; not `--force`"],["`git pull` stops with \"CONFLICT (content)\"","Same lines changed on both sides","Open file, resolve markers, `git add`, `git commit`"],["`git checkout feature` fails: \"would be overwritten\"","Uncommitted changes conflict with the target branch","Commit or `git stash`, then switch"],["`git branch -d feature` refused: \"not fully merged\"","Branch has commits not in the current branch","Merge it first, or `-D` only if you really discard it"],["`git add .` then secrets (.env, application.properties with keys) committed","Secret in history","`git rm --cached .env`, add to `.gitignore`, rotate the secret; rewrite history only if needed"],["`git reset --hard` run by mistake","Uncommitted work lost","Prevention: stash/commit first; recover committed work via `git reflog`"],["`HEAD detached`","You checked out a commit, not a branch","`git switch -c new-branch` to keep work, or `git switch main`"],["`git clone` into a folder then `git push` to someone else's repo is denied (403)","No write permission","Fork the repository, push to your fork, open a pull request"]]}]}],"pitfalls":["git merge brings changes **into** the branch you are on. Check which that is.","Do not forget git add before commit, or quotes around the message.","Do not use git push --force to fix a rejected push.","Explain why each command is needed. A bare list loses marks."]},{"id":"git-merge-conflicts","title":"Merge conflicts: why they happen and how to resolve them","priority":6,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"2/7 papers (2025-26 s1 Q1d) + hinted \"merge issue and resolve\" last year","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**A conflict occurs when two branches change the same part of a file (or one deletes what the other edits) and Git cannot decide. Resolve: pull, open the file, edit the markers into the correct final code, git add, git commit, test, push.**","mnemonic":"PEACE: Pull, Edit markers, Add, Commit, Examine (test)","source":["CT417_Notes_02_Git_GitHub","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why conflicts arise","items":["Two developers (or branches) change the same lines, or one edits a file the other deleted or renamed, then both are merged.","Long-lived feature branches drift from main so overlapping edits accumulate. High activity periods make this worse.","**Sequential merge**: if changes do not overlap Git merges automatically, no conflict (slide: one branch adds a method, the other adds a constructor, auto-merged).","**Type 1 conflict (easy)**: both add the same thing or the textual change is simple; a human just chooses/combines.","**Type 2 conflict (tricky)**: semantic conflict. Branch A changes `length` from int to float, branch B changes it to short and uses it in a method. Git can show the text, but the right answer needs design judgement; code may even merge cleanly yet fail to compile or behave wrongly."]},{"heading":"Anatomy of a conflict","items":[{"type":"code","text":"<<<<<<< HEAD\nprivate float length;\n=======\nprivate short length;\n>>>>>>> feature/pen-size"},"`<<<<<<< HEAD` to `=======` is YOUR current branch version; `=======` to `>>>>>>> branch` is the INCOMING branch version.","`git status` lists \"both modified\" (unmerged) files. The merge is paused until you resolve all of them."]},{"heading":"Resolution walk-through","items":[{"type":"code","text":"git checkout feature-x\ngit pull origin main        # or: git merge main  -> CONFLICT in Pen.java\ngit status                  # shows \"both modified: Pen.java\"\n# open Pen.java, decide the final code, delete the <<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>> lines\nmvn test                    # prove the merged result still builds and passes\ngit add Pen.java            # mark as resolved\ngit commit                  # completes the merge commit\ngit push origin feature-x"},"1. Identify conflicted files (`git status`, or the IDE/GitHub conflict editor).","2. Understand both changes (`git log`, `git diff`, ask the author). Decide: keep ours, keep theirs, or combine.","3. Edit so the file contains the correct final code only, with all markers removed.","4. Build and run tests. A clean text merge can still be logically wrong.","5. `git add` the file, `git commit` to finish the merge, then push.","6. To abandon: `git merge --abort` returns to the state before the merge."]},{"heading":"Preventing conflicts (answer for \"practical process\")","items":["Short-lived feature branches; merge to main often (integrate daily, the CI idea).","Pull/rebase main into your branch regularly so you resolve small conflicts early.","Small, focused pull requests with review; agree code ownership and split work by file/module.","Consistent formatting (formatter, linter) so whitespace changes do not cause noise.","Branch protection: PR required, CI must pass, at least one reviewer.","Communication: tell the team when refactoring shared files."]},{"heading":"Model answer: SkyBankAI merge conflicts (5 marks)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Arise: feature branches live for days while main moves; two teams edit the same payment/user classes; a PR merge detects overlapping hunks."},{"type":"p","text":"Prevent: short branches, frequent `git pull --rebase origin main`, small PRs, CI on every PR, branch protection, clear module ownership."},{"type":"p","text":"Resolve: fetch, update branch with main, open conflicted files, edit markers to the intended combination, run the test suite, git add, commit, push, let CI re-run and re-request review."}]}],"pitfalls":["Leaving conflict markers in the file or committing without re-running the tests.","Choosing \"ours\" blindly: you may delete a teammate's fix.","Resolving by force-pushing over the other branch.","Not distinguishing textual from semantic (type 2) conflicts."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"git pull","sub":"conflict reported","color":0},{"label":"Open the file","color":1},{"label":"Edit the markers","sub":"leave the correct final code","color":2},{"label":"git add","color":3},{"label":"git commit","color":4},{"label":"Test, then git push","color":0}]}}},{"id":"dockerfile","title":"Dockerfile: instructions and steps to construct one","priority":7,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q3 (write a Dockerfile, steps to construct); 2/7 papers involve Docker builds","marks":"6-12 marks","summary":"**Inspect the project, decide build vs runtime, then write FROM (runtime image), WORKDIR, COPY (the artefact), EXPOSE (port), ENTRYPOINT (start command), and build with docker build -t name .**","mnemonic":"F-W-C-R-E-E: FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN, EXPOSE, ENTRYPOINT","source":["CT417_Notes_05b_Docker","CT417_Notes_05a_Deployment_Tools"],"sections":[{"heading":"Steps to construct a Dockerfile (the lecturer's question: \"steps to construct\")","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["1 Inspect the repository","2 How is it built? what artefact?","3 What runtime executes it?","4 What files go in the image?","5 Configuration and port","6 Start command","7 Write Dockerfile","8 Build and verify"],"direction":"row"},"caption":"Think through the project before typing instructions."},"1. **Inspect**: `ls`, `cat pom.xml`. pom.xml means Maven/Java; package.json means Node; requirements.txt means Python.","2. **Build and artefact**: `./mvnw clean package` produces `target/app.jar`.","3. **Runtime**: Java 17 needed, so choose a compatible image such as `eclipse-temurin:17-jre`. Do not copy a FROM line blindly from Stack Overflow; match the project's Java version. Avoid `:latest` (not reproducible).","4. **Files**: which files must exist in the image (the JAR, config)? Everything else stays out (.dockerignore).","5. **Configuration and port**: read secrets/config from environment variables; note the app port (8080).","6. **Startup command**: `java -jar app.jar`.","7. **Write** the Dockerfile, 8. **build** (`docker build -t my-app .`) and **verify** (run, ps, logs, curl)."]},{"heading":"Lecturer's step sequence (Docker slides, steps 1-13)","items":["Steps 1-2: inspect the repository (pom.xml, mvnw, src/main/resources/application.properties, target/); decide what artefact is produced and which runtime and VERSION executes it. A base-image tag is part of your execution environment: treat it as an engineering dependency, not decoration.","Step 3: decide where the app lives inside the image (`WORKDIR /app`). Your host path does not exist in the image; WORKDIR just establishes a predictable location.","Step 4: understand the **build context** before COPY. The final `.` in `docker build -t my-app .` means \"use the current directory as the build context\". Left side of COPY = path in the build context; right side = location inside the image. Docker cannot copy a file it cannot see (a common mistake: running docker build from a directory without target/app.jar).","Step 5: control the context with `.dockerignore` (.git, .idea, .vscode, *.log, .env). It reduces data sent to the builder and the chance of secrets entering the context, but is NOT a substitute for proper secret management. Whether `target/` is ignored depends on strategy: needed if the JAR is built on the host, unnecessary if built in a Docker build stage.","Step 6: find the application's ACTUAL internal port (Spring Boot 8080) for EXPOSE; do not copy a port from an example. `-p 9090:8080` = host 9090 to container 8080.","Step 7: startup command: `java -jar app.jar` becomes `ENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]`. RUN builds the image (build time); ENTRYPOINT/CMD define what runs when the image becomes a container. CMD is often a default command or default arguments that can be overridden. What matters is which process Docker should launch.","Step 8: keep configuration separate from the image; supply secrets at run time with `-e`.","Step 9: each instruction answers a question: FROM = which runtime environment? WORKDIR = where does the app live and run? COPY = which artefact goes in the image? EXPOSE = which internal port? ENTRYPOINT = which process starts?","Step 10: build OUTSIDE Docker (Option A: `./mvnw clean package` on host/CI, then COPY the JAR; software build and container build are clearly separate, JAR must already exist in the context) or INSIDE Docker (Option B: multi-stage). Build-time and runtime requirements differ; multi-stage makes that explicit. `-DskipTests` is a pipeline decision, not a universal Docker rule: ask \"Where in our delivery process is correctness verified?\"","Step 11: layers and cache. Put relatively stable build inputs (pom.xml, dependency download) before frequently changing ones (src). `COPY . .` then `RUN mvn package` invalidates the layer on any change. Understand why order matters; do not distort the build just to chase cache speed.","Step 12: same reasoning for other stacks: Spring Boot (pom.xml, Maven/Gradle, JAR, Java, `java -jar`); Node/Express (package.json, lockfile, npm dependencies, Node, `npm start` or `node ...`); Python/Flask (requirements.txt, Python, `python ...` or an app server command). Reasoning: inspect, build/dependencies, artefact, runtime, configuration, port, startup command, Dockerfile.","Step 13: a successful `docker build` proves Docker could execute the instructions, NOT that the app works. Keep verifying: build, image exists, run, container remains running, docker ps, docker logs, port mapping, HTTP request, application responds."]},{"heading":"Dockerfile derivation checklist (slide)","items":["What build system? What artefact reaches the final image? Which runtime and version? Why this base image?","What is the build context? Does every COPY source exist inside it? Is a .dockerignore appropriate?","What internal port? What command starts the app? Which instructions run at image-build time and which command at container start?","Are secrets/configuration supplied at runtime rather than embedded? Does the final image contain unneeded build tools? Does the container remain running? Can the app actually be reached and exercised?",{"type":"note","text":"Workflow to quote: Inspect, derive, build, run, verify. The idea to keep: build the application, build the image, run containers from that image. The image is the reusable deployment package; the container is a disposable runtime instance."}]},{"heading":"Instructions explained","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Instruction","Purpose","Notes"],"rows":[["`FROM image:tag`","Base image (OS + runtime) every Dockerfile starts from","Pin a version, e.g. `eclipse-temurin:17-jre`; JRE enough to RUN; JDK/Maven needed to BUILD"],["`WORKDIR /app`","Set the working directory inside the image; created if missing","Not your host path; later COPY/RUN/ENTRYPOINT are relative to it"],["`COPY src dest`","Copy files from the build context (host) into the image","`COPY target/app.jar app.jar` puts /app/app.jar; context = folder given to `docker build`"],["`ADD`","Like COPY plus URL and tar extraction","Prefer COPY"],["`RUN cmd`","Execute a command at BUILD time and commit a new layer","e.g. `RUN mvn clean package -DskipTests`, `RUN npm ci`"],["`ENV KEY=value`","Set an environment variable in the image","Never put secrets here: they stay in the image/history"],["`ARG`","Build-time variable","Not available at run time"],["`EXPOSE 8080`","Documents the port the app listens on","Does not publish it; use `-p` at run time"],["`ENTRYPOINT [\"java\",\"-jar\",\"app.jar\"]`","The fixed command run when the container starts","Exec (JSON) form so signals reach the process"],["`CMD [...]`","Default arguments / command, overridable at `docker run`","ENTRYPOINT + CMD: CMD supplies default args"],["`USER app`","Run as a non-root user","Security best practice"],["`HEALTHCHECK`","Command Docker runs to test the container is healthy","Used by orchestrators"],["`VOLUME`","Declare a mount point for persistent data","Containers are ephemeral"],["`LABEL`","Metadata (maintainer, version)","Optional"]]},{"type":"note","text":"RUN executes while creating the IMAGE (docker build). ENTRYPOINT/CMD determine what executes in the CONTAINER (docker run)."}]},{"heading":"Dockerfile 1: simple, JAR built outside","items":[{"type":"code","text":"FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY target/app.jar app.jar\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]"},"Build outside first (`./mvnw clean package`, Maven on host or CI), then `docker build -t my-app .`. The image is small because it has only a JRE and the JAR."]},{"heading":"Dockerfile 2: multi-stage (build inside Docker)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"# Stage 1: build\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nRUN mvn dependency:go-offline\nCOPY src ./src\nRUN mvn clean package -DskipTests\n\n# Stage 2: runtime\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/app.jar app.jar\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]"},"Stage 1 has JDK + Maven (large) to BUILD; stage 2 has only a Java runtime to RUN. `COPY --from=build` takes just the JAR. The final image is small and has no build tools.","**Layer caching**: copy `pom.xml` and resolve dependencies BEFORE copying `src`. Source changes often, dependencies rarely, so the expensive dependency layer is reused. Bad: `COPY . .` then `RUN mvn package` re-downloads dependencies every time.","**.dockerignore** keeps files out of the build context: `target/`, `.git`, `.idea`, `.vscode`, `*.log`, `.env`. Smaller, faster builds and no secrets leaked."]},{"heading":"Dockerfile 3: Node.js microservice (SkyBank style)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"FROM node:20-alpine\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\nCOPY . .\nEXPOSE 3000\nUSER node\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]"}]},{"heading":"Configuration and secrets","items":["Read configuration in code from the environment: `System.getenv(\"YOUTUBE_API_KEY\")`.","Bad: `ENV YOUTUBE_API_KEY=actual-secret-value` in the Dockerfile (the secret becomes part of the image and its build history).","Good: inject at run time: `docker run -e YOUTUBE_API_KEY=\"$YOUTUBE_API_KEY\" -p 8080:8080 my-app`. Same image, different runtime configuration.","Ports: `-p HOST:CONTAINER`, e.g. `-p 9090:8080` makes the app reachable at localhost:9090 while it listens on 8080 inside."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing EXPOSE and thinking the port is published; you still need -p.","Using the latest tag or a JDK image for runtime without reason.","Putting secrets in ENV or COPYing .env into the image.","COPY path wrong because the JAR was never built, or is excluded by .dockerignore.","Explaining only what lines do, not the steps/reasoning for constructing the file."]},{"id":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","title":"Docker commands and troubleshooting","priority":8,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q3 part 2; 2/7 papers (2025-26 s1 and autumn Q1)","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**Verify in order: image exists, container running (docker ps), logs (docker logs), port mapping, HTTP check. For failures use docker ps -a and docker logs to find why it exited.**","mnemonic":"B-R-P-L-E: build, run, ps, logs, exec","source":["CT417_Notes_05b_Docker","CT417_Notes_05a_Deployment_Tools","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"Command list","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Command","Meaning"],"rows":[["`docker --version`, `docker run hello-world`","Check install and that the daemon works"],["`docker build -t my-app .`","Build an image from the Dockerfile in the current directory (`.` = build context); `-t` names/tags it"],["`docker build -t my-app:1.0 -t my-app:latest .`","Apply several tags"],["`docker images`","List local images"],["`docker run -d --name my-app-container -p 8080:8080 my-app`","`-d` detached, `--name` name it, `-p HOST:CONTAINER` publish port"],["`docker run -e APP_MODE=prod ...`","Set environment variable at run time"],["`docker ps`","Running containers"],["`docker ps -a`","All containers including stopped/exited ones"],["`docker logs <name>` (`-f` to follow)","Container stdout/stderr: first place to look when it fails"],["`docker stop <name>`","Stop a running container"],["`docker start <name>`","Start an existing stopped container"],["`docker restart <name>`","Stop then start"],["`docker rm <name>`","Remove a stopped container (`-f` forces)"],["`docker rmi <image>`","Remove an image"],["`docker exec -it <name> /bin/sh`","Open a shell inside a running container (if the image has one)"],["`docker inspect <name>`","Full low-level JSON (ports, env, mounts, state)"],["`docker pull image:tag` / `docker push image:tag`","Download from / upload to a registry"],["`docker login ghcr.io`","Authenticate to a registry"],["`docker tag my-app ghcr.io/org/my-app:1.0`","Add a registry name/tag to an image"],["`docker compose up -d` / `down`","Start/stop the multi-container stack defined in docker-compose.yml"],["`docker manifest inspect image:tag`","Check whether a tag exists in the registry"],["`docker system prune`","Clean unused data"]]}]},{"heading":"Standard verification sequence","items":[{"type":"code","text":"docker build -t my-app .\ndocker run -d --name my-app-container -p 8080:8080 my-app\ndocker ps\ndocker logs my-app-container\ncurl http://localhost:8080/\n# if it is not running:\ndocker ps -a\ndocker logs my-app-container"},"Reasoning: image exists, container remains running, inspect `docker ps`, inspect `docker logs`, verify port mapping, make an HTTP request, application responds.","Image vs container: an image is an immutable layered template; a container is a running instance. One image can create many independent containers, each with different ports/env."]},{"heading":"Troubleshooting approach (slide)","items":["If the container exits immediately: `docker ps -a` then `docker logs <name>`. Possible causes (slide list): incorrect startup command; missing artefact; incompatible runtime; missing configuration; application startup failure.","If the container is running but the app is unreachable, investigate: the application's internal listening port; the `-p HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT` mapping; application logs; whether the service is actually listening.","Do not immediately rewrite the application source. First decide whether the problem is BUILD, IMAGE, STARTUP, CONFIGURATION, NETWORK / PORT MAPPING or APPLICATION. That distinction is part of deployment engineering."]},{"heading":"Troubleshooting table","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause","Diagnose and fix"],"rows":[["Build: \"COPY failed: file not found target/app.jar\"","JAR not built yet, wrong path, or target excluded by .dockerignore","Run `mvn clean package` first (or use multi-stage), check `ls target`, fix path/.dockerignore"],["Build: \"pull access denied / manifest unknown\" for base image","Wrong image name/tag, typo, no network","Check name and tag on the registry; `docker pull` it manually"],["Run: container exits immediately (`docker ps` empty)","App crashed or main process ended","`docker ps -a` shows Exited code; `docker logs <name>`; fix error, rebuild"],["\"no main manifest attribute\" / \"Error: Unable to access jarfile\"","Plain JAR not executable, wrong file name, wrong WORKDIR","Use spring-boot-maven-plugin repackage; match ENTRYPOINT to actual jar name"],["\"UnsupportedClassVersionError\"","Built with a newer Java than the runtime image","Use matching base image (build with 17, run on 17)"],["\"port is already allocated\" / \"address already in use\"","Host port taken by another container/process","Use another host port `-p 9090:8080` or stop the other container (`docker ps`)"],["Container runs but browser cannot connect","-p missing or swapped (CONTAINER:HOST), app bound to 127.0.0.1 inside container, wrong port","Check `docker ps` PORTS column; use `-p HOST:CONTAINER`; bind to 0.0.0.0"],["\"container name already in use\"","Old container with same name exists","`docker rm -f <name>` then run again"],["App fails: null API key / missing config","Environment variable not provided","`docker run -e KEY=...` or env file; never bake into image"],["Exit code 137","Killed: out of memory (OOM) or `docker kill`","Increase memory limit or fix the leak; inspect `docker inspect` State.OOMKilled"],["\"exec: /bin/bash not found\"","Slim/alpine image has no bash","Use `/bin/sh`"],["`docker pull` -> \"manifest unknown\" for :latest","That tag was never pushed to the registry","`docker manifest inspect`; push the tag, or pull an existing tag (e.g. the commit SHA)"],["`docker push` -> \"denied/unauthorized\"","Not logged in or token lacks write:packages","`docker login`; use token with correct scope"]]}]},{"heading":"Worked case: \"manifest unknown\" in a GitHub Actions deployment","items":[{"type":"code","text":"Run docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:latest\nError response from daemon: manifest for ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:latest not found: manifest unknown\nError: Process completed with exit code 1"},"**Cause**: the build job only pushed the tag `:<commit-sha>`; no job ever pushed `:latest`. The deploy job pulls `:latest`, which does not exist in GHCR, so the registry replies \"manifest unknown\". Build succeeds because it only needs to push the SHA tag; deploy fails because it references a different tag.","**Diagnose with git + docker**: `git log -1` and the workflow run show the SHA that was built; `docker manifest inspect ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:<sha>` succeeds while `:latest` fails; `git diff` / read the workflow to see the tags pushed.","**Rectify**: tag and push both (see GitHub Actions topic), re-run the workflow, then redeploy; better still deploy by SHA.",{"type":"code","text":"docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:latest\ndocker compose up -d payment-api\ndocker ps\ndocker logs payment-api --tail 50\ncurl -f http://localhost:3000/health"}]},{"heading":"Containers vs VMs, why containers","items":["Containers virtualise the OS (share the host kernel, isolate app and dependencies); VMs virtualise hardware and need a full OS each: heavy, slower startup.","Why: **portability** (same everywhere), **scalability** (microservices), **efficiency** (fewer resources), **isolation** (no interference), **faster deployment/rollback** (start in seconds).","Why not just a JAR? A JAR is the artefact; execution still needs the right runtime, libraries and configuration. The image packages all of that."]}],"pitfalls":["Using docker ps and forgetting -a when the container has exited.","Not checking docker logs first: it usually names the exact error.","Reversing -p: it is HOST:CONTAINER.","Describing commands without saying what they confirm in the verification chain."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Image exists?","sub":"docker images","color":0},{"label":"Container running?","sub":"docker ps (-a)","color":1},{"label":"Why did it exit?","sub":"docker logs","color":2},{"label":"Port mapping right?","color":3},{"label":"HTTP check","color":4}]}}},{"id":"github-actions","title":"GitHub Actions: workflow YAML anatomy, triggers, jobs, steps","priority":9,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"3/7 papers (2024-25 Q1c, 2025-26 s1 Q1b, autumn Q1); hinted","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**A workflow (YAML in .github/workflows) has name, on (triggers), jobs (each with runs-on and steps). Steps either use an action (uses) or run a shell command (run). needs orders jobs; secrets hold credentials.**","mnemonic":"N-O-J-R-S: name, on, jobs, runs-on, steps (uses/run)","source":["CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3","CT417_Notes_04_Build_Tools_CI"],"sections":[{"heading":"Anatomy","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Key","Meaning"],"rows":[["`name`","Workflow name shown in the Actions tab"],["`on`","Trigger events: `push`, `pull_request`, `workflow_dispatch` (manual), `schedule` (cron), `release`; filter with `branches:`"],["`env`","Environment variables for the workflow/job/step"],["`permissions`","Scopes granted to GITHUB_TOKEN (e.g. `packages: write`)"],["`jobs`","Named jobs; run in parallel by default"],["`runs-on`","Runner: `ubuntu-latest`, `windows-latest`, `macos-latest`"],["`needs`","Job dependency: this job waits for another and runs after it succeeds"],["`if`","Condition, e.g. `github.event_name == 'push'` or `github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'`"],["`strategy.matrix`","Run the job over several versions (JDK 11/17, OS)"],["`steps`","Ordered list of steps in a job; share the runner filesystem"],["`uses`","Run a reusable action, e.g. `actions/checkout@v4`"],["`with`","Inputs for an action (java-version, path, etc.)"],["`run`","Run a shell command"],["`secrets.NAME`","Encrypted repository secret, never hard-code credentials"],["`${{ github.sha }}`","Expression: commit SHA of the run"]]},"Terms: **workflow** (the YAML file), **event** (trigger), **job** (set of steps on one runner), **step** (one task), **action** (reusable unit), **runner** (VM that executes the job), **artefact** (files stored from a run).","YAML rules: indentation with spaces (2) defines structure; `-` starts a list item; `key: value` pairs. A tab or wrong indent breaks the workflow."]},{"heading":"Example: Java CI with Maven, JaCoCo, Docker (test on PRs, build image on main)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"name: vet-clinic-ci\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\nenv:\n  IMAGE: ghcr.io/vetclinic/vet-api\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Set up JDK 17\n        uses: actions/setup-java@v4\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '17'\n          cache: maven\n      - name: Build, test and coverage\n        run: mvn --batch-mode clean verify\n      - name: Upload coverage report\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n        with:\n          name: jacoco-report\n          path: target/site/jacoco\n\n  docker:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      packages: write\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Log in to GHCR\n        run: echo \"${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\" | docker login ghcr.io -u ${{ github.actor }} --password-stdin\n      - name: Build image with SHA and latest tags\n        run: docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest .\n      - name: Push both tags\n        run: |\n          docker push $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }}\n          docker push $IMAGE:latest\n\n  deploy-staging:\n    needs: docker\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Deploy to staging\n        run: ssh deploy@staging.example.com \"docker pull $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} && docker compose up -d\""},"Read it as: PR runs `test` only; push to main runs `test`, then `docker`, then `deploy-staging` in order because of `needs`."]},{"heading":"Explaining the 2024-25 workflow (7 marks): \"what does the build job do?\"","items":[{"type":"code","text":"name: Build and Test Java Application\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v2\n      - name: Set up JDK 11\n        uses: actions/setup-java@v1\n        with:\n          java-version: '11'\n      - name: Build with Maven\n        run: mvn clean package\n      - name: Run tests and generate a code coverage report\n        run: mvn verify\n      - name: Build container image\n        run: docker build -t my-app .\n      - name: Push container image to registry\n        run: docker push myregistry/my-app:latest"},"Trigger: runs on every push to `main` only (not on pull requests).","Job `build` runs on a fresh Ubuntu runner VM.","1. Checkout: downloads the repository code onto the runner (v2 is outdated).","2. Set up JDK 11: installs Java 11 so Maven can compile.","3. `mvn clean package`: deletes target, compiles, runs unit tests, builds the JAR.","4. `mvn verify`: runs later lifecycle phases (integration tests, checks, JaCoCo report if configured); tests run twice, which is wasteful.","5. `docker build -t my-app .`: builds the image from the Dockerfile using the JAR.","6. `docker push myregistry/my-app:latest`: publishes to the registry.","**Problems to mention (bonus marks)**: no `docker login` so push will fail; the image is built as `my-app` but pushed as `myregistry/my-app:latest` (tag mismatch); `:latest` only, no version/SHA tag so no rollback; no test on pull requests; outdated action versions and JDK 11; no dependency cache; no secrets handling; no security scan."]},{"heading":"Rewriting the \"build and push\" step (2025-26 autumn Q1b, 5 marks)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"- name: Build and push image\n  run: |\n    docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest .\n    docker push $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }}\n    docker push $IMAGE:latest"},"Build once with two `-t` tags (same image id), then push each tag. `docker push --all-tags $IMAGE` is an equivalent shorthand. The deploy job pulling `:latest` now finds a manifest.","Also valid: the `docker/build-push-action` with `tags:` listing both. Commit SHA gives an immutable version for rollback; latest is a moving pointer."]},{"heading":"Designing a pipeline (2025-26 s1 Q1b: Node.js microservice)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"name: node-service\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with: { node-version: 20, cache: npm }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test\n  image:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: echo \"${{ secrets.GHCR_TOKEN }}\" | docker login ghcr.io -u skybankai --password-stdin\n      - run: |\n          docker build -t ghcr.io/skybankai/svc:${{ github.sha }} .\n          docker push ghcr.io/skybankai/svc:${{ github.sha }}\n  deploy-staging:\n    needs: image\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ssh deploy@staging \"docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/svc:${{ github.sha }} && docker compose up -d\""},"Key steps to list and explain: trigger (PR + main), checkout, set up runtime + cache, install (`npm ci`), run tests, (add SAST/SCA scan), log in to registry, build image tagged with SHA, push, deploy to staging with the same tag, smoke test."]},{"heading":"Risky pipeline and improvements (2025-26 s1 Q1c)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Risk in design","Improvement"],"rows":[["Tests only on main: bugs found after merge","Run tests on every pull request; protect main (required checks + review)"],["Every commit to main deploys straight to production: no staging, no approval","Deploy to staging first, smoke tests, then manual approval (environment protection) before production; or canary/blue-green"],["No rollback strategy: a bad release causes downtime","Immutable versioned images (SHA tags); keep previous version; redeploy previous tag or `git revert`; feature flags; health-check-based automatic rollback"],["Latest tag only","Tag by SHA/semantic version"],["No security scans","SAST, SCA/dependency scan, container scan in the pipeline"]]}]}],"pitfalls":["Wrong indentation or tabs in YAML; mixing `uses` and `run` in the same step.","Forgetting `needs`, so jobs run in parallel and deploy before build finishes.","Hard-coding passwords/tokens instead of `secrets`.","Building the image under one name and pushing another, or pushing without docker login.","Pulling `:latest` when only a SHA tag was pushed."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"name","color":0},{"label":"on","sub":"triggers","color":1},{"label":"jobs","sub":"needs sets the order","color":2},{"label":"runs-on","sub":"the runner","color":3},{"label":"steps","sub":"uses an action, or run a command","color":4}]}}},{"id":"maven-structure-lifecycle","title":"Maven: directory structure, lifecycle, repositories, dependencies","priority":10,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"1/7 papers (2023-24 Autumn Q3); hinted Q2","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Maven builds by convention: standard folders, a pom.xml, a lifecycle of ordered phases (validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, deploy) that run plugin goals, and automatic dependency download.**","mnemonic":"Very Cool Tests Pack Very Impressive Deployments: validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, deploy","source":["CT417_Notes_04_Build_Tools_CI","CT417_Exam_2023-2024_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"What Maven is","items":["Maven is a build tool that manages the project build, reporting and documentation: compile source, copy resources, compile and run tests, package, deploy, clean.","Developers wanted an easy build, a standard way to build projects, a clear definition of the project, easy publishing of project information and sharing of JARs. It embraces **convention over configuration**: if you follow the standard layout you configure almost nothing.","Benefits vs manual builds (2023 Autumn Q3a): repeatable and automated, one command for compile/test/package, dependency management (downloads libraries and their transitive dependencies), consistent structure across teams and IDEs, easy CI integration, plugin ecosystem, less human error.","Core features (Q3b): (1) POM-driven project description; (2) dependency management with repositories and transitive dependencies; (3) standard build lifecycle with plugins; (4) convention over configuration; (5) multi-module projects and inheritance."]},{"heading":"Standard directory layout","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"tree","data":{"root":{"label":"my-app (project home)","children":[{"label":"pom.xml","edge":"build descriptor"},{"label":"src","children":[{"label":"main","children":[{"label":"java","edge":"deliverable source code"},{"label":"resources","edge":"config, properties"}]},{"label":"test","children":[{"label":"java","edge":"unit test source"},{"label":"resources","edge":"test resources"}]}]},{"label":"target","edge":"build output (generated)","children":[{"label":"classes"},{"label":"my-app-1.0.jar"}]}]}},"caption":"Maven standard directory layout."},{"type":"table","head":["Path","Contents"],"rows":[["`pom.xml`","Project Object Model at the project root"],["`src/main/java`","Deliverable Java source code"],["`src/main/resources`","Resources shipped with the app (application.properties, templates)"],["`src/test/java`","Unit/test source (JUnit test classes)"],["`src/test/resources`","Resources only needed for tests"],["`target/`","Build output: compiled classes, test reports, packaged JAR/WAR (do not commit)"]]},"Example: with artifactId my-app and version 1.0, `mvn package` compiles, runs tests and creates `target/my-app-1.0.jar`."]},{"heading":"Build lifecycle","items":["A lifecycle is a list of named phases that give order to goal execution. Running a phase runs all earlier phases too: `mvn test` runs validate, compile and test.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"validate","sub":"project correct?"},{"label":"compile","sub":"compiler:compile"},{"label":"test","sub":"surefire:test"},{"label":"package","sub":"jar/war"},{"label":"verify","sub":"integration checks"},{"label":"install","sub":"to ~/.m2"},{"label":"deploy","sub":"to remote repo"}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"Default lifecycle phases in order."},{"type":"table","head":["Phase","What happens"],"rows":[["validate","Check the project is correct and information is available"],["compile","Compile the main source code"],["test","Run unit tests (Surefire) on the compiled code; packaging not needed"],["package","Bundle into JAR/WAR"],["verify","Run checks on the package (integration tests, quality gates such as JaCoCo thresholds)"],["install","Copy the artefact into the local repository (~/.m2) for other local projects"],["deploy","Copy to a remote repository for sharing"]]},"**Goals** are provided by plugins and bound to phases: `compiler:compile` is bound to compile; `surefire:test` to test. `mvn clean` is a separate lifecycle (pre-clean, clean, post-clean) that deletes `target/`.","Typical typed steps of the Java build process (7 marks): resolve dependencies, generate sources, compile sources, compile test sources, execute tests, package (.jar/.war), run health checks/verify, generate reports, install/deploy.",{"type":"table","head":["Command","Effect"],"rows":[["`mvn clean`","Delete target/"],["`mvn compile`","Compile main code"],["`mvn test`","Compile and run unit tests"],["`mvn package`","Test and build the JAR in target/"],["`mvn clean package -DskipTests`","Fresh build, skip tests (use sparingly)"],["`mvn verify`","Run through verify (integration tests, JaCoCo checks)"],["`mvn install`","Package and put in local repo"],["`mvn dependency:tree`","Show direct and transitive dependencies"]]}]},{"heading":"Dependency management and repositories","items":["Dependencies are identified by coordinates: **groupId, artifactId, version** (GAV). If your project depends on a JAR, Maven downloads it and stores it in the local repository.","**Transitive dependencies**: if library A needs library B, Maven pulls B too (a huge benefit that early Maven lacked).","Dependency features: management (control versions of transitive deps), **scope** (compile, test, provided, runtime) so libraries are only on the classpath where needed, and **exclusions** (drop an unwanted transitive dependency).",{"type":"table","head":["Scope","Available when","Example"],"rows":[["compile (default)","compile, test, runtime","commons-lang3"],["provided","compile and test, not packaged (container supplies it)","servlet-api"],["runtime","run and test, not compile","JDBC driver"],["test","test code only","JUnit, Mockito"]]},"**Local repository**: `~/.m2` (Windows `C:\\Users\\<user>\\.m2`). Maven searches local first, then remote repositories (Maven Central at mvnrepository.com, or a company repository).","Large projects use multi-module builds: a parent POM lists modules and one command builds them all; child POMs inherit from the parent. All POMs inherit from the **super POM** (default directories, plugins)."]},{"heading":"Maven vs Gradle (2023 Autumn)","items":["Maven: XML (pom.xml), convention over configuration, fixed lifecycle, very widely used, declarative.","Gradle: Groovy/Kotlin build scripts, flexible task graph, incremental builds and build cache so often faster, more configurable but less standard.","Both automate compile, test, package and dependency management."]}],"pitfalls":["Putting tests in src/main or resources in src/main/java: break convention and the build ignores them.","Saying mvn package skips tests: it runs them unless -DskipTests.","Confusing the phase (test) with the goal (surefire:test).","Confusing install (local repo) with deploy (remote repo)."]},{"id":"pom-xml","title":"pom.xml anatomy: coordinates, dependencies, plugins (write a pom)","priority":11,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q2 (\"pom files\"); 1/7 papers","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**A POM declares project coordinates (groupId, artifactId, version, packaging), properties, dependencies and build plugins. Be able to write a complete one from a description and to explain each tag.**","mnemonic":"M-G-A-V-P-D-B: modelVersion, groupId, artifactId, version, packaging/properties, dependencies, build","source":["CT417_Notes_04_Build_Tools_CI","WK10_Introduction_to_JaCoCo"],"sections":[{"heading":"Tag by tag","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Tag","Purpose"],"rows":[["`<project>`","Root element of the descriptor"],["`<modelVersion>`","Version of the POM model; always 4.0.0"],["`<parent>`","Inherit config from another POM (e.g. spring-boot-starter-parent)"],["`<groupId>`","Organisation/project namespace, e.g. ie.universityofgalway.vet"],["`<artifactId>`","Name of this artefact, e.g. vet-clinic"],["`<version>`","Version, e.g. 1.0.0 or 1.0-SNAPSHOT"],["`<packaging>`","jar (default), war, pom"],["`<name>`, `<description>`","Human readable info"],["`<properties>`","Variables such as `java.version`, `project.build.sourceEncoding`"],["`<dependencies>`","Libraries the project needs (GAV + scope)"],["`<dependencyManagement>`","Central place to fix versions without adding the dependency"],["`<build><plugins>`","Configure plugins bound to lifecycle phases"],["`<modules>`","Child modules in a multi-module build"]]},"The three coordinates groupId + artifactId + version uniquely identify the project (and artefact) in a repository."]},{"heading":"Full example: plain Java project with JUnit 5 and JaCoCo","items":[{"type":"code","text":"<project xmlns=\"http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0\"\n         xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n         xsi:schemaLocation=\"http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd\">\n  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>\n\n  <groupId>ie.clinic</groupId>\n  <artifactId>vet-clinic</artifactId>\n  <version>1.0.0</version>\n  <packaging>jar</packaging>\n\n  <properties>\n    <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>\n    <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>\n    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>\n  </properties>\n\n  <dependencies>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>\n      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>\n      <version>5.10.2</version>\n      <scope>test</scope>\n    </dependency>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>\n      <artifactId>mockito-core</artifactId>\n      <version>5.11.0</version>\n      <scope>test</scope>\n    </dependency>\n  </dependencies>\n\n  <build>\n    <plugins>\n      <plugin>\n        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>\n        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>\n        <version>3.2.5</version>\n      </plugin>\n      <plugin>\n        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>\n        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>\n        <version>0.8.11</version>\n        <executions>\n          <execution>\n            <id>prepare-agent</id>\n            <goals><goal>prepare-agent</goal></goals>\n          </execution>\n          <execution>\n            <id>report</id>\n            <phase>test</phase>\n            <goals><goal>report</goal></goals>\n          </execution>\n        </executions>\n      </plugin>\n    </plugins>\n  </build>\n</project>"},"Plugin = an extension that adds tasks (goals) to the build, packaged as a JAR and added in `<build><plugins>`. Examples: compiler (compile), surefire (run unit tests), jar, jacoco (coverage), spring-boot-maven-plugin (executable JAR).","JaCoCo set-up: `prepare-agent` sets the argLine so Surefire starts the JVM with the JaCoCo agent; `report` reads `target/jacoco.exec` and writes the HTML report to `target/site/jacoco/`. Bound to the test phase, `mvn clean test` produces the report."]},{"heading":"Dependencies in detail","items":["Add a dependency: write its GAV (find it on mvnrepository.com); Maven downloads it and its transitive dependencies.","Use scope `test` for JUnit/Mockito so they are not shipped.","Version conflicts: nearest definition wins; use `dependencyManagement` or exclusions to force versions.","Exclusion example: `<exclusions><exclusion><groupId>commons-logging</groupId><artifactId>commons-logging</artifactId></exclusion></exclusions>` inside a dependency.","SCA link: dependency versions in the pom are what Dependabot/Trivy read; bumping a vulnerable version is a pom edit."]},{"heading":"Typical exam asks","items":["\"Write a pom.xml for project X using Java 17, with JUnit 5 and Mockito\": use the example above and change coordinates.","\"Explain the highlighted parts of this pom\": go tag by tag using the table.","\"A build fails with a missing artefact / wrong Java version\": check the dependency GAV, the repository, `maven.compiler.release`/`java.version`, then run `mvn -X` or `mvn dependency:tree`."]}],"pitfalls":["Forgetting packaging, or modelVersion 4.0.0.","Putting test libraries in compile scope.","Leaving the version out of a dependency that has no parent/management to supply it.","Hand-editing target/ instead of the pom."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Coordinates","items":["groupId","artifactId","version","packaging"]},{"title":"properties","items":["Java version and settings"]},{"title":"dependencies","items":["Libraries you use"]},{"title":"build","items":["Plugins"]}]}}},{"id":"spring-boot","title":"Spring Boot: starter parent pom, annotations, project structure","priority":12,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q2 (same question as the pom question); 0/7 papers","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Spring Boot is an opinionated framework: the pom inherits spring-boot-starter-parent, adds starters, and a single @SpringBootApplication class bootstraps an embedded server; controllers, services and repositories are wired by annotations.**","mnemonic":"PSS: Parent pom, Starters, @SpringBootApplication; then Controller-Service-Repository","source":["CT417_Notes_01a_SE_Revision","CT417_Notes_11c_Prometheus","CT417_Notes_06d_DAST"],"sections":[{"heading":"What it is","items":["A software framework: common generic code that user code can selectively override. Spring Boot adds **auto-configuration** (configures beans from the classpath), **starters** (curated dependency bundles) and an **embedded server** (Tomcat), so an app runs with `java -jar`.","Bootstrapping: the `main` method calls `SpringApplication.run(...)`, which creates the application context, scans for components, auto-configures and starts the server."]},{"heading":"Spring Boot pom (the usual \"same as the pom question\")","items":[{"type":"code","text":"<project xmlns=\"http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0\" ...>\n  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>\n\n  <parent>\n    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>\n    <version>3.2.5</version>\n    <relativePath/>\n  </parent>\n\n  <groupId>ie.clinic</groupId>\n  <artifactId>vet-api</artifactId>\n  <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>\n\n  <properties>\n    <java.version>17</java.version>\n  </properties>\n\n  <dependencies>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>\n    </dependency>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>\n    </dependency>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>\n    </dependency>\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>\n      <scope>test</scope>\n    </dependency>\n  </dependencies>\n\n  <build>\n    <plugins>\n      <plugin>\n        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>\n      </plugin>\n    </plugins>\n  </build>\n</project>"},"The **starter parent** supplies default Java version, plugin versions, resource filtering and, crucially, **dependency management**: that is why the starters have no `<version>`.","**Starters**: `spring-boot-starter-web` (Spring MVC, REST, embedded Tomcat, Jackson); `-data-jpa` (Hibernate, JPA); `-test` (JUnit, Mockito, Spring Test); `-actuator` (health/metrics endpoints); `-security`.","`spring-boot-maven-plugin` repackages the JAR into an executable \"fat jar\" and offers `mvn spring-boot:run`.","Monitoring link (week 11): Actuator + `micrometer-registry-prometheus` exposes `/actuator/prometheus` for Prometheus to scrape. `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info,prometheus`."]},{"heading":"Project structure","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"tree","data":{"root":{"label":"vet-api","children":[{"label":"pom.xml"},{"label":"src/main/java/ie/clinic","children":[{"label":"VetApiApplication.java","edge":"@SpringBootApplication"},{"label":"controller","edge":"REST endpoints"},{"label":"service","edge":"business logic"},{"label":"repository","edge":"data access"},{"label":"model","edge":"entities"}]},{"label":"src/main/resources","children":[{"label":"application.properties"}]},{"label":"src/test/java/ie/clinic","edge":"tests"}]}},"caption":"Typical Spring Boot layered structure on top of the Maven layout."},"Layers: **controller** (HTTP in/out) calls **service** (business rules) calls **repository** (database). High cohesion and loose coupling: each class one purpose.","The main class must sit in a root package so component scanning finds the sub-packages."]},{"heading":"Key annotations","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Annotation","Meaning"],"rows":[["`@SpringBootApplication`","= @Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan on the main class"],["`@RestController`","Controller whose return values are written as JSON (@Controller + @ResponseBody)"],["`@RequestMapping(\"/api/pets\")`","Base path for a controller/method"],["`@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`","Map HTTP verb and path to a method"],["`@PathVariable`, `@RequestParam`, `@RequestBody`","Bind URL segment, query parameter, JSON body"],["`@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Component`","Mark a class as a Spring-managed bean (service, data access, generic)"],["`@Autowired` / constructor injection","Inject a dependency; prefer constructor injection (final fields, easy to test)"],["`@Entity`, `@Id`, `@GeneratedValue`","JPA mapping of a class to a table"],["`@Configuration`, `@Bean`","Define beans in code"],["`@Value(\"${key}\")`","Inject a property from application.properties"],["`@SpringBootTest`","Load the full application context for an integration test"],["`@WebMvcTest`","Test only the web layer (use MockMvc)"],["`@MockBean` (Mockito) ","Replace a bean with a mock in the Spring test context"]]}]},{"heading":"Minimal working example","items":[{"type":"code","text":"@SpringBootApplication\npublic class VetApiApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(VetApiApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/visits\")\npublic class VisitController {\n    private final VisitService service;\n\n    public VisitController(VisitService service) {   // constructor injection\n        this.service = service;\n    }\n\n    @GetMapping\n    public List<Visit> list(@RequestParam(required = false) Long petId) {\n        return petId == null ? service.findAll() : service.findByPet(petId);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class VisitService {\n    private final VisitRepository repo;\n    public VisitService(VisitRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }\n    public List<Visit> findAll() { return repo.findAll(); }\n    public List<Visit> findByPet(Long petId) { return repo.findByPetId(petId); }\n}\n\npublic interface VisitRepository extends JpaRepository<Visit, Long> {\n    List<Visit> findByPetId(Long petId);\n}"},"Config: `server.port=8080`, `spring.datasource.url=...`, `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info`.","Run: `mvn spring-boot:run` or `mvn clean package` then `java -jar target/vet-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar`. This JAR is what the Dockerfile copies."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing versions on every starter (the parent manages them) or forgetting the parent.","Putting the main class in a sub-package so scanning misses sibling packages.","Mixing business logic into the controller; keep the layering.","Using field injection and then being unable to unit test without Spring."]},{"id":"unit-testing-junit","title":"Unit testing with JUnit 5: writing tests, assertions, AAA, mocking","priority":13,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q4c (you must write tests); 1/7 papers (Calculator.add, 6 marks)","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**A unit test checks one small unit in isolation, fast and repeatably. Write it as Arrange-Act-Assert with a descriptive name, test normal, boundary and error cases, and mock external dependencies.**","mnemonic":"AAA: Arrange, Act, Assert; and FIRST: Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-validating, Timely","source":["CT417_Notes_09a_Unit_Testing","CT417_Notes_09c_Unit_Testing_Challenges","CT417_Notes_10c_Testing_for_Security","CT417_Exam_2023-2024_1_1_5"],"sections":[{"heading":"What and why","items":["Unit testing checks the **smallest testable parts** of the code (a method or class) as discrete behaviours. Test code runs automatically (test automation); JUnit is the most popular Java framework, Mockito mocks dependencies.","Benefits: early bug detection; improved code quality (testable means modular); safe refactoring (tests prove behaviour is intact); faster development cycle (rerun in seconds); runs in CI to catch regressions.","Principles: **isolate** the unit (no real DB/network: use mocks and stubs); **repeatable** (same result any environment/order); **fast**; **independent** (no shared state or order dependence).","Test pyramid: many fast unit tests (isolated business behaviour); fewer component/controller tests (HTTP behaviour); few end-to-end tests (whole app meets user requirement). Integration tests check modules working together."]},{"heading":"JUnit 5 building blocks","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Element","Use"],"rows":[["`@Test`","Marks a test method"],["`@BeforeEach` / `@AfterEach`","Run before/after every test (fresh fixture each time)"],["`@BeforeAll` / `@AfterAll`","Once per class (static)"],["`@DisplayName(\"...\")`","Readable name"],["`@Disabled`","Skip a test"],["`@ParameterizedTest` + `@CsvSource` / `@ValueSource`","Same test with several inputs"],["`assertEquals(expected, actual)`","Equality; for doubles `assertEquals(e, a, delta)`"],["`assertTrue` / `assertFalse`","Boolean conditions"],["`assertNull` / `assertNotNull`","Null checks"],["`assertThrows(Ex.class, () -> ...)`","Expect an exception"],["`assertAll(...)`","Group several assertions"],["`assertSame` / `assertNotEquals`","Reference identity / inequality"]]}]},{"heading":"Anatomy of a test: Arrange, Act, Assert","items":[{"type":"code","text":"import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\n\nclass CalculatorTest {\n    @Test\n    void evaluatesSumOfThreeNumbers() {\n        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();      // Arrange\n        int sum = calculator.evaluate(\"1+2+3\");        // Act\n        assertEquals(6, sum);                          // Assert\n    }\n}"},"Arrange = set up context and inputs; Act = call the method under test; Assert = check the result matches expectation.","Naming: say the behaviour and condition, e.g. `addReturnsNegativeWhenBothOperandsNegative`. One behaviour per test. Avoid over-mocking. Test edge cases, not only the happy path."]},{"heading":"Worked answer: tests for Calculator.add (2023-24 Q4c, 6 marks)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"class CalculatorTest {\n    private final Calculator calc = new Calculator();\n\n    @Test\n    void addsTwoPositiveNumbers() {\n        assertEquals(5, calc.add(2, 3));\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void addsTwoNegativeNumbers() {\n        assertEquals(-5, calc.add(-2, -3));\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void addsPositiveAndNegativeToZero() {\n        assertEquals(0, calc.add(5, -5));\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void zeroIsIdentity() {\n        assertEquals(7, calc.add(7, 0));\n        assertEquals(0, calc.add(0, 0));\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void integerOverflowWraps() {\n        // documents current behaviour: int overflow wraps to MIN_VALUE\n        assertEquals(Integer.MIN_VALUE, calc.add(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 1));\n    }\n}"},"Three test cases required by the question: (1) positive operands, (2) negative operands (and mixed sign), (3) zero / boundary values (MAX_VALUE overflow). Explain WHY each is chosen: different sign combinations exercise different arithmetic paths; zero is the identity; overflow is the boundary of the int type. If the requirement says overflow must be rejected, implement `Math.addExact` and `assertThrows(ArithmeticException.class, ...)`."]},{"heading":"Boundary tests and exceptions (EmailAddress style)","items":["Domain rules (hospital email): domain hospital.com, length 15 to 77, local part a-z, 0-9 and one dot, not starting/ending with a dot.","Accept boundaries: exactly 15 chars; exactly 77 chars; a local part at its limit. Reject boundaries: 14 chars; 78 chars; multiple @; wrong domain; subdomain; leading/trailing dot; more than one dot; null, \"null\", \"0\", \"\\t\" (whitespace), underscore.",{"type":"code","text":"@Test\nvoid rejectsAddressShorterThanFifteenCharacters() {\n    assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,\n        () -> new EmailAddress(\"a@hospital.com\"));   // 14 chars\n}\n\n@ParameterizedTest\n@ValueSource(strings = {\"jane@hospital.com\", \"jane01@hospital.com\", \"jane.doe@hospital.com\"})\nvoid acceptsValidAddresses(String email) {\n    assertEquals(email, new EmailAddress(email).value());\n}"},"Check rejections as well as successes, and that a rejected operation leaves existing state unchanged.","Domain primitive (slide): EmailAddress is a value object representing an important concept of the domain model (domain-driven design: class and method names match the business domain). Constructing it validates with `matchesPattern(value, regex, message)`, so invalid addresses cannot exist; new rules (e.g. reject null/empty) are added in one place (versions 1, 2, 3 of the class).","Slide boundary cases. Accept: exactly 15 chars `repeat(\"X\", 2) + \"@hospital.com\"` (2 + 13); local part 64 chars `repeat(\"X\", 64) + \"@hospital.com\"`, which is also exactly 77 chars (64 + 13). Reject: exactly 14 chars `a@hospital.com`; local part 65 chars `repeat(\"X\", 65) + ...`; `vv@b@hospital.com`; `repeat(\"X\", 5) + \"@hospitals.com\"`; `vv.vv@here.hospital.com`; `.jane@hospital.com` and `jane.@hospital.com`; `Jane.h.doe@hospital.com`. Invalid inputs: null, \"null\", \"0\", \"\t\", \"Jane_Doe@hospital.com\". `StringUtils.repeat` builds long test strings."]},{"heading":"Mocking with Mockito","items":["A **mock** replaces a dependency (repository, email service, API client) so only your class is under test. Stub a return value with `when(...).thenReturn(...)`; verify an interaction with `verify(...)`.",{"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)\nclass AppointmentServiceTest {\n    @Mock AppointmentRepository repo;\n    @Mock NotificationService notifier;\n    @InjectMocks AppointmentService service;\n\n    @Test\n    void booksAppointmentAndNotifiesOwner() {\n        // Arrange\n        Pet pet = new Pet(1L, \"Rex\", \"owner@mail.ie\");\n        when(repo.isSlotFree(LocalDateTime.of(2026, 1, 5, 10, 0))).thenReturn(true);\n\n        // Act\n        boolean booked = service.book(pet, LocalDateTime.of(2026, 1, 5, 10, 0));\n\n        // Assert\n        assertTrue(booked);\n        verify(repo).save(any(Appointment.class));\n        verify(notifier, times(1)).send(eq(\"owner@mail.ie\"), anyString());\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void rejectsBookingWhenSlotTaken() {\n        when(repo.isSlotFree(any())).thenReturn(false);\n        boolean booked = service.book(new Pet(1L, \"Rex\", \"o@mail.ie\"), LocalDateTime.now());\n        assertFalse(booked);\n        verify(repo, never()).save(any());\n        verifyNoInteractions(notifier);\n    }\n}"},"A test that only reads back a stubbed value proves the configuration, not the code. Keep the behaviour under test real; mock only what crosses the test boundary."]},{"heading":"Unit tests in CI/CD","items":["Run automatically on every push/PR (`mvn test` or `mvn verify` in the workflow) for fast feedback and regression protection.","Metrics: code coverage (how much is executed; high coverage helps confidence but is not the only quality measure) and flakiness (tests that fail randomly erode trust)."]}],"pitfalls":["Tests with no assertions, or asserting only that a mock returns what you stubbed.","Only happy-path tests: always add negative, zero, boundary and exception cases.","Tests that depend on each other, on order, on the real database, time or random values.","One giant test covering many behaviours; vague names like test1."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Arrange","sub":"set up inputs and mocks","color":0},{"label":"Act","sub":"call the one unit","color":1},{"label":"Assert","sub":"check the result","color":2},{"label":"Cover","sub":"normal, boundary and error cases","color":3}]}}},{"id":"code-critique-flawed-code","title":"Find what is wrong with this code (design and intent flaws)","priority":14,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q4c (5 marks): not syntax; one-liners, functional Java","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**Ignore syntax. Look for hidden side effects, null handling, unclear names, mutability, overloaded one-liners (streams, lambdas, Optional misuse) and untestable coupling. For each say the problem, why it matters, and the fix.**","mnemonic":"SNUMOC: Side effects, Null handling, Unclear names, Mutability, Overloaded one-liners, Coupling/untestable (Some Nasty Unseen Mistakes Often Creep in)","source":["CT417_Notes_09a_Unit_Testing","CT417_Notes_09c_Unit_Testing_Challenges","CT417_Notes_10a_Software_Quality","CT417_Notes_07_Software_Architecture"],"sections":[{"heading":"How to attack it","items":["The first sub-part is \"identify the problem in this implementation\". The code compiles. The lecturer writes dense one-liners and functional Java (streams, lambdas, Optional) to hide the flaw, so slow down and ask: what does this actually do, and what could go wrong or surprise a caller?","Checklist SNUMOC: **S**ide effects (does a getter/stream mutate state?), **N**ull handling (null returns, Optional.get, NPE), **U**nclear naming and magic numbers, **M**utability (leaked internal collections, static state), **O**verloaded one-liners (too many operations; hidden business rules), **C**oupling that blocks tests (new inside methods, static calls, LocalDate.now, System.out).","Structure of each point (1-1.5 marks): the problem, why it is a problem (bug risk / maintainability / testability), the fix (ideally a 2-5 line rewrite).","Link to testing: if you cannot unit test it in isolation (time, I/O, randomness hardcoded) the design is flawed. Link to SRP: if the name needs \"and\" the method does too much."]},{"heading":"Example 1: hidden side effect in a getter","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public class Invoice {\n    private final List<LineItem> items = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public double getTotal() {\n        double total = 0;\n        for (LineItem i : items) {\n            total += i.getPrice() * i.getQty();\n            i.setBilled(true);          // mutates state\n        }\n        items.removeIf(i -> i.getQty() == 0);   // mutates the list\n        return total;\n    }\n}"},"**Problem**: `getTotal()` reads like a query but changes state (marks items billed, removes items). Calling it twice or just to display a total changes the data.","**Why**: surprising behaviour, order-dependent results, hard to test (the first call differs from the second), not thread safe.","**Fix**: separate query from command: `getTotal()` is pure; `markBilled()` is an explicit method. `double total = items.stream().mapToDouble(i -> i.getPrice() * i.getQty()).sum();`","Also: `double` for money (rounding); use `BigDecimal` or integer cents."]},{"heading":"Example 2: stream/forEach with external mutation","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public List<String> activeOwnerNames(List<Owner> owners) {\n    List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\n    owners.stream()\n          .filter(o -> o.getPets().size() > 0)\n          .forEach(o -> names.add(o.getName().toUpperCase()));\n    return names;\n}"},"**Problem**: `forEach` is used to mutate an outside list from inside the lambda (side effect in a stream). It also dereferences `getName()` and `getPets()` with no null check.","**Why**: breaks if someone switches to `parallelStream()` (race condition, ArrayList not thread-safe, lost elements); defeats the declarative purpose of streams; hard to reason about.","**Fix**: `return owners.stream().filter(o -> !o.getPets().isEmpty()).map(o -> o.getName().toUpperCase()).collect(Collectors.toList());` and handle null names; `!isEmpty()` is clearer than `size() > 0`."]},{"heading":"Example 3: Optional misuse","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public Optional<Pet> findPet(String id) {\n    Pet pet = repo.find(id);          // returns null when absent\n    return Optional.of(pet);          // (1)\n}\n\npublic String petName(String id) {\n    Optional<Pet> p = findPet(id);\n    if (p.isPresent()) {\n        return p.get().getName();\n    } else {\n        return null;                   // (2)\n    }\n}\n\nPet p = findPet(\"42\").get();           // (3)"},"(1) `Optional.of(null)` throws NullPointerException: use `Optional.ofNullable(pet)`.","(2) Returning null from a method that already models absence defeats Optional; callers still get NPEs. isPresent/get is just a null check in disguise.","(3) `.get()` with no check throws NoSuchElementException.","**Fix**: `return Optional.ofNullable(repo.find(id));` and `return findPet(id).map(Pet::getName).orElse(\"unknown\");` or `.orElseThrow(() -> new PetNotFoundException(id))`. Do not use Optional for fields or method parameters."]},{"heading":"Example 4: null handling and magic values","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public Visit latest(Pet pet) {\n    Visit result = null;\n    for (Visit v : pet.getVisits()) {          // visits may be null\n        if (result == null || v.getDate().isAfter(result.getDate())) result = v;\n    }\n    return result;                              // null when none\n}\n\npublic int ageInYears(Pet pet) {\n    if (pet.getBirth() == null) return -1;       // magic value\n    return Period.between(pet.getBirth(), LocalDate.now()).getYears();\n}"},"**Problems**: possible NPE on `getVisits()`; returns null for \"no visits\" so every caller must remember a null check; -1 is a magic \"error\" value that can be summed or displayed as an age; hardcoded `LocalDate.now()` makes the age test dependent on the date.","**Fix**: return empty collections not null; `Optional<Visit> latest(...)` via `visits.stream().max(Comparator.comparing(Visit::getDate))`; throw `IllegalStateException` or return `OptionalInt`; inject a `Clock`."]},{"heading":"Example 5: mutability leaks","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public class Clinic {\n    public static List<Appointment> ALL = new ArrayList<>();   // global mutable state\n    private final List<Vet> vets = new ArrayList<>();\n    public List<Vet> getVets() { return vets; }                // leaks internals\n    public Date opened;                                         // mutable public field\n}"},"**Problems**: `final` only stops reassigning the reference, not changing the list; any caller can `getVets().clear()`, bypassing rules; the public static list shares state between tests and threads (tests become order-dependent); `Date` is mutable.","**Fix**: return `List.copyOf(vets)` or `Collections.unmodifiableList(vets)`; provide `addVet(Vet v)` with validation; remove static state (inject a repository); use `LocalDate`; make fields private; consider immutable value objects (records)."]},{"heading":"Example 6: untestable, tightly coupled code","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public void sendReminders(List<Appointment> list) {\n    EmailService svc = new EmailService(\"smtp.clinic.ie\");     // hard-wired\n    for (Appointment a : list) {\n        if (a.getTime().isBefore(LocalDateTime.now().plusDays(1))) {  // wall clock\n            svc.send(a.getOwner().getEmail(), \"Reminder \" + new Random().nextInt());\n            System.out.println(\"sent to \" + a.getOwner().getEmail());\n        }\n    }\n}"},"**Problems**: creates its own collaborator, so a test would send real emails; depends on the system clock and randomness (non-repeatable, flaky); prints instead of returning/logging; returns void so there is nothing to assert; mixes selection logic with sending.","**Fix**: constructor-inject an `EmailSender` interface and a `java.time.Clock`; extract `dueWithin24h(list)`; return the number sent or the list. Now a test uses a mock sender and `Clock.fixed(...)` for deterministic results."]},{"heading":"Example 7: the overloaded one-liner","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public double p(List<Double> l, double d) {\n    return l.stream().filter(x -> x > d).mapToDouble(x -> x * 1.23).sum() / (l.size() == 0 ? 1 : l.size());\n}"},"**Problems**: name `p` and parameters `l`, `d` say nothing; the magic number 1.23 hides a tax rate; it filters, taxes, sums and averages in one line (SRP); divides by the TOTAL list size rather than the filtered count (probably a bug); the ternary hack silently returns 0 for an empty list; `double` for money.","**Fix**: `private static final double VAT_RATE = 0.23;` `averageGrossAbove(List<Double> netPrices, double threshold)`; compute filtered list, handle empty explicitly (throw or Optional), divide by `filtered.size()`, use BigDecimal."]},{"heading":"Other Java traps worth naming","items":["`==` on Strings or boxed `Integer` compares references (use `equals`, and `\"x\".equals(var)` to avoid NPE).","Reusing a consumed stream throws IllegalStateException; streams are single-use.","Swallowing exceptions: `catch (Exception e) {}` hides failures; catch the specific one and log or rethrow.","Modifying a collection while iterating it throws ConcurrentModificationException.","Boolean flag parameters (`process(order, true, false)`) are unreadable; use enums/methods.","Long parameter lists, deep nesting, god classes (high cyclomatic complexity), duplicated code.","Time/randomness/IO inside business logic: not repeatable, not testable.","Integer division and floating point equality (`0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is false)."]}],"pitfalls":["Pointing out syntax or formatting: the question says the problem is not syntactic.","Naming the problem without the consequence or the fix.","Saying \"one-liners are bad\" in general: say WHICH hidden behaviour is the trap.","Missing the side effect hidden inside a stream (forEach/peek/map mutating state)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Look for","items":["Hidden side effects","Null handling","Unclear names","Mutability"]},{"title":"And","items":["Overloaded one-liners","Untestable coupling"]},{"title":"For each, say","items":["The problem","Why it matters","The fix"]}]}}},{"id":"tdd","title":"Test-Driven Development: Red, Green, Refactor","priority":15,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q4c; 0/7 papers","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**Write a failing test first (red), write the minimum code to pass it (green), then improve the code without changing behaviour (refactor); repeat per behaviour.**","mnemonic":"RED - GREEN - REFACTOR, then next RED","source":["CT417_Notes_09b_TDD","CT417_Notes_09a_Unit_Testing"],"sections":[{"heading":"The cycle","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"RED","sub":"write a failing test"},{"label":"Run: it fails","sub":"for the expected reason"},{"label":"GREEN","sub":"minimum code to pass"},{"label":"Run: all pass"},{"label":"REFACTOR","sub":"clean up, same behaviour"}],"direction":"row","loopBack":"next behaviour: new failing test"},"caption":"TDD cycle."},"**Red**: the test states the requirement and fails initially. The first run must fail for the expected reason (assertion), not a build error, otherwise the test proves nothing.","**Green**: write just enough code to make the test pass; no extra features.","**Refactor**: improve structure (remove duplication, clearer names) while all tests stay green; no new behaviour in this phase.","A new behaviour (e.g. empty list) starts a new cycle with a new failing test. One failing feature test can drive many small cycles (BDD outer loop).","**Given-When-Then** describes a scenario: given a starting state, when an action occurs, then an expected result follows. It informs test cases without a BDD framework."]},{"heading":"Why TDD and challenges","items":["Benefits: every line of code has a test; design is driven to be testable (loose coupling); small steps and fast feedback; living specification; safe refactoring; fewer defects found late.","Challenges: needs discipline and skill; slower at first; hard on legacy or poorly designed code; over-mocking makes brittle tests; needs clean code, good tests, testable architecture, known tech stack and team safety (the iceberg).","Best practice: write tests first, keep them small, run them often, never skip the refactor."]},{"heading":"Worked example: GradeBook (from the lecture)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Requirement: a GradeBook containing 90, 80 and 70 should report an average of 80."},{"type":"code","text":"// RED: GradeBookTest\n@Test\npublic void calculatesAverageOfThreeGrades() {\n    GradeBook gradeBook = new GradeBook();\n    gradeBook.addGrade(90);\n    gradeBook.addGrade(80);\n    gradeBook.addGrade(70);\n    assertEquals(80.0, gradeBook.calculateAverage(), 0.0001);\n}\n\n// stub so it compiles but fails on the assertion (expected 80.0, actual -1.0)\npublic class GradeBook {\n    public void addGrade(int grade) { }\n    public double calculateAverage() { return -1.0; }\n}"},{"type":"code","text":"// GREEN: just enough\npublic class GradeBook {\n    private final List<Integer> grades = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void addGrade(int grade) { grades.add(grade); }\n    public double calculateAverage() {\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int grade : grades) { sum += grade; }\n        return (double) sum / grades.size();\n    }\n}\n\n// REFACTOR: same behaviour, tidier\npublic double calculateAverage() {\n    int sum = grades.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();\n    return (double) sum / grades.size();\n}"},{"type":"code","text":"// NEXT RED: empty gradebook (currently returns NaN because 0/0 in double arithmetic)\n@Test\npublic void emptyGradeBookReturnsZero() {\n    assertEquals(0.0, new GradeBook().calculateAverage(), 0.0001);\n}\n// GREEN: add the guard\npublic double calculateAverage() {\n    if (grades.isEmpty()) return 0.0;\n    return (double) grades.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum() / grades.size();\n}"}]}],"pitfalls":["Writing the code first and the test after (that is not TDD).","Making the test pass with too much code, or skipping refactor.","Refactoring while a test is red.","A first test that fails because of a compile error rather than the assertion."]},{"id":"jacoco-coverage","title":"Code coverage and JaCoCo","priority":16,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"3/7 papers (coverage in Q2 each of 2024-26); hinted JaCoCo","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**Coverage measures what the tests EXECUTE (line, branch, method, instruction, complexity), not whether assertions are right. JaCoCo instruments bytecode via a JVM agent and reports in target/site/jacoco. Use gaps as questions, not targets.**","mnemonic":"LIB-MC: Line, Instruction, Branch, Method, Complexity; executed is not verified","source":["CT417_Notes_10b_JaCoCo","WK10_Introduction_to_JaCoCo","CT417_Notes_10d_Measuring_Code_Coverage","CT417_Notes_10a_Software_Quality"],"sections":[{"heading":"Coverage types","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Type","Measures"],"rows":[["Line (statement)","% of executable lines run by the tests (excludes comments, method headers)"],["Branch","Whether both true and false outcomes of every decision (if, loop, switch) ran. Twice as many branches as conditionals"],["Method / function","Every method called at least once"],["Instruction","Executed bytecode instructions (JaCoCo's base counter)"],["Complexity","Cyclomatic complexity covered: a review guide, not a verdict"]]},"High coverage can signal robust testing but does not guarantee quality: coverage measures execution, not the correctness of assertions. A line can be green with no assertion at all."]},{"heading":"Worked calculation: line vs branch","items":[{"type":"code","text":"1  int classify(int age) {\n2      if (age < 0) {\n3          throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n4      }\n5      if (age < 18) {\n6          return 0;\n7      }\n8      return 1;\n9  }"},"Executable lines: 2, 3, 5, 6, 8 = 5 lines. Branches: 2 decisions x 2 outcomes = 4.","Test classify(10): lines 2, 5, 6 run = 3/5 = **60% line**; branches: first if false, second if true = 2/4 = **50% branch**.","Add classify(-1): lines 2, 3, 5, 6 = 4/5 = 80%; branches +1 (first if true) = 3/4 = 75%.","Add classify(30): all 5 lines = 100%; branches 4/4 = 100%.","Classic trap: `if (x > 0) y = 1;` with one test x = 5 gives 100% line coverage but only 50% branch coverage (the false path never ran)."]},{"heading":"How JaCoCo works and how to run it","items":["JaCoCo (Java Code Coverage) runs an **agent inside the JVM** that instruments Java bytecode and records which instructions execute while the tests run. It receives events via JVMTI; a dump writes `target/jacoco.exec`, turned into HTML.",{"type":"code","text":"<plugin>\n  <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>\n  <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>\n  <version>0.8.11</version>\n  <executions>\n    <execution>\n      <id>prepare-agent</id>\n      <goals><goal>prepare-agent</goal></goals>\n    </execution>\n    <execution>\n      <id>report</id>\n      <phase>test</phase>\n      <goals><goal>report</goal></goals>\n    </execution>\n  </executions>\n</plugin>"},"`prepare-agent` sets the `argLine` that Surefire passes to the test JVM so the agent loads; `report` reads jacoco.exec and writes `target/site/jacoco/index.html` (default phase verify; bound to test here so `mvn clean test` produces it).","Run: `mvn clean test` (or `mvn clean verify`), open `target/site/jacoco/index.html`. A failing test stops the build before the report; to still get a report: `mvn clean test -Dmaven.test.failure.ignore=true`.","Reading the report: summary, then package, class, method, source. Green = covered, yellow = partly covered (missed branch), red = not executed. Counters: Instructions, Lines, Branches, Complexity. One overall percentage can hide very different classes."]},{"heading":"Interpreting and improving coverage","items":["Ask of each gap: which requirement or failure mode is this code? Would a miss violate business behaviour? Is it already tested elsewhere? Is it missing tests, maintainability debt, or code not worth acting on? A justified decision not to change anything is valid.","Do not invent rules (null handling, validation) just to raise a score. 100% coverage is neither necessary nor sufficient. No arbitrary 80/90/100% target.","Exam answer (explain how to use a tool to find low coverage + one strategy): run JaCoCo on the payment module, open the HTML report, sort by missed branches/lines, inspect red/yellow lines in critical business classes. Strategy: write fast, isolated unit tests (mocked dependencies) for uncovered branches and boundary rules; parameterised tests; set a `jacoco:check` rule on the critical package; run heavy integration tests nightly so the PR pipeline stays fast; run unit tests in parallel and cache dependencies.",{"type":"code","text":"<execution>\n  <id>check</id>\n  <goals><goal>check</goal></goals>\n  <configuration>\n    <rules>\n      <rule>\n        <element>BUNDLE</element>\n        <limits>\n          <limit>\n            <counter>BRANCH</counter>\n            <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>\n            <minimum>0.80</minimum>\n          </limit>\n        </limits>\n      </rule>\n    </rules>\n  </configuration>\n</execution>"},"Other tools: Istanbul/nyc (JavaScript), Coverage.py (Python). In CI use the existing workflow, upload the report as an artefact; do not duplicate workflows.","Role of coverage in unit testing (2 marks): shows which code the tests exercise, highlights untested paths, supports refactoring confidence and CI quality gates; it is a necessary-not-sufficient indicator."]},{"heading":"Lecturer's Exam Registry / PetClinic method (week 10 slides)","items":["Three ideas of JaCoCo: **instrumentation** (instruments Java bytecode to track execution during tests), **reporting** (Maven generates instruction, line, branch and complexity summaries with source-level highlighting), **interpretation** (neither a green line nor a high overall percentage proves the intended requirement was checked).","Step 0: meaningful tests first (`java -version`, `mvn -version`, `mvn clean test`); investigate test failures BEFORE interpreting coverage. Do not create a new project, re-add the plugin or write a second GitHub Actions workflow; do not change the JDK to 17 just to show reports.","Step 1: open the report; start at the summary, drill down to package, class, method, source. Slide example: instruction and branch coverage both 85%; one overall percentage hides very different classes; a class page shows which methods never ran (three Student accessors).","Step 2: \"85% instruction coverage: is that good?\" Cannot answer from the percentage alone. Ask four questions: which requirement/failure mode does the code represent; would a missed case violate important business behaviour; is it already checked by another suitable test; is it missing test protection, a maintainability problem, or not worth immediate action? A tool finding is evidence to investigate, not an instruction to add tests.","Step 3: add one test for an agreed business rule (Exam Registry: valid scores are in the inclusive range 0-100; a score outside must be rejected). Step 4: instruction coverage can rise (86% to 96%) while branch coverage stays at 90% (same 2 of 20 branches missed): a percentage alone does not say which test to write.","Step 5: distinguish **missing behaviour test** (documented boundary/rejection rule not covered) from **code smell** (duplication, long method, coupling, complex control flow) and **technical debt** (design trade-off with ongoing cost). Adding a test does not make code easier to change.","Step 6: compare the same counter at the same package/class/method level before and after; record unchanged or missed branches honestly; ask whether another test or refactor justifies its cost. 100% coverage is neither necessary nor sufficient.","Versions: Exam Registry uses JUnit Jupiter 5 and JaCoCo 0.8.11; PetClinic uses inherited JUnit 4-style tests and JaCoCo 0.8.2 (JDK 11). PetClinic checkpoint command: `./mvnw --batch-mode clean verify`; identify ONE justified improvement, preserve behaviour, compare before and after.","Counters (slide): Instructions = executed bytecode instructions; Lines = source lines executed, missed or partially executed; Branches = alternatives of conditionals and switch statements executed or missed; Complexity = control-flow measure guiding deeper review, not an automatic refactoring verdict."]}],"pitfalls":["Equating high coverage with correctness.","Forgetting that a method can have 100% instruction coverage but miss a branch.","Saying coverage target must be 100%.","Mixing up `prepare-agent` (before tests) and `report` (after tests)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Counts","items":["Line","Branch","Method","Instruction","Complexity"]},{"title":"How","items":["JVM agent instruments bytecode","Report in target/site/jacoco"]},{"title":"Remember","items":["Executed is not the same as checked"]}]}}},{"id":"api-design-openapi","title":"API design, API-first, OpenAPI and Swagger","priority":17,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted Q4b; 0/7 papers","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**API-first: agree the contract (OpenAPI) before building. Use nouns for resources and HTTP methods for actions, correct status codes, versioning, additive not breaking changes, and secure with auth, rate limiting, validation and HTTPS.**","mnemonic":"Nouns + verbs + codes; Contract first: Define, Mock, Test, Implement","source":["CT417_Notes_08a_API_First_Design","CT417_Notes_08b_OpenAPI_Swagger"],"sections":[{"heading":"API and API-first","items":["An **API** lets software systems communicate, sharing data and functionality securely. A client sends a request, the API gets data from the server and the response (usually JSON) returns.","**API contract**: methods, data formats and conventions two programs follow: method, path, parameters, status codes and response schema, i.e. the behaviour clients observe and rely on.","**API-first**: treat the contract as a design artefact agreed BEFORE implementing the behaviour behind it. In an existing system: define the intended contract change before changing the implementation.","Workflow: **Define contract, Mock APIs, Test APIs, Implement APIs**. The contract is the single source of truth for development and documentation.","Benefits: consistency across teams; reduced integration risk (issues surface while it is still a design); frontend and backend work in parallel (faster development); safer evolution (changes checked against the contract); better developer experience and onboarding; docs, mocks, tests, SDKs generated from one spec."]},{"heading":"Design best practice","items":["Name resources with **nouns** (plural), let the HTTP **method** say what happens: `/api/pets`, `/api/pets/8/visits`.",{"type":"table","head":["Method","Use","Typical success code"],"rows":[["GET","Retrieve resource(s); safe, no change","200 OK"],["POST","Create a new resource","201 Created"],["PUT","Replace/update an existing resource (or create)","200 OK / 204"],["PATCH","Partially update","200 OK"],["DELETE","Remove","204 No Content"]]},{"type":"table","head":["Error code","Meaning"],"rows":[["400 Bad Request","Invalid request parameters"],["401 Unauthorized","Authentication required"],["403 Forbidden","Authenticated but insufficient permissions"],["404 Not Found","Resource not found"],["409 Conflict","State conflict (duplicate booking)"],["500 Internal Server Error","Generic server error"]]},"Use a **consistent error format** (JSON with code, message, details) and the right status code.","**Versioning**: URI versioning (`/v1/users`), header versioning (custom header); deprecate gracefully giving clients time to migrate.","**Additive change** (new optional capability, existing requests and responses preserved) vs **breaking change** (removes/changes behaviour clients rely on). Example: `GET /api/visits` still returns the full list while `GET /api/visits?petId=8` adds an optional filter (additive). Making petId mandatory would break existing clients. A cleaner design is not permission to break a contract: make an explicit compatibility decision."]},{"heading":"API security (four points)","items":["**Authentication**: OAuth2 access tokens (delegated, limited access without sharing credentials), JWT (compact, self-contained, stateless tokens carrying claims), API keys (simple identification of calling app, passed in header).","**Rate limiting**: thresholds per API key or IP protect against DoS/abuse (429 Too Many Requests).","**Input validation**: validate types, formats and ranges; prefer allowlists to denylists; parameterised queries.","**HTTPS everywhere**: TLS encrypts data in transit and prevents man-in-the-middle attacks."]},{"heading":"OpenAPI and Swagger","items":["**OpenAPI Specification**: a standard, language-agnostic format (YAML or JSON) describing RESTful APIs. It is the contract between provider and consumer. Since 2015 the specification is called OpenAPI; **Swagger** is the tooling around it.",{"type":"table","head":["Swagger tool","What it does"],"rows":[["Swagger Editor","Write OpenAPI definitions with real-time error feedback"],["Swagger UI","Interactive API documentation generated from the spec"],["Swagger Codegen","Generate server stubs and client SDKs"],["SwaggerHub","Team collaboration on API design/docs"]]},"One spec carries an API from design to docs to code to tests; mock servers let the frontend build in parallel."]},{"heading":"Example OpenAPI document (vet clinic visits)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"openapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: Vet Clinic API\n  version: 1.0.0\nservers:\n  - url: https://api.vetclinic.ie/v1\npaths:\n  /visits:\n    get:\n      summary: List visits, optionally for one pet\n      parameters:\n        - name: petId\n          in: query\n          required: false\n          schema:\n            type: integer\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: A list of visits\n          content:\n            application/json:\n              schema:\n                type: array\n                items:\n                  $ref: '#/components/schemas/Visit'\n        '400':\n          description: Malformed petId\n        '404':\n          description: Unknown pet\n    post:\n      summary: Record a visit\n      requestBody:\n        required: true\n        content:\n          application/json:\n            schema:\n              $ref: '#/components/schemas/NewVisit'\n      responses:\n        '201':\n          description: Visit created\n  /visits/{id}:\n    get:\n      summary: Get one visit\n      parameters:\n        - name: id\n          in: path\n          required: true\n          schema:\n            type: integer\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: The visit\n        '404':\n          description: Not found\ncomponents:\n  schemas:\n    Visit:\n      type: object\n      required: [id, petId, date]\n      properties:\n        id: { type: integer }\n        petId: { type: integer }\n        date: { type: string, format: date }\n        notes: { type: string }\n    NewVisit:\n      type: object\n      required: [petId, date]\n      properties:\n        petId: { type: integer }\n        date: { type: string, format: date }\n        notes: { type: string }"},"Key blocks: `openapi` version, `info`, `servers`, `paths` (each path with methods, parameters, requestBody, responses with status codes), `components/schemas` reused via `$ref`."]}],"pitfalls":["Using verbs in URLs (/getVisits) instead of nouns + HTTP methods.","Making a breaking change (mandatory new parameter) without versioning.","Confusing OpenAPI (the spec) with Swagger (the tools).","Returning 200 for errors, or 500 for client mistakes."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Agree the contract","sub":"OpenAPI first","color":0},{"label":"Nouns for resources","sub":"HTTP methods for actions","color":1},{"label":"Correct status codes","color":2},{"label":"Version","sub":"additive, not breaking","color":3},{"label":"Secure","sub":"auth, rate limit, validate, HTTPS","color":4}]}}},{"id":"sca-security","title":"SCA: Software Composition Analysis, dependencies and supply chain","priority":18,"tier":"core","hint":"likely","freq":"hinted (\"SCA security y\", Q4a); 1/7 papers (2025-26 autumn Q3a,b) + scenario mentions","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**SCA scans the third-party and transitive dependencies you ship for known vulnerabilities (CVEs). Prioritise by severity, exposure, business impact and fix availability; remediate through a branch, version bump, tests, review, CI and safe deployment.**","mnemonic":"SEBF: Severity, Exposure, Business impact, Fix available","source":["CT417_Notes_06a_Software_Security","CT417_Notes_06c_Static_Code_Analysis","CT417_Notes_06b_Security_Vulnerabilities","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why SCA: supply chain security","items":["Modern apps use hundreds of third-party components; software supply chains are a major attack surface. A compromised dependency compromises everything that uses it.","**Direct dependencies** are chosen by us and declared in a manifest (pom.xml, package.json). **Transitive dependencies** are pulled in by those dependencies: we cannot easily control them, and reading only the manifest is not enough to know the deployed component set.","**SAST vs SCA**: SAST asks \"which security-relevant patterns or flows in OUR code deserve investigation?\"; SCA asks \"which known vulnerable components have we INHERITED?\" Evidence: SAST = file, line, rule, flow; SCA = package, version, dependency path, advisory/CVE. We write code AND inherit software; both need checking.","**SCA tool functions**: component inventory (bill of materials, SBOM), dependency tree visualisation, vulnerability data enrichment (CVE/GHSA/severity), alerts on vulnerable dependencies, licence checks."]},{"heading":"Tools and vocabulary","items":["**Dependabot** (GitHub-native): reduces risk from vulnerable dependencies, keeps software up to date, minimises manual effort. Features: 1 **Security alerts**, 2 **Security updates** (auto PR to the patched version), 3 **Version updates** (routine updates).","Other SCA/component tools: Trivy (images and filesystems), OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk, `npm audit`.","A security alert contains: package name, vulnerability id (GitHub Advisory GHSA-xxxx), CVE identifier (CVE-2024-xxxx), severity (Low/Moderate/High/Critical), affected version range, patched version range, dependency type (direct/transitive), manifest file path, reference links.","**CVE** (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures): public reference method and unique identifier for known vulnerabilities, maintained by MITRE. **CWE** (Common Weakness Enumeration): categorises the types of weaknesses behind CVEs (fewer entries)."]},{"heading":"A finding is evidence, not a verdict","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["Finding","Verify the condition","Check exposure and reachability","Consider consequence and context","Make a security decision","Act where justified","Retest"],"direction":"row"},"caption":"Reasoning chain for any scanner finding."},"Distinguish: finding (tool observed something), vulnerability (weakness verified and relevant to this system), risk (consequence and likelihood in the deployment context).","HIGH severity does not automatically mean change code immediately; severity helps prioritise investigation. \"Not currently exploitable in this context\" is not the same as \"false positive\".","A successful scanner run does not mean the application has no risk."]},{"heading":"Prioritising findings (2025-26 autumn Q3a, pick TWO to fix)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Finding","Package / version","Severity","Exposure","Fix","Verdict"],"rows":[["A auth-api","express 4.17.1","High","Public API","Yes","FIX: high severity on an internet-facing authentication service, fix available"],["B payment-api","lodash 4.17.20","High","Internal batch processing","Yes","Next: high but internal exposure; schedule soon"],["C web-app","React deps 4.0.3","Medium","Build-time scripts","Requires major upgrade","Defer: medium, not runtime, costly upgrade"],["D auth-api","jsonwebtoken 8.5.1","Critical","Customer session tokens","Yes","FIX FIRST: critical, protects session tokens/authentication, fix available"]]},"**Answer**: fix **D** (critical, used for customer sessions: token forgery/account takeover, fix exists) and **A** (high, public API on the authentication service). Justify with severity, exposure, business impact (authentication and banking trust, compliance) and fix availability. B is internal, C is medium/build-time and needs a risky major upgrade."]},{"heading":"Safe remediation workflow for the top finding (5 marks)","items":["1. **Git**: create a branch `fix/jsonwebtoken-upgrade` from main (or accept the Dependabot PR).","2. **Package management**: upgrade to the patched version (`npm install jsonwebtoken@latest-patched` or edit pom.xml), commit the updated manifest and lockfile, check transitive impact (`npm ls`, `mvn dependency:tree`), read the changelog for breaking changes.","3. **Test**: run unit and integration tests locally; add a regression test for token verification (valid, expired, tampered).","4. **CI/CD**: push, open a pull request; CI runs build, tests, SAST and SCA again (the alert should disappear).","5. **Review**: at least one reviewer approves; branch protection requires green checks.","6. **Deploy safely**: merge, deploy to staging, smoke test and DAST, then production by canary/blue-green with monitoring and a rollback plan (redeploy the previous image tag).","7. **Verify and record**: confirm the alert is closed, document the decision, keep the SBOM updated."]}],"pitfalls":["Ranking by severity alone: exposure, business impact and fix availability must be argued.","Believing transitive dependencies are not your responsibility.","Updating a library and deploying straight to production with no tests or rollback.","Mixing up SCA (dependencies) and SAST (your code)."]},{"id":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","title":"Incident metrics: MTTD, MTTR, MTBF calculations","priority":19,"tier":"likely","hint":"likely","freq":"2/7 papers (2025-26 s1 Q3d, autumn Q3c); hinted calculations","marks":"4-10 marks","summary":"**MTTD = start to detection; MTTR = detection to restoration (as the papers define it); average = sum / number of incidents. Convert everything to one unit first, then identify the incident with the smallest times.**","mnemonic":"Detect time, Repair time: D then R; add up, divide by n","source":["CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3","CT417_Notes_11a_Reliability_Monitoring"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions as used in the papers","items":["**MTTD (mean time to detect)**: average time from the incident starting to it being detected.","**MTTR (mean time to repair/recover)**: average time from detection to service restoration (autumn 2025 paper definition). Total downtime = MTTD + MTTR for an incident.","**MTBF (mean time between failures)**: normally operating time between failures divided by the number of failures. The Semester 1 paper told you to ASSUME MTBF = MTTD + MTTR per incident: follow the question's assumption and state it.","High observability and automated alerting reduce MTTD and MTTR."]},{"heading":"Worked example A (2025-26 Semester 1 Q3d)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["#","MTTD","MTTR","Total downtime","MTBF = MTTD + MTTR"],"rows":[["1","4 h","2 h","6 h","6 h"],["2","30 min = 0.5 h","1 h","1.5 h","1.5 h"],["3","6 h","4 h","10 h","10 h"]]},"Average MTBF = (6 + 1.5 + 10) / 3 = 17.5 / 3 = **5.83 h** (5 h 50 min).","Best performance: **incident 2**: smallest MTTD (30 min) and MTTR (1 h), shortest downtime (1.5 h), because automated alerting was active.","Two improvements: (1) automated alert correlation and tuned alerting thresholds/on-call paging to cut MTTD; (2) runbooks, automated rollback/patch pipelines and better triage to cut MTTR. Also observability (logs, metrics, traces), chaos/game days, blameless postmortems."]},{"heading":"Worked example B (2025-26 Autumn Q3c, from timestamps)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Incident","Start","Detected","Restored","MTTD (start to detect)","MTTR (detect to restore)"],"rows":[["1","09:00","09:40","10:30","40 min","50 min"],["2","13:15","13:20","13:50","5 min","30 min"],["3","18:00","19:30","21:00","90 min","90 min"]]},"Average MTTD = (40 + 5 + 90) / 3 = 135 / 3 = **45 min**. Average MTTR = (50 + 30 + 90) / 3 = 170 / 3 = **56.7 min** (56 min 40 s).","Best: **incident 2** (5 min + 30 min = 35 min total outage; incident 1 = 90 min; incident 3 = 180 min)."]},{"heading":"Relating to availability","items":["Total downtime in a period = sum of (MTTD + MTTR) over incidents. Plug it into the SLI formula: example A total downtime 6 + 1.5 + 10 = 17.5 h over a quarter (about 2,160 h): SLI = (2,160 - 17.5) / 2,160 = 99.19%.","To protect an SLO you can reduce frequency (fewer failures), MTTD (detect sooner) or MTTR (recover faster)."]}],"pitfalls":["Mixing hours and minutes in the average.","Computing MTTR from incident start instead of detection (check the definition in the question).","Forgetting to state the assumption MTBF = MTTD + MTTR when the question gives it.","Not justifying the \"best\" incident with numbers."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Incident starts","color":0},{"label":"Detected","sub":"start to here = time to detect","color":1},{"label":"Restored","sub":"detection to here = time to restore","color":2},{"label":"Average","sub":"sum divided by number of incidents","color":3}]}}},{"id":"docker-concepts","title":"Containerisation concepts: images, containers, layers, VMs","priority":20,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium (week 5); 2019 Q2c","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Containers package an app with its dependencies and share the host OS kernel; an image is an immutable layered template, a container is a running instance of it.**","mnemonic":"Image = recipe/template, Container = the meal; PEIFI: Portability, Efficiency, Isolation, Faster deployment, scalability","source":["CT417_Notes_05a_Deployment_Tools","CT417_Notes_05b_Docker"],"sections":[{"heading":"Concepts","items":["**Containerisation**: packaging software code and all its dependencies so it runs uniformly across any infrastructure. Like shipping containers in logistics.","**Container**: an isolated environment where an application runs, giving consistent behaviour across dev, test and production.","**Image**: a standardised package with the files, binaries, libraries and configuration needed to run a container. **Immutable** (change = new image) and **layered** (each layer a set of file-system changes: base OS, runtime, dependencies, app).","Image to container: `docker run` creates a container from an image; many containers can come from one image and be configured differently (host port, env vars).","Build flow: source code, Maven/Gradle, JAR, `docker build`, Docker image, `docker run`, running container. Dockerfile is the recipe."]},{"heading":"Containers vs virtual machines","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Virtual machine","Container"],"rows":[["Virtualises","Hardware (hypervisor)","Operating system"],["Size","Heavy: full guest OS each","Lightweight: shares host kernel"],["Startup","Minutes","Seconds"],["Isolation","Strong (separate kernel)","Process-level isolation"],["Resource use","Higher","More efficient"]]},"Benefits: portability, scalability (microservices), efficiency, isolation, faster deployment and rollback."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying containers contain a full OS: they share the host kernel.","Confusing image and container."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Image","items":["Immutable template","Built in layers"]},{"title":"Container","items":["A running instance of an image"]},{"title":"vs Virtual machine","items":["Containers share the host OS kernel"]}]}}},{"id":"git-version-control","title":"Version control concepts, centralised vs distributed, workflows","priority":21,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium (week 2)","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Version control records changes over time so you can roll back, see who changed what, collaborate and troubleshoot. Git is distributed; feature branches and pull requests are the workflow.**","mnemonic":"BRCTS: Backup, Record, Collaborate, Troubleshoot, Statistics","source":["CT417_Notes_02_Git_GitHub"],"sections":[{"heading":"What and why","items":["Version (source/revision) control = a system that records changes to files over time so you can recall specific versions later. It tracks what changed, by whom and when.","Why: backup and rollback to a previous version; record of who did what and when; collaboration in a team; troubleshooting (analyse history to find what caused a problem); statistics.","Tools: CVS, Subversion (centralised); Git, Mercurial (distributed); Team Foundation Server."]},{"heading":"Centralised vs distributed","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Centralised (CVCS)","Distributed (DVCS, Git)"],"rows":[["Repository","One central repository shared in real time","Every developer has a full local repository (clone/fork)"],["Offline work","Needs the server to commit","Commit, diff, inspect history with no network"],["Failure","Server outage blocks everyone","If trunk breaks you keep working locally"],["Example","CVS, Subversion","Git, Mercurial"]]},"Git history: Linus Torvalds used patches and archives for Linux (1991-2002), then BitKeeper from 2002; the licence was revoked in 2005 and Git was created in April 2005."]},{"heading":"Feature branch workflow and pull requests","items":["Git encourages a branch for every feature, regardless of size. When the feature is complete it is merged into trunk (main).","A pull request asks another developer/maintainer to merge your feature into their repository; the team reviews and discusses before merge. Leads can track changes.","pull = fetch + merge (can run individually); push uploads local commits; clone copies a remote repository."]},{"heading":"What to check in","items":["Check in everything that influences the build (config files, encodings, settings) and everything needed to set up the project from a clean checkout (source, docs, manuals, images, datasets).","Do NOT check in: binaries (JARs, build outputs), intermediate files (.pyc, .o), files with absolute paths, personal preferences/IDE junk."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying Git and GitHub are the same: Git is the VCS, GitHub is a hosting and collaboration service.","Committing build output such as target/."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Why","sub":"roll back, see who changed what, collaborate","color":0},{"label":"Git is distributed","color":1},{"label":"Feature branch","color":2},{"label":"Pull request","sub":"review","color":3},{"label":"Merge to main","color":4}]}}},{"id":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","title":"SAST, DAST, SonarQube and OWASP ZAP in the pipeline","priority":22,"tier":"likely","freq":"3/7 papers (2024-25 Q2, 2025-26 s1 Q2, autumn Q2)","marks":"5-20 marks","summary":"**SAST analyses source code without running it (early, needs code); DAST attacks the running app from outside (later, no code needed). They find different things, so use both in CI/CD. Tune SonarQube with incremental analysis; fix ZAP failures by ensuring the target is up and reports are saved.**","mnemonic":"SAST = Source, Static, Soon (early). DAST = Dynamic, Deployed, Down-the-line","source":["CT417_Notes_06c_Static_Code_Analysis","CT417_Notes_06d_DAST","CT417_Notes_06e_OWASP_ZAP","CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"SAST vs DAST (5-mark comparison)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","SAST (Static Application Security Testing)","DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing)"],"rows":[["When","Early: while coding, on commit/PR, before running (shift left)","Later: needs a running app, in test/staging, after deployment"],["Source code needed?","Yes (white-box: source, bytecode, config)","No (black-box: only HTTP/HTTPS surface)"],["Finds","Insecure code patterns, injection flows (source to sink), hard-coded secrets, dangerous APIs, also bugs/code smells","Runtime issues: missing security headers, cookie flags, exposed endpoints (h2-console), auth/session problems, error handling, some injection/XSS, config issues"],["Misses","Runtime configuration, whether it is reachable/exploitable","Unreachable code, paths the scanner never discovers, logic flaws, dependency issues not exercised"],["Speed/cost","Fast, cheap to fix (early)","Slower, needs environment"],["Tools","SonarQube, Semgrep, CodeQL","OWASP ZAP, Burp Suite"]]},"**SCA** (third): dependencies and known CVEs. The three see different surfaces and complement each other: none gives a complete picture. SAST = what the source suggests might be wrong; SCA = known weaknesses in components; DAST = what an external client can observe.","Why integrate in CI/CD (2 marks): finds vulnerabilities early and automatically on every change, cheaper to fix, consistent (no manual-only checks), keeps release speed, provides continuous security evidence and compliance (shift-left)."]},{"heading":"How static analysis works","items":["Source/program representation, parse structure, apply rules and analysis, track data flow and control flow, report findings, human investigation.","Techniques: syntax/structural rules, semantic analysis (names, types), data-flow analysis (source to sensitive sink), control-flow analysis.","False positives: tool misunderstood code, unreachable pattern, unused capability, another control mitigates, valid but low context risk."]},{"heading":"OWASP ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy)","items":["DAST tool placed between a client/browser and the running app: intercepting proxy plus automated spider and scanner. Parts: **History** (requests/responses), **Sites tree** (discovered paths), **Passive scan** (inspects traffic, no attack payloads: headers, cookies, disclosure), **Active scan** (sends attack-like requests: intrusive, may change state, only against systems you are authorised to test).","Alerts have a **risk** (potential impact) and **confidence**; triage: alert, request/response, affected URL, rule explanation, context, reproduce, judgement. \"No discovery means no meaningful test\": POST endpoints may not be found by spidering.","Passive vs active: passive does not generate attacks and is low impact; active probes aggressively."]},{"heading":"Why ZAP fails in a pipeline: causes and fixes (2 x 2 marks / 5 marks)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Likely technical cause","Practical fix"],"rows":[["Target app not running/ready when the scan starts, or wrong URL / network (localhost inside the ZAP container is ZAP itself)","Start the staging app first, wait for the health endpoint, put ZAP and the app on the same Docker network and use the service name"],["Scan finds nothing: spider cannot reach POST/REST endpoints or authenticated areas","Seed ZAP with the OpenAPI spec or known URLs, configure authentication/context, use API scan mode"],["Pipeline step fails on warnings (non-zero exit of baseline scan) or times out on a long active scan","Set alert thresholds/ignore rules, limit scan scope and duration, run baseline passive scan on PRs and full active scan nightly"],["No report produced: output not written/mounted or artefact not uploaded","Use report options (`-r`/`-J`), mount a writable volume, upload as a workflow artefact (`actions/upload-artifact`) even on failure (`if: always()`)"],["Resource limits/memory on the runner, or permissions on the report directory","Larger runner, increase memory, fix directory permissions"]]}]},{"heading":"SonarQube slowing the pipeline: two improvements with trade-offs","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Improvement","Why it works","Trade-off"],"rows":[["Incremental / pull-request analysis of changed files only; analyse new code (quality gate on new code)","Scans a small diff instead of the whole code base, so feedback is fast","Legacy code issues are not re-examined each PR; need periodic full scans"],["Run the full scan asynchronously/nightly or on merge to main, in parallel to build/tests","Takes the slow scan off the PR critical path","Findings arrive later, so a vulnerable change could merge before the result"],["Exclude generated, test, vendor and third-party code; cache the scanner cache and dependencies","Less code to analyse; no repeated downloads","Risk of excluding something that matters; cache management"],["Dedicated/larger runner or analyse modules in parallel","More compute reduces time","Costs more infrastructure"]]},"Do not reduce quality: keep the same rules and quality gate; change WHEN and WHAT is analysed, not how strictly."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying DAST needs source code or SAST needs a running app.","Treating a scanner alert as proof of a vulnerability.","Suggesting disabling rules to speed up SonarQube (reduces quality).","Running ZAP active scan on systems you do not own."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"SAST","items":["Reads source code","Does not run it","Early","SonarQube"]},{"title":"DAST","items":["Attacks the running app","No code needed","Later","OWASP ZAP"]},{"title":"Pipeline","items":["Use both"]}]}}},{"id":"test-doubles-flaky","title":"Test doubles, over-mocking and flaky tests","priority":23,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium (week 9)","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Stub supplies answers, mock verifies interactions, spy wraps real objects, fake is a simple working substitute. Over-mocking hides bugs; flaky tests pass and fail randomly: fix with injected clocks and controlled async.**","mnemonic":"Stub answers, Mock verifies, Spy watches, Fake works (in memory)","source":["CT417_Notes_09c_Unit_Testing_Challenges"],"sections":[{"heading":"Test doubles","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Double","Role","Example"],"rows":[["Stub","Supplies predefined answers so the unit gets controlled inputs","`when(repo.find(1)).thenReturn(pet)`"],["Mock","Stub that also lets the test verify interactions","`verify(notifier).send(...)` called once"],["Spy","Wraps a real object; real behaviour with selective stubbing/verification","`spy(new ArrayList<>())`"],["Fake","Simplified working substitute","In-memory repository"]]},"Why mock: isolate the unit, run fast (no real DB), repeatable outcomes. A passing mock test does not prove the real repository or network integration works: that needs integration tests."]},{"heading":"Over-mocking","items":["Mocking so many collaborators that tests pass although the real objects are buggy; tests become brittle and unreadable.","Do not mock critical business logic; do not test logic only through mocks; mock only the boundary (DB, network, clock).","If repository and API client are both mocked, bugs in the real integration are never caught."]},{"heading":"Flaky tests","items":["A flaky test passes or fails intermittently with no code change. Causes: uncontrolled services (DB, APIs), timing and timeouts, random data, async and multithreading, test-order dependence, wall-clock time.","Why bad: developer time wasted diagnosing, pipelines fail randomly, trust erodes and tests get ignored.","Mitigation: make inputs deterministic; **inject a `java.time.Clock`** and use `Clock.fixed(...)` in the test; isolate external services in unit tests but keep separate integration tests; control async completion explicitly rather than `Thread.sleep`; retries can diagnose infrastructure flakiness but must not hide nondeterminism.",{"type":"code","text":"class Reminder {\n    private final Clock clock;\n    Reminder(Clock clock) { this.clock = clock; }\n    boolean isDue(LocalDateTime t) { return t.isBefore(LocalDateTime.now(clock).plusDays(1)); }\n}\n\n// test\nClock fixed = Clock.fixed(Instant.parse(\"2026-01-05T09:00:00Z\"), ZoneOffset.UTC);\nassertTrue(new Reminder(fixed).isDue(LocalDateTime.of(2026, 1, 5, 12, 0)));"}]},{"heading":"Practical takeaways","items":["1. Keep the behaviour under test real. 2. Assert observable results, not merely that a stub returns what it was told. 3. Avoid wall-clock time, randomness, order dependence and arbitrary sleeps. 4. Check rejected operations and that state is unchanged after rejection."]}],"pitfalls":["Confusing mock and stub.","Adding retries instead of fixing the nondeterminism."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Doubles","items":["Stub: gives answers","Mock: verifies calls","Spy: wraps real object","Fake: simple substitute"]},{"title":"Dangers","items":["Over-mocking hides bugs"]},{"title":"Flaky tests","items":["Injected clocks","Controlled async"]}]}}},{"id":"design-patterns-refactoring","title":"Refactoring, polymorphism, Factory and other design patterns","priority":24,"tier":"possible","freq":"4/7 papers (2019; 2024-25 Q4; 2025-26 s1 Q4 and autumn Q4); not in the hint list","marks":"4-20 marks","summary":"**Refactoring restructures code without changing behaviour to remove duplication and coupling. Replace role if/else with an abstract User + subclasses (polymorphism), a Factory for creation, Strategy for payment methods, Observer for notifications, honouring the Open/Closed Principle.**","mnemonic":"Branch on type? Use polymorphism. Creating by type? Factory. Swappable algorithm? Strategy. Notify many? Observer","source":["CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3","CT417_Notes_07_Software_Architecture"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why design patterns and refactoring (4-mark answers)","items":["**Design pattern**: a reusable, proven solution to a recurring design problem, described by name, problem, solution and consequences. Importance: shared vocabulary, proven structure, maintainability and extensibility, loose coupling, less duplication, faster design, promotes OO principles (SOLID).","**Refactoring**: restructuring existing code without changing external behaviour. Improves reusability by: removing duplicated logic into shared methods/classes; extracting abstractions (interfaces/abstract classes) so many callers reuse one implementation; splitting large classes into cohesive units (SRP); lowering coupling so parts can be reused independently; making code easier to test, which lets you refactor safely; reducing technical debt.","**Cohesion/coupling**: aim for high cohesion (a class has one well-defined purpose = Single Responsibility) and loose coupling (depend on abstractions, limit dependencies).","**Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**: open for extension, closed for modification: add new behaviour by adding new classes, not editing tested code."]},{"heading":"Refactoring 1: polymorphism instead of per-role methods (2024-25 Q4c)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public abstract class User {\n    protected final String username;\n    protected final String password;\n    protected User(String username, String password) {\n        this.username = username;\n        this.password = password;\n    }\n    public abstract boolean authenticate();      // each role implements its own logic\n}\n\npublic class Customer extends User {\n    public Customer(String u, String p, String loyaltyId) { super(u, p); }\n    @Override public boolean authenticate() {\n        System.out.println(\"Authenticated Customer\");\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Manager extends User {\n    public Manager(String u, String p, String storeLocation) { super(u, p); }\n    @Override public boolean authenticate() {\n        System.out.println(\"Authenticated Manager\");\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class UserController {\n    public boolean authenticate(User user) {      // one method for all roles\n        return user != null && user.authenticate();\n    }\n}"},"The controller depends on the abstraction `User`. A new role needs a new subclass only; no if/else chain to edit."]},{"heading":"Refactoring 2: Factory pattern for creation (2024-25 Q4d)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"public class UserController {\n    public User createAccount(String role, String username, String password, String extraInfo) {\n        User user = UserFactory.createUser(role, username, password, extraInfo);\n        // persist user, send welcome message ...\n        return user;\n    }\n}"},"The two duplicated methods (createCustomerAccount, createManagerAccount) collapse into one that delegates object creation to the factory. Creation logic is centralised; the controller does not use `new Customer(...)`."]},{"heading":"Refactoring 3: Open/Closed factory (2024-25 Q4e)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"@FunctionalInterface\ninterface UserCreator {\n    User create(String username, String password, String extraInfo);\n}\n\npublic class UserFactory {\n    private static final Map<String, UserCreator> CREATORS = new HashMap<>();\n    static {\n        register(\"customer\", Customer::new);\n        register(\"manager\", Manager::new);\n    }\n    public static void register(String role, UserCreator creator) {\n        CREATORS.put(role.toLowerCase(), creator);\n    }\n    public static User createUser(String role, String username, String password, String extraInfo) {\n        UserCreator creator = CREATORS.get(role.toLowerCase());\n        if (creator == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid role: \" + role);\n        return creator.create(username, password, extraInfo);\n    }\n}\n\n// adding Admin needs no change to createUser:\nUserFactory.register(\"admin\", Admin::new);"},"The original switch must be edited for every new role (violates OCP). A registry map (or Spring-injected `Map<String, UserCreator>` of beans) lets new roles register themselves. Reflection is another option but is less type-safe."]},{"heading":"SkyBankAI: other problems and patterns (2025-26 Q4b)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Problem in the scenario","Pattern","Why it helps"],"rows":[["Repetitive logic for different user roles (if role equals ...)","Factory + polymorphism (abstract User)","One creation point and role-specific behaviour in subclasses: extensible, no controller edits (OCP)"],["Payment methods handled by conditional statements","Strategy","Each method (card, bank transfer, wallet) implements `PaymentStrategy.pay()`; choose at runtime; add a method without touching existing code"],["Hard-coded notification integrations (email, SMS, push)","Observer (or Strategy/Adapter behind an interface)","Publishers notify subscribed channels; add/remove channels without changing the core; adapters isolate vendor APIs"],["Asynchronous workflows planned","Observer / event-driven messaging, Command","Decouple producers from consumers; queue work; easier scaling and retry"]]},"Format for each (3 marks): describe the specific problem, name the pattern, explain the improvement in extensibility, maintainability or modularity.",{"type":"code","text":"interface PaymentStrategy { void pay(long amountCents); }\nclass CardPayment implements PaymentStrategy { public void pay(long c) { /* card gateway */ } }\nclass WalletPayment implements PaymentStrategy { public void pay(long c) { /* wallet */ } }\nclass Checkout {\n    private final PaymentStrategy strategy;\n    Checkout(PaymentStrategy strategy) { this.strategy = strategy; }\n    void complete(long cents) { strategy.pay(cents); }\n}"}]},{"heading":"Pattern vs architectural style (2019)","items":["An **architectural style** is a high-level organisation of a whole system (layered, client-server, microservices, pipe-and-filter, plugin). A **design pattern** is a lower-level solution to a recurring design problem inside a component (Factory, Observer, Singleton, Proxy).","Example: a microservices architecture whose services internally use the Factory and Strategy patterns."]}],"pitfalls":["Describing patterns in the abstract without writing or sketching the refactored classes.","Keeping the if/else role switch inside the factory and calling it OCP-compliant.","Changing behaviour while \"refactoring\".","Forgetting to name the pattern when the question asks for it."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Refactoring","items":["Same behaviour","Less duplication and coupling"]},{"title":"Patterns","items":["Polymorphism: replace if/else","Factory: creation","Strategy: payment methods","Observer: notifications"]},{"title":"Principle","items":["Open/Closed"]}]}}},{"id":"cicd-concepts","title":"CI/CD pipelines: goals, benefits, pitfalls, pipeline improvements","priority":25,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"6/7 papers historically, but hinted unlikely this year","marks":"6-15 marks","summary":"**CI merges and tests every change automatically; CD delivers/deploys it through automated stages. Benefits: fast feedback, fewer integration conflicts, reliable releases. Pitfalls: flaky tests, slow pipelines, poor culture, security gaps.**","mnemonic":"Build, Test, Scan, Package, Deploy, Observe (BTSPDO)","source":["CT417_Notes_04_Build_Tools_CI","CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2023-2024_1_1_5"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Continuous Integration (CI)**: developers integrate code into a shared mainline frequently; each integration is built and tested automatically.","**Continuous Delivery**: every passing change is automatically prepared for release; production deployment is a manual approval. **Continuous Deployment**: every passing change is released to production automatically.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["Commit / PR","Build","Unit tests","SAST + SCA","Package / image","Deploy staging","DAST + smoke tests","Deploy production","Monitor"],"direction":"row"},"caption":"A secure CI/CD pipeline."}]},{"heading":"Advantages of CI (3 marks) and goals/benefits of CI/CD","items":["Early bug detection and fast feedback (broken build visible in minutes).","Fewer integration conflicts and smaller, easier merges.","Reliable, repeatable, automated builds and releases; less manual error.","Faster, more frequent releases and shorter time-to-market.","Higher quality through automated regression tests and code quality gates."]},{"heading":"Pitfalls/challenges and strategies","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Challenge","Strategy"],"rows":[["Slow pipeline, long feedback","Parallel jobs, caching, incremental analysis, test selection, split fast PR checks from slow nightly ones"],["Flaky tests erode trust","Fix/quarantine, deterministic tests (fixed clock), no sleeps"],["Cultural resistance, skills gap","Training, small pilots, shared ownership (DevOps culture)"],["Toolchain complexity and cost","Standardise on one platform, infrastructure as code, start simple"],["Environment mismatch (works in staging, fails in prod)","Containers, IaC, staging-production parity"],["Security checks late or manual","Shift left: SAST, SCA, secret scanning, DAST in pipeline"],["Risky production deploys","Canary/blue-green, feature flags, automated rollback, approvals"]]}]},{"heading":"Model answer: four improvements for FoodieGo (8 marks)","items":["1. Automate tests (unit, integration, regression) on every pull request instead of manual testing: faster, consistent releases at peak periods.","2. Trunk-based / short-lived branches with CI on every merge and mandatory PR checks to cut integration conflicts.","3. Staging environment identical to production (containers/IaC), smoke tests, canary or blue-green deployment and automated rollback to stop bad updates causing downtime.","4. Shift-left security: SAST (SonarQube), SCA/dependency scan, secret scan on PRs and DAST on staging, failing the build on high severity findings."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing generic definitions only; tie every point to the scenario problem.","Confusing continuous delivery with continuous deployment."]},{"id":"security-fundamentals","title":"Security fundamentals: vulnerability, zero-day, buffer overflow, injection","priority":26,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"5/7 papers historically (definitions, zero-day, buffer overflow); hinted unlikely (\"other security, definitions\")","marks":"2-10 marks","summary":"**Vulnerability = a weakness that could be exploited; countermeasure reduces threat/vulnerability/attack; zero-day = flaw unknown to the vendor with no patch; buffer overflow = writing past a buffer to corrupt memory, countered by bounds-checking, stack canaries, ASLR, DEP.**","mnemonic":"Zero-day lifecycle: Introduced, Discovered, Exploited, Disclosed, Patched (IDEDP)","source":["CT417_Notes_06a_Software_Security","CT417_Notes_06b_Security_Vulnerabilities","CT417_Exam_2024-2025_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2025-2026_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions (RFC 2828 Internet Security Glossary)","items":["**Software security**: implementing mechanisms and development practices so software resists attacks and keeps functioning when attacked; it is a concern, not a feature.","**Vulnerability** (RFC 2828): a flaw or weakness in a system's design, implementation, or operation and management that could be exploited to violate the system's security policy. Most systems have some; success of an attack depends on the degree of vulnerability, the strength of the attack and the effectiveness of countermeasures. In software they mainly arise from defects.","**Threat**: a potential negative action or event facilitated by a vulnerability that results in an unwanted impact; intentional (cyberattack) or accidental (earthquake).","**Countermeasure**: an action, device, procedure or technique that reduces a threat, a vulnerability or an attack, by eliminating or preventing it, minimising the harm it can cause, or discovering and reporting it so corrective action can be taken (e.g. patching, input validation, firewall, code review).","**Exploit**: software, data or commands that take advantage of a vulnerability to cause unintended behaviour (control, privilege escalation, DoS). Remote exploit works over a network without prior access; local exploit needs prior access and usually escalates privileges.","**CIA**: Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability. **STRIDE** threats: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege.","Code-level threats: unintentional (lack of secure-coding knowledge, e.g. buffer overflow); intentional (malicious insider logic bomb). Mitigations: security training, static and dynamic analysis, peer code review, job rotation, mandatory vacation."]},{"heading":"Zero-day vulnerability and its life cycle","items":["**Zero-day vulnerability**: a flaw unknown to the vendor/developer (or without an available patch), so there are \"zero days\" to fix it before it can be exploited. A **zero-day exploit/attack** uses it. Examples: Stuxnet (industrial control systems), WannaCry 2017 (Windows exploit).",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["1 Introduction: flaw created in code","2 Discovery: attacker/researcher finds it, vendor unaware","3 Exploitation: exploit developed and used (zero-day window)","4 Disclosure: vendor learns / public advisory, CVE","5 Patch: fix released and deployed; window closes"],"direction":"row"},"caption":"Five-stage zero-day life cycle (orderings vary slightly; state yours)."},"Risk persists until users apply the patch; mitigations before a patch: virtual patching/WAF rules, network segmentation, least privilege, monitoring and rapid incident response."]},{"heading":"Buffer overflow","items":["**Definition**: an attacker (or bug) puts more data into a fixed-size buffer than it can hold, so the excess spills into adjacent memory, overwriting program data or code such as the saved return address. Unsafe C functions (`strcpy`, `gets`, `sprintf`) do no bounds checks.",{"type":"code","text":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\nvoid copyCustomerName(char *input) {\n    char name[8];\n    strcpy(name, input);      // no length check: input longer than 7 chars overflows\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", name);\n}"},"**Exploit**: attacker supplies a long input that overwrites the saved return address on the stack with the address of injected shellcode (or existing code), so on return the attacker's code runs with the program's privileges (code execution, privilege escalation, crash/DoS).","**Countermeasures (give two)**: (1) safe coding: bounds-checked functions and explicit length validation (`strncpy` with terminator, `snprintf`, `fgets`), or memory-safe languages (Java, Rust); (2) platform defences: stack canaries (compiler `-fstack-protector`), ASLR (randomise memory layout), DEP/NX (non-executable stack/heap); also code review, static analysis, fuzzing, least privilege.",{"type":"code","text":"void copyCustomerName(const char *input) {\n    char name[8];\n    strncpy(name, input, sizeof(name) - 1);\n    name[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0';\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", name);\n}"}]},{"heading":"Common web vulnerability families (week 6)","items":["**SQL injection**: untrusted input changes the structure of a query (data becomes instructions). Bad: `\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '\" + username + \"'\"`. Fix: parameterised queries `prepareStatement(\"... WHERE username = ?\")` with `setString`; validation helps but does not replace parameterisation.","**XSS (cross-site scripting)**: attacker data is returned to the browser where it is executed as script. Controls: context-appropriate output encoding, safe templating, Content Security Policy.","**CSRF**: a browser is induced to send an unwanted state-changing request with the user's credentials. Controls: CSRF tokens, SameSite cookies, origin validation.","**Insecure deserialisation**: untrusted serialised data rebuilt into objects triggers unintended behaviour; restrict types.","**Improper input validation**: assumptions about type, length, range, format, allowed values not enforced; validate at trust boundaries, allowlists.","Security misconfiguration, insecure dependencies and human error are the key vulnerabilities behind most issues."]}],"pitfalls":["Defining zero-day as \"a new virus\": it is an unpatched vulnerability unknown to the vendor.","Describing buffer overflow without saying what is overwritten and what the attacker gains.","Giving only one countermeasure when two are requested.","Fixing SQL injection only with input validation instead of parameterised queries."]},{"id":"devsecops","title":"DevSecOps: shifting security left","priority":27,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted \"n?\"; est. low","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**DevSecOps integrates security throughout the DevOps lifecycle and moves it earlier (shift left) so vulnerabilities are found when cheap to fix, using automated security testing in CI/CD.**","mnemonic":"Shift left: Security as Code, Automate tests, Continuous monitoring, IaC, Training","source":["CT417_Notes_06a_Software_Security"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why and what","items":["Traditional cycles put security at the end, so costly vulnerabilities reached production. Faster development, microservices, containers and cloud increase attack surface; 2023 saw more supply-chain attacks, phishing and ransomware.","**DevSecOps**: integrate security throughout the entire DevOps lifecycle. **Shift left**: do security earlier to catch vulnerabilities before deployment: cheaper and easier to fix, fewer attack vectors, real-time visibility.","Benefits: reduced time to fix bugs; continuous security (automated tests and monitoring); better compliance (GDPR, PCI-DSS) via continuous checks; improved collaboration (security is shared responsibility).","Best practices: **security as code** (policies/tests in version control), **automated testing** in CI/CD (static, dynamic, dependency checks), **continuous monitoring**, **infrastructure as code** (secure repeatable configuration), training and awareness."]}],"pitfalls":["Treating security as a final gate; the point is early and continuous."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Plan","color":0},{"label":"Code","color":1},{"label":"Build","color":2},{"label":"Test","color":3},{"label":"Release and run","sub":"shift left = do security checks near the top","color":4}]}}},{"id":"agile-vs-devops-sre","title":"Agile vs DevOps, DevOps practices and SRE","priority":28,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted unlikely (Agile vs DevOps)","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Agile = iterative development with customer feedback; DevOps = automation and collaboration between development and operations across delivery; SRE applies software engineering to operations with SLOs and error budgets.**","mnemonic":"Agile builds the right thing in iterations; DevOps ships it reliably; SRE keeps it running","source":["CT417_Notes_01b_Agile_DevOps","CT417_Notes_11a_Reliability_Monitoring","CT417_Notes_01a_SE_Revision"],"sections":[{"heading":"Comparison","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Agile","DevOps","SRE"],"rows":[["Focus","Iterative development and customer feedback; shorter feedback loop","Automating delivery; collaboration and integration of dev and ops; whole process faster releases","Reliability, performance, scalability using software engineering on operations"],["Practices","Sprints, backlog, stand-ups, retrospectives","CI/CD pipelines, Infrastructure as Code (Terraform, Ansible), monitoring and logging, shared ownership","SLIs/SLOs/SLAs, error budgets, toil reduction, blameless postmortems"],["Gap it fills","Speeds development but stops short of deployment/maintenance","Fills the operations, infrastructure and automation gap Agile does not cover","Adds rigorous engineering to large-scale operations"]]},"DevOps = Development + Operations: a culture and practices integrating the two. DevOps engineer entry point is normally a software engineering role. Netflix and Spotify combine Agile with DevOps for continuous delivery and high availability.","Benefits of Agile + DevOps: faster, more frequent delivery; better communication; fewer deployment errors; adapt to feedback; higher quality; shorter time to market."]}],"pitfalls":["Presenting Agile and DevOps as competitors; they complement each other."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Agile","items":["Iterations","Customer feedback"]},{"title":"DevOps","items":["Automation","Dev and Ops collaborate"]},{"title":"SRE","items":["Software engineering for operations","SLOs and error budgets"]}]}}},{"id":"build-basics","title":"What is a build? Libraries and build tools","priority":29,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted unlikely; 1/7 (2023 Autumn Q3)","marks":"3-5 marks","summary":"**A build is the process covering all steps to create a deliverable: generate sources, compile, compile tests, run tests, package, run checks, generate reports.**","mnemonic":"G-C-T-T-P-H-R: Generate, Compile, Test-compile, Test, Package, Health-check, Report","source":["CT417_Notes_04_Build_Tools_CI"],"sections":[{"heading":"Build and libraries","items":["The IDE hides the build behind run/build buttons. A build tool makes it reproducible outside the IDE (CI servers have no IDE).","A **build**: generating sources, compiling sources, compiling test sources, executing tests, packaging (.jar, .war, .ejb), running health checks, generating reports.","Code libraries package reusable functionality (artefacts/dependencies): JAR files in Java, DLL files in Windows/.NET.","Build tools: Maven, Gradle (Java), npm (JavaScript), make (C). They apply build automation: the same steps every time on every machine.","Build output belongs in `target/` or `build/` and is not committed."]}],"pitfalls":["Treating compile and build as the same thing; a build includes tests, packaging and checks."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Generate sources","color":0},{"label":"Compile","color":1},{"label":"Compile and run tests","color":2},{"label":"Package","color":3},{"label":"Run checks","color":4},{"label":"Generate reports","color":0}]}}},{"id":"architecture-monolith-microservices","title":"Software architecture: monolith, microservices, cohesion/coupling, ADRs","priority":30,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted unlikely (week 7 content); 2019 old syllabus architecture questions","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**Architecture is the structure of components and how they interact. Monoliths are simple to build and deploy but scale and change as one unit; microservices are independently deployable and scalable but add distributed complexity.**","mnemonic":"Mono = one unit, Micro = many small independent services","source":["CT417_Notes_07_Software_Architecture"],"sections":[{"heading":"Concepts","items":["**Software architecture**: the structure and organisation of a system's components and how they interact; the blueprint for functional and non-functional requirements. The right architecture makes systems scalable, maintainable and efficient.","**High cohesion**: a class has a single, well-defined purpose (Single Responsibility, avoid \"Swiss army knife\" classes). **Loose coupling**: classes have limited dependencies, use abstractions, so internals can change without affecting others.","Four views: **logical** (static design model), **process** (dynamic view), **physical** (mapping onto hardware), **development** (organisation of components in the development environment)."]},{"heading":"Monolithic vs microservices","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","Monolithic","Microservices"],"rows":[["Codebase","Single, unified","Multiple independent codebases"],["Deployment","Single unit","Each service independently"],["Scalability","Scale the whole system","Scale individual services (e.g. payment only)"],["Technology","One stack","Different stacks per service"],["Fault isolation","Failure can take everything down","Failures isolated"],["Data","Usually shared database","Decentralised: each service owns its data"],["Strengths","Simple development, easy deployment, easy debugging","Flexibility, scalability, fault isolation"],["Weaknesses","Hard to scale parts, maintenance grows, tight coupling, slow risky deployments","Distributed-system complexity, operations and monitoring overhead"]]},"Netflix, Amazon and Uber moved to microservices for global scale. For small applications a monolith is often simpler."]},{"heading":"Architecture Decision Record (ADR)","items":["A short document capturing an architectural decision and its context, visible to developers and stakeholders, revealing the motivation behind decisions.","Nygard format: **Title, Context, Decision, Status, Consequences**."]}],"pitfalls":["Claiming microservices are always better; state the trade-offs."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Monolith","items":["Simple to build and deploy","Scales and changes as one"]},{"title":"Microservices","items":["Deploy and scale alone","Distributed complexity"]},{"title":"Record why","items":["ADR"]}]}}},{"id":"serverless-faas","title":"Serverless and Function-as-a-Service","priority":31,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"week 8; hinted unlikely (architecture)","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Serverless: the provider runs the servers and you write code; FaaS deploys short stateless event-triggered functions with automatic scaling and pay-per-use billing; the main drawback is cold-start latency.**","mnemonic":"Events trigger, Auto-scale, Pay per use; fight cold starts with small packages and provisioned concurrency","source":["CT417_Notes_08c_Serverless_FaaS"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions and features","items":["**Serverless**: the developer focuses on code; the cloud provider manages infrastructure (servers still exist). **FaaS** is a subset: small, stateless, short-lived functions run in response to events. Examples: AWS Lambda, Azure Functions, Google Cloud Functions.","Four principles: event-driven execution; automatic scaling; no server management; on-demand billing (no cost when idle).","FaaS components: function, event source, FaaS provider, execution environment, scaling mechanism, invocation (sync/async), monitoring and logging, external state/storage.",{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","Traditional","Serverless"],"rows":[["Infrastructure","Managed by developers","Managed by provider"],["Scaling","Manual/pre-configured","Automatic"],["Billing","Allocated resources","Per execution"],["State","Can keep in process","Stateless: external storage"],["Cold start","Usually long-running","Possible startup delay"]]},"Microservices suit steady APIs and complex domains (you manage the platform); serverless suits event-driven, bursty glue code."]},{"heading":"Cold start","items":["A cold start is the delay when no warm environment is ready: first call after deployment or inactivity, scaling from zero, host change, expired instance. Cost: latency from milliseconds to seconds, reduced throughput in bursts, variability.","Mitigation: lighter runtimes (Python/Node over Java/.NET), small package, remove unused dependencies, keep shared data outside handlers, cache; keep warm by scheduled invocation (costs); **provisioned concurrency**; SnapStart.","Architecture components: functions, API gateway (routing, authentication, throttling), event sources, data storage, managed authentication, third-party services.","Drawbacks: monitoring, security and debugging complexity; platform lock-in; shared security responsibility."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying there are no servers; the provider runs them."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Event happens","color":0},{"label":"Function starts","sub":"cold start adds delay","color":1},{"label":"Runs","sub":"short and stateless","color":2},{"label":"Scales automatically","color":3},{"label":"Billed per use","color":4}]}}},{"id":"monitoring-observability","title":"Monitoring, observability, Prometheus and Grafana","priority":32,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted unlikely (tools); concepts est. low","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**Monitoring tracks known metrics against thresholds (Prometheus scrapes, Grafana visualises); observability (logs, metrics, traces) explains why. A target being UP means the scrape worked, not that the app is correct.**","mnemonic":"LMT: Logs, Metrics, Traces; Prometheus pulls, Grafana paints","source":["CT417_Notes_11a_Reliability_Monitoring","CT417_Notes_11b_Grafana","CT417_Notes_11c_Prometheus"],"sections":[{"heading":"Monitoring vs observability","items":["**Monitoring**: continuously tracking system metrics (latency, uptime, error rate, throughput, CPU, memory); proactive; alerts when predefined thresholds are crossed; detects KNOWN problems.","**Observability**: deep insight into internal state from outputs, to understand WHY something fails. Three pillars: **logs** (text event records), **metrics** (numbers over time), **traces** (path of a request across services). Reduces MTTR.","**Playbook**: organisational goals, escalation paths, communication, general incident guidelines. **Runbook**: step-by-step technical instructions for an on-call engineer responding to a specific alert.","**Blameless postmortem**: after an incident, document what happened (timeline), why (root cause, 5 Whys) and what to do better; focus on process, not blame.","SRE vs DevOps: DevOps = culture of shared responsibility, CI/CD, IaC; SRE = specific engineering practice with SLOs, error budgets and toil reduction. They complement each other."]},{"heading":"Prometheus","items":["Open-source monitoring system: **pull-based scraping** of an HTTP metrics endpoint, stores **time series** (metric name + labels) in its TSDB, query with **PromQL**, alert rules sent to Alertmanager (email, PagerDuty).","`up{job=\"petclinic\"}` is 1 when the latest scrape succeeded, 0 when it failed. Example query: `sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m]))` (only if the metric exists).","Spring Boot: add `spring-boot-starter-actuator` and `micrometer-registry-prometheus`; expose `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info,prometheus`.",{"type":"code","text":"global:\n  scrape_interval: 15s\nscrape_configs:\n  - job_name: \"petclinic\"\n    metrics_path: \"/petclinic/actuator/prometheus\"\n    static_configs:\n      - targets: [\"host.docker.internal:9966\"]"},"Inside a container, `localhost` means the container itself: use the container name on a shared Docker network."]},{"heading":"Grafana","items":["Visualisation tool: dashboards made of panels; queries Prometheus as a data source (`http://ct417-prometheus:9090`); supports annotations (mark a deployment) and alerting.","Each panel answers one operational question (scrape reachability, request traffic, JVM heap). Alert on impact-related symptoms over an evaluation period, not every spike. Correlation across panels is not causation.","A healthy dashboard or UP target is not the same as a healthy application: also verify the user-facing operation.",{"type":"code","text":"docker network create ct417-monitoring\ndocker run --detach --name ct417-grafana --network ct417-monitoring --publish 127.0.0.1:3000:3000 grafana/grafana"}]}],"pitfalls":["Treating monitoring and observability as the same thing.","Spending time on tool detail when only the SLI/SLO calculations are likely."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Monitoring","items":["Known metrics vs thresholds","Prometheus scrapes","Grafana draws"]},{"title":"Observability","items":["Explains why","Logs","Metrics","Traces"]}]}}},{"id":"sqa-metrics-complexity","title":"Software quality assurance, code smells and cyclomatic complexity","priority":33,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted: CC formula unlikely; est. low","marks":"3-5 marks","summary":"**QA is a process (prevent defects) while testing finds defects in the product. Metrics: coverage, code smells, cyclomatic complexity CC = E - N + 2P (equals decision points + 1).**","mnemonic":"CC = E - N + 2P: Edges minus Nodes plus two Paths-components","source":["CT417_Notes_10a_Software_Quality"],"sections":[{"heading":"SQA concepts","items":["**SQA**: systematic process ensuring software meets quality standards at each phase of the lifecycle: reviews standards, tools, procedures and methodologies, not just testing at the end. **Shift left**: do QA earlier (design and coding) to cut cost of fixing defects.","**QA vs testing**: QA is process-focused, proactive, prevents issues; software testing evaluates the final product and reactively catches defects.","Proactive: code reviews, TDD, static analysis up front. Reactive: bug tracking, incident reports, regression testing, post-deployment response.","**Static testing**: examine code/artefacts without running (inspection, SonarQube). **Dynamic testing**: execute code (unit, integration, system, functional/non-functional, finds memory leaks)."]},{"heading":"Code smells","items":["A code smell is a symptom in the source that indicates deeper problems or technical debt: long methods, duplicated code, large classes (violating Single Responsibility), excessive comments, poor naming, dead code.","Smells raise complexity and make code harder to maintain and test. Missing behaviour tests, smells and technical debt are different things: a test protects behaviour but does not make the code easier to change."]},{"heading":"Cyclomatic complexity","items":["Measures independent paths through code; more decision points (if, for, while, case, &&, ||) means higher complexity and more tests needed.",{"type":"formula","label":"Cyclomatic complexity","text":"CC = E - N + 2P"},"E = edges in the control flow graph, N = nodes, P = connected components (1 for a single method). Shortcut: CC = number of decision points + 1.","Worked: E = 8, N = 7, P = 1 gives CC = 8 - 7 + 2(1) = 3.",{"type":"table","head":["CC","Meaning"],"rows":[["1-10","Low: easy to understand and maintain"],["11-20","Moderate: increased testing effort"],["21-50","High: difficult to maintain and test, higher bug risk"],["> 50","Very high: unmanageable, refactor"]]},"Best practice: code reviews, automated tests in CI/CD, dashboards (SonarQube, Jenkins) with alert thresholds for degrading metrics."]}],"pitfalls":["Forgetting the 2P term.","Treating a complexity warning as an automatic refactor order."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"QA vs testing","items":["QA: process, prevents defects","Testing: finds defects"]},{"title":"Metrics","items":["Coverage","Code smells"]},{"title":"Cyclomatic","items":["E - N + 2P","= decisions + 1"]}]}}},{"id":"sdlc-software-basics","title":"Software, SDLC, functional vs non-functional, frameworks (week 1)","priority":34,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"hinted unlikely (SDLC, definitions)","marks":"2-4 marks","summary":"**Software = instructions + data structures + documentation; it is engineered not manufactured and does not wear out. SE builds large systems by teams using process, methods and tools.**","mnemonic":"Software = Instructions + Data + Docs","source":["CT417_Notes_01a_SE_Revision","CT417_Notes_00_Module_Overview"],"sections":[{"heading":"Key definitions","items":["**Software** (textbook): (I) instructions that when executed provide desired features, function and performance; (II) data structures that let programs manipulate information; (III) descriptive information (hard copy and virtual) describing operation and use. Types: commercial, open-source, freeware, shareware, SaaS.","Software is a logical not physical component, is developed/engineered (not manufactured), does not wear out, is built from abstract logic, and must meet functional and non-functional requirements.","Ariane 5 failure: a 64-bit number crammed into a 16-bit space caused overflow that crashed primary and backup computers (same software); cost nearly $8b with a $500m payload; software reused from Ariane 4 where it worked. Lesson: need resilient, correct software and re-validation when reusing.","**Software engineering**: the field of CS dealing with building systems so large or complex they are built by teams (Ghezzi); a process, a collection of methods and an array of tools for high-quality software (Pressman). DevOps = software engineering + IT operations.","**SDLC**: the process used to design, develop and test software; many approaches (waterfall, agile, etc.), each focusing on a different aspect from planning to continuous improvement.","**Framework**: an abstraction where common code providing generic functionality can be selectively overridden or specialised by user code (Spring Boot, Django, React, Angular, Flask, TensorFlow). **Low-code/no-code**: build applications with GUIs and embedded functions with little or no hand-written code."]},{"heading":"Module facts","items":["Assessment: continuous assessment 30% (individual weekly checkpoints WK02-WK11, PetClinic) and final exam 70% (2 hours, weeks 1-12). Weekly topics: WK01 overview, WK02 Git, WK03 requirements and change, WK04 build and CI, WK05 containers, WK06 DevSecOps, WK07 architecture, WK08 APIs, WK09 testing and TDD, WK10 quality and technical debt, WK11 reliability and observability."]}],"pitfalls":["Spending time on definitions the lecturer flagged as unlikely."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Software is","items":["Instructions","Data structures","Documentation"]},{"title":"It is","items":["Engineered, not manufactured","Does not wear out"]},{"title":"Software engineering","items":["Large systems","Teams","Process, methods, tools"]}]}}},{"id":"legacy-topics","title":"Legacy topics in older papers (Z notation, architecture styles, virtualisation, web security)","priority":35,"tier":"low","hint":"unlikely","freq":"2019-2023 papers only; not in the current notes","marks":"5-20 marks (old papers)","summary":"**The 2019-2023 papers asked topics no longer in the current notes: Z formal specification, architectural styles, hypervisors and VirtualBox networking, CAP/ACID/BASE, cookies/XSS/SQL injection/hashing. A brief safety-net summary.**","mnemonic":"Old syllabus: Z, styles, VMs, CAP, cookies/XSS. New syllabus: DevOps toolchain","source":["CT417_Exam_2019-2020_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2021-2022_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2023-2024_1_1_5","CT417_Exam_2023-2024_1_2_3"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why this is here","items":[{"type":"warn","text":"These topics are not in the CT417 notes provided and the lecturer says the exam is from weeks 1-12 of the current notes. Revise only if everything else is done."}]},{"heading":"Formal specification with Z","items":["Formal specification precisely and mathematically describes requirements/behaviour. Advantages: removes ambiguity; allows reasoning and proof of properties; finds errors early; basis for systematic testing; improves understanding.","Notation: P X power set; `X -> Y` total function; `X +-> Y` partial function; `X -» Y` surjective function (every element of Y is the image of some x: ran f = Y); `dom`, `ran`; `#S` size; `U` union; `\\` set difference; `(+)` function override; primes (x') mean after state; `?` input; `!` output; `Delta S` operation changes state S; `Xi S` state unchanged.","Surjective example: f : {1,2,3} -» {a,b} with f = {1 |-> a, 2 |-> b, 3 |-> a}. ran f = {a,b} so it is surjective.","Cursor (terminal display): state: line : 1..numLines, column : 1..numColumns. Init: line' = 1 and column' = 1. DownKeyNormal: Delta Cursor, precondition line < numLines, line' = line + 1, column' = column. DownKeyAtBottom: Xi Cursor with precondition line = numLines (cursor stays). Full behaviour: DownKey = DownKeyNormal or DownKeyAtBottom (schema disjunction).","Train booking: state: booked : P PERSON, #booked <= trainCapacity. Init: booked = {}. Book: Delta TrainBooking, p? : PERSON, p? not in booked, #booked < trainCapacity, booked' = booked U {p?}. Cancel: p? in booked, booked' = booked \\ {p?}.","Supermarket: state: carried : P ITEM; level : ITEM +-> N; dom level = carried (every carried item has a level, nothing else does). Init: carried = {} and level = {}. Buy: Delta SupermarketItems, item? in carried, qty? : N, qty? <= level(item?), level' = level (+) {item? |-> level(item?) - qty?}, carried' = carried."]},{"heading":"Architecture, virtualisation, cloud, CAP","items":["Architectural styles: layered (layers with a purpose; an open layer may be bypassed), client-server, pipe-and-filter, microkernel/plugin architecture, microservices. Monolithic kernel (all OS services in kernel space) vs microkernel (minimal kernel, services in user space; more modular, safer, but IPC overhead).","IaaS (rented VMs/storage/network, e.g. need for raw compute), PaaS (platform to deploy code, e.g. developers wanting no server management), SaaS (finished application, e.g. email/CRM users).","Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on hardware (bare metal); Type 2 runs on a host OS (VirtualBox). Host = physical machine; guest = VM. Virtual appliance = pre-built VM image. Docker = OS-level containers sharing the host kernel.","VirtualBox networking: NAT (guest behind host NAT, can reach out, not reachable from outside), Bridged adapter (guest appears as own device on the physical LAN with its own IP), NAT network (several guests share one NAT network and can talk to each other and out).","**CAP theorem**: a distributed store cannot guarantee all of Consistency, Availability and Partition tolerance at once; under a partition choose C or A. **ACID** (atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability) favours consistency; **BASE** (basically available, soft state, eventually consistent) favours availability.","Proxy vs callback object pitfalls: stale/inconsistent state, network failure/latency (remote call can fail), identity and equality problems (proxy is not the real object)."]},{"heading":"Secure by design (2021)","items":["Cookies for login, shop basket and forum: session cookie holding a random session id (not user data), flags `Secure` (HTTPS only), `HttpOnly` (not readable by JavaScript), `SameSite` (CSRF), `Path`, `Domain`, `Expires/Max-Age`. Lifecycle: set via `Set-Cookie` on login, sent with every request, refreshed/expired on timeout, destroyed on logout.","**Reflected XSS**: script in a crafted link parameter echoed straight back by the server and run in the victim's browser. **Persistent (stored) XSS**: script saved (forum post) and served to every viewer. Outcomes: session cookie theft/hijack, defacement, phishing, actions as the victim. Hardening: output encoding, input validation, CSP, HttpOnly cookies.","**SQL injection**: input like `' OR '1'='1` alters the query to bypass login or dump/modify credentials; prevent with prepared statements, least-privilege DB accounts, validation.","Password hashing: store salted slow hashes (bcrypt, Argon2, PBKDF2), never plain text. Good hash: deterministic, pre-image resistant (one-way), collision resistant, avalanche effect. **Dictionary attack**: hash common passwords and compare with leaked hashes; defend with salts, slow hashing, rate limiting and strong password policy."]}],"pitfalls":["Prioritising this over the hinted topics."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Older papers asked","items":["Z formal specification","Architectural styles","Hypervisors, VirtualBox"]},{"title":"Also","items":["CAP, ACID, BASE","Cookies, XSS","SQL injection, hashing"]}]}}}],"flashcards":[{"id":"req-1","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"Name the attributes of a good requirement.","back":"Unambiguous, complete, concise, necessary, verifiable, attainable, consistent, traceable, solution-neutral (WHAT not HOW).","hook":"U-C-N-V-F-C-T"},{"id":"req-2","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"What does \"verifiable\" mean for a requirement?","back":"You can prove it is met by test, inspection, demonstration or analysis. Ask: how will we know?"},{"id":"req-3","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"What does \"necessary\" mean for a requirement?","back":"If you left it out a real need would go unmet. Ask: what happens if we do not include it?"},{"id":"req-4","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"Requirement says WHAT not HOW. Explain.","back":"It states the needed capability or property, not the technology or design used to deliver it (unless it is a true constraint)."},{"id":"req-5","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"What is a stakeholder?","back":"Anyone affected by or affecting the system (users, owners, regulators, support)."},{"id":"req-6","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"User story template?","back":"As a <role> I want <capability> so that I can <benefit>."},{"id":"req-7","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"What are conditions of satisfaction?","back":"Testable behaviours from the user's view that expand a user story; numbered (1.1, 1.2) and prioritised."},{"id":"req-8","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"What does INVEST stand for?","back":"Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable.","hook":"INVEST in good stories"},{"id":"req-9","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"Functional vs non-functional requirement?","back":"Functional = what the system does (features). Non-functional = how well (performance, security, availability, usability, scalability)."},{"id":"req-10","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"How to make a vague NFR measurable?","back":"Add numbers and conditions, e.g. 200 simultaneous clients with under 300 ms latency on a 4-core 16 GB server."},{"id":"req-11","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"Name 4 elicitation methods.","back":"Interviews, observation, workshops, prototypes (also document analysis, scenarios)."},{"id":"req-12","topicId":"req-attributes","front":"Red flags when gathering requirements?","back":"Only talking to managers, leading questions, a single method, missing quiet stakeholders."},{"id":"agile-1","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"The four Agile Manifesto values?","back":"Individuals and interactions over processes and tools; working software over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration over contract negotiation; responding to change over following a plan.","hook":"PSCC"},{"id":"agile-2","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Manifesto small print: what does \"over\" mean?","back":"There is value in the items on the right, but the items on the left are valued more."},{"id":"agile-3","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 1?","back":"Satisfy the customer through early and continuous delivery of valuable software."},{"id":"agile-4","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 2?","back":"Welcome changing requirements, even late; harness change for the customer's competitive advantage."},{"id":"agile-5","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 3?","back":"Deliver working software frequently (weeks rather than months, preferring the shorter scale)."},{"id":"agile-6","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 4?","back":"Business people and developers work together daily throughout the project."},{"id":"agile-7","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 5?","back":"Build projects around motivated individuals; give them support and trust them."},{"id":"agile-8","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 6?","back":"Face-to-face conversation is the most efficient and effective way to convey information."},{"id":"agile-9","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 7?","back":"Working software is the primary measure of progress."},{"id":"agile-10","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 8?","back":"Sustainable development: sponsors, developers and users can maintain a constant pace indefinitely."},{"id":"agile-11","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 9?","back":"Continuous attention to technical excellence and good design enhances agility."},{"id":"agile-12","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 10?","back":"Simplicity: the art of maximising the amount of work not done is essential."},{"id":"agile-13","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 11?","back":"The best architectures, requirements and designs emerge from self-organising teams."},{"id":"agile-14","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Agile principle 12?","back":"At regular intervals the team reflects on how to be more effective and adjusts (retrospective)."},{"id":"agile-15","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Which principle answers \"requirements keep changing\"?","back":"Principle 2: welcome changing requirements, even late in development."},{"id":"agile-16","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Which principle answers \"business and devs not collaborating\"?","back":"Principle 4: business people and developers work together daily."},{"id":"agile-17","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Scrum in one line?","back":"Work in sprints of 2-4 weeks with planning, daily stand-up, review and retrospective; roles: product owner, scrum master, team."},{"id":"agile-18","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"Kanban in one line?","back":"Visualise work on a board and limit work in progress (WIP)."},{"id":"agile-19","topicId":"agile-manifesto","front":"What is XP?","back":"Extreme Programming: technical excellence, pair programming, TDD, CI, frequent releases."},{"id":"chg-1","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"What is requirements management?","back":"Capturing, validating and committing to requirements, keeping change history and rationale, maintaining traceability and resolving inconsistencies."},{"id":"chg-2","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"What is bidirectional traceability?","back":"Links from requirements down to design/code/tests and back up to the stakeholder need."},{"id":"chg-3","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Why engineer requirements? (5)","back":"Trace satisfaction, establish responsibility, create visibility, assess change, support V&V."},{"id":"chg-4","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Steps of a change request?","back":"Raise, clarify, impact analysis, decide (change authority), implement, verify, update and trace."},{"id":"chg-5","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"What is a change authority?","back":"The body/person (change control board, product owner) that approves, rejects or defers change requests."},{"id":"chg-6","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Readiness vs priority?","back":"Readiness = how well defined (raw, clarified, ready). Priority = importance. A change can be urgent yet not ready."},{"id":"chg-7","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"What goes in a GitHub Issue for a change?","back":"Source request, clarified requirement, acceptance criteria, open questions, assignee, labels, discussion."},{"id":"chg-8","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Jira vs GitHub Issues + Projects?","back":"Jira = larger organisational workflow; GitHub = repository-centred (Issue = record, Project = board, PR = code link). Same discipline, different tool."},{"id":"chg-9","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Additive vs breaking API change?","back":"Additive keeps existing requests/responses (optional param); breaking removes or changes what clients rely on."},{"id":"git-1","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git init?","back":"Create a new empty local repository."},{"id":"git-2","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git clone <url>?","back":"Copy a remote repository with full history and set origin."},{"id":"git-3","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git status?","back":"Show modified, staged, untracked files and current branch."},{"id":"git-4","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git add . ?","back":"Stage all changes for the next commit."},{"id":"git-5","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git commit -m \"msg\"?","back":"Record the staged snapshot in the local repository."},{"id":"git-6","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git push origin feature?","back":"Upload local commits of that branch to the remote."},{"id":"git-7","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git pull = ?","back":"git fetch + git merge: bring in remote commits."},{"id":"git-8","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git fetch vs pull?","back":"Fetch downloads without merging; pull downloads and merges."},{"id":"git-9","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git checkout -b feature?","back":"Create the branch and switch to it (git switch -c)."},{"id":"git-10","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git merge feature?","back":"Merge feature INTO the branch you are currently on."},{"id":"git-11","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git log --oneline --graph?","back":"Compact history with branch graph."},{"id":"git-12","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git diff?","back":"Show unstaged changes (--staged for staged)."},{"id":"git-13","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git stash?","back":"Shelve uncommitted changes temporarily; git stash pop restores."},{"id":"git-14","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git revert vs reset?","back":"revert makes a new commit undoing an old one (safe for shared history); reset moves the branch pointer (can lose work)."},{"id":"git-15","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git push rejected (non-fast-forward): fix?","back":"git pull --rebase (or pull), resolve conflicts, push again. Do not force push."},{"id":"git-16","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git push -u origin branch?","back":"Push a new branch and set its upstream tracking."},{"id":"git-17","topicId":"git-commands","front":"What is a pull request?","back":"A request to merge your branch; teammates review and discuss; CI runs."},{"id":"git-18","topicId":"git-commands","front":"Sequence to commit to an online repo?","back":"status, add, commit, pull, push.","hook":"SACPP"},{"id":"git-19","topicId":"git-commands","front":"What is .gitignore for?","back":"Lists files not to track: build output, IDE files, secrets."},{"id":"git-20","topicId":"git-commands","front":"git reset --hard danger?","back":"Discards all uncommitted work irreversibly."},{"id":"mc-1","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"What causes a merge conflict?","back":"Two branches change the same lines (or one edits what the other deleted) and Git cannot choose."},{"id":"mc-2","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"What do <<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>> mean?","back":"Current branch version, divider, incoming branch version."},{"id":"mc-3","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"Steps to resolve a conflict?","back":"Find files, edit to final code removing markers, test, git add, git commit, push.","hook":"PEACE"},{"id":"mc-4","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"How to abort a merge?","back":"git merge --abort."},{"id":"mc-5","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"Type 1 vs type 2 conflict?","back":"Type 1 easy textual choice; type 2 semantic (e.g. int vs float vs short) needs design judgement."},{"id":"mc-6","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"How to prevent merge conflicts?","back":"Short-lived branches, pull/rebase main often, small PRs, ownership, formatting rules, CI and branch protection."},{"id":"mc-7","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","front":"Why test after resolving a conflict?","back":"A clean-looking merge can still be logically wrong or fail to compile."},{"id":"vc-1","topicId":"git-version-control","front":"Centralised vs distributed VCS?","back":"Centralised: one shared server repo (SVN). Distributed: every developer has a full local repo (Git)."},{"id":"vc-2","topicId":"git-version-control","front":"Why use version control?","back":"Backup/rollback, record of who/when, collaboration, troubleshooting, statistics."},{"id":"vc-3","topicId":"git-version-control","front":"What should NOT be checked in?","back":"Binaries/JARs, intermediate build files, absolute-path files, personal settings, secrets."},{"id":"vc-4","topicId":"git-version-control","front":"Git vs GitHub?","back":"Git is the version control system; GitHub is a hosted service for Git repositories with collaboration tools."},{"id":"vc-5","topicId":"git-version-control","front":"Feature branch workflow?","back":"One branch per feature, merged into main after review."},{"id":"mvn-1","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Maven standard layout?","back":"pom.xml; src/main/java; src/main/resources; src/test/java; src/test/resources; target/."},{"id":"mvn-2","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Convention over configuration?","back":"Follow the standard layout and lifecycle and you need almost no configuration."},{"id":"mvn-3","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Default lifecycle phases in order?","back":"validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, deploy.","hook":"Very Cool Tests Pack Very Impressive Deployments"},{"id":"mvn-4","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"What does mvn test run?","back":"All phases up to and including test (validate, compile, test)."},{"id":"mvn-5","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Phase vs goal?","back":"A phase is a lifecycle step; a goal is a plugin task bound to it (compiler:compile to compile)."},{"id":"mvn-6","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"install vs deploy?","back":"install copies to the local repo (~/.m2); deploy to a remote repository."},{"id":"mvn-7","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"What does mvn clean do?","back":"Deletes the target directory."},{"id":"mvn-8","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"What are transitive dependencies?","back":"Libraries your dependency needs; Maven downloads them automatically."},{"id":"mvn-9","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Maven dependency scopes?","back":"compile (default), provided, runtime, test."},{"id":"mvn-10","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Where is the local Maven repository?","back":"~/.m2 (C:\\Users\\<user>\\.m2)."},{"id":"mvn-11","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"How to exclude a transitive dependency?","back":"Add an <exclusion> with groupId/artifactId inside the dependency."},{"id":"mvn-12","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Two benefits of Maven over manual builds?","back":"Automated repeatable build and automatic dependency management (also standard structure, CI-friendly)."},{"id":"mvn-13","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Command to build JAR skipping tests?","back":"mvn clean package -DskipTests."},{"id":"mvn-14","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"Command to show dependency tree?","back":"mvn dependency:tree."},{"id":"mvn-15","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","front":"What is the super POM?","back":"The default parent all POMs inherit: default directories, plugins and config."},{"id":"pom-1","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"GAV in a POM?","back":"groupId, artifactId, version: uniquely identify an artefact."},{"id":"pom-2","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"What is modelVersion?","back":"POM model version, always 4.0.0."},{"id":"pom-3","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"What is packaging?","back":"Artefact type: jar (default), war, pom."},{"id":"pom-4","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"What is a Maven plugin?","back":"An add-on JAR that provides goals executed during the build (compiler, surefire, jacoco)."},{"id":"pom-5","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"Where do plugins go?","back":"<build><plugins><plugin>..."},{"id":"pom-6","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"Surefire plugin does what?","back":"Runs unit tests in the test phase."},{"id":"pom-7","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"dependencyManagement?","back":"Fixes dependency versions centrally without adding the dependency itself."},{"id":"pom-8","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"Which scope for JUnit?","back":"test."},{"id":"pom-9","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"What are <properties> used for?","back":"Variables such as java version and source encoding."},{"id":"pom-10","topicId":"pom-xml","front":"Multi-module POM tag?","back":"<modules> in a parent pom with packaging pom."},{"id":"sb-1","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"What does @SpringBootApplication combine?","back":"@Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan."},{"id":"sb-2","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"Why no versions on Spring Boot starters?","back":"spring-boot-starter-parent manages dependency versions."},{"id":"sb-3","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"What is a starter?","back":"A curated dependency bundle, e.g. spring-boot-starter-web (MVC, Tomcat, Jackson)."},{"id":"sb-4","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"spring-boot-maven-plugin does what?","back":"Repackages an executable fat JAR; enables mvn spring-boot:run."},{"id":"sb-5","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"@RestController vs @Controller?","back":"@RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody: return values written as JSON."},{"id":"sb-6","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"Layers of a Spring Boot app?","back":"Controller (HTTP), Service (logic), Repository (data), Model/entity."},{"id":"sb-7","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"@GetMapping / @PostMapping?","back":"Map HTTP GET/POST to a handler method."},{"id":"sb-8","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"@PathVariable vs @RequestParam vs @RequestBody?","back":"URL segment, query parameter, JSON body."},{"id":"sb-9","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"Preferred injection style?","back":"Constructor injection (final fields, easy to unit test)."},{"id":"sb-10","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"@SpringBootTest vs @WebMvcTest?","back":"Full context vs web layer only (with MockMvc)."},{"id":"sb-11","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"What does starter-actuator add?","back":"Health and metrics endpoints; with micrometer-prometheus exposes /actuator/prometheus."},{"id":"sb-12","topicId":"spring-boot","front":"How to run a Spring Boot app?","back":"mvn spring-boot:run or java -jar target/app.jar."},{"id":"dk-1","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile FROM?","back":"Base image to start from, e.g. eclipse-temurin:17-jre."},{"id":"dk-2","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile WORKDIR?","back":"Sets the working directory inside the image (created if missing)."},{"id":"dk-3","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile COPY?","back":"Copies files from the build context into the image."},{"id":"dk-4","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile RUN?","back":"Runs a command at build time and creates a layer."},{"id":"dk-5","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile EXPOSE?","back":"Documents the app port; does not publish it (-p does)."},{"id":"dk-6","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"ENTRYPOINT vs CMD?","back":"ENTRYPOINT = fixed start command; CMD = default arguments, overridable at run."},{"id":"dk-7","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"RUN vs ENTRYPOINT timing?","back":"RUN executes at docker build; ENTRYPOINT/CMD at docker run."},{"id":"dk-8","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why avoid :latest tag?","back":"Not reproducible: the image can change under you. Pin a version."},{"id":"dk-9","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why multi-stage builds?","back":"Build with JDK+Maven in stage 1, copy only the JAR into a small JRE image: smaller, no build tools."},{"id":"dk-10","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why COPY pom.xml before src?","back":"Layer caching: dependencies change rarely so their layer is reused."},{"id":"dk-11","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"What is .dockerignore for?","back":"Keeps files (target, .git, .env) out of the build context."},{"id":"dk-12","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"How to pass secrets to a container?","back":"At run time via -e or env files; never ENV in the Dockerfile."},{"id":"dk-13","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Steps to construct a Dockerfile?","back":"Inspect project, build and artefact, runtime, files, config and port, start command, write, build and verify."},{"id":"dk-14","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why does JRE base image suffice at runtime?","back":"Running a JAR needs a Java runtime only; JDK/Maven are build tools."},{"id":"dkc-1","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker build -t my-app .?","back":"Build an image tagged my-app from the Dockerfile in the current directory."},{"id":"dkc-2","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name x my-app?","back":"Run detached, publish host 8080 to container 8080, name it x."},{"id":"dkc-3","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"-p HOST:CONTAINER meaning?","back":"Left = port on your machine, right = port inside the container."},{"id":"dkc-4","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker ps vs docker ps -a?","back":"ps = running containers only; -a includes stopped/exited."},{"id":"dkc-5","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"First command when a container exits?","back":"docker logs <name> (after docker ps -a)."},{"id":"dkc-6","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker exec -it name /bin/sh?","back":"Open an interactive shell in a running container."},{"id":"dkc-7","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker stop / start / rm?","back":"Stop a running container; start a stopped one; remove a stopped one."},{"id":"dkc-8","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Error \"manifest unknown\" on pull?","back":"That image tag does not exist in the registry (e.g. :latest never pushed)."},{"id":"dkc-9","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"\"port is already allocated\"?","back":"Host port in use: choose another host port or stop the other container."},{"id":"dkc-10","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Exit code 137?","back":"Killed, usually out of memory (OOM)."},{"id":"dkc-11","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker images?","back":"List local images."},{"id":"dkc-12","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker inspect?","back":"Detailed JSON: ports, env, mounts, state."},{"id":"dkc-13","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Verification sequence?","back":"build, run, ps, logs, curl.","hook":"B-R-P-L-C"},{"id":"dkc-14","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"docker push needs what first?","back":"docker login to the registry (and correct tag name with registry prefix)."},{"id":"dc-1","topicId":"docker-concepts","front":"Image vs container?","back":"Image = immutable layered template; container = running instance of an image."},{"id":"dc-2","topicId":"docker-concepts","front":"Container vs VM?","back":"Containers share the host kernel and virtualise the OS; VMs virtualise hardware with a full guest OS each."},{"id":"dc-3","topicId":"docker-concepts","front":"Benefits of containers?","back":"Portability, scalability, efficiency, isolation, faster deployment."},{"id":"dc-4","topicId":"docker-concepts","front":"What is an image layer?","back":"A set of file-system changes (add/modify/remove) stacked to form an image."},{"id":"dc-5","topicId":"docker-concepts","front":"Why is a JAR not enough?","back":"Running also needs runtime, libraries and configuration; the image packages them."},{"id":"gha-1","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Where do workflows live?","back":".github/workflows/*.yml."},{"id":"gha-2","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Workflow top-level keys?","back":"name, on, env, permissions, jobs."},{"id":"gha-3","topicId":"github-actions","front":"uses vs run?","back":"uses runs a reusable action; run runs a shell command."},{"id":"gha-4","topicId":"github-actions","front":"What does needs do?","back":"Makes a job wait for another job to succeed."},{"id":"gha-5","topicId":"github-actions","front":"runs-on?","back":"The runner OS, e.g. ubuntu-latest."},{"id":"gha-6","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Common triggers?","back":"push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch (manual), schedule (cron)."},{"id":"gha-7","topicId":"github-actions","front":"actions/checkout does what?","back":"Downloads the repository onto the runner."},{"id":"gha-8","topicId":"github-actions","front":"How to hold credentials?","back":"GitHub secrets: ${{ secrets.NAME }}."},{"id":"gha-9","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Tag image with SHA and latest?","back":"docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest . then push both."},{"id":"gha-10","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Why push a SHA tag?","back":"Immutable, traceable version that supports rollback."},{"id":"gha-11","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Strategy matrix?","back":"Run a job across several versions/OSes."},{"id":"gha-12","topicId":"github-actions","front":"Safer pipeline vs \"deploy every main commit\"?","back":"Test on PRs, staging first, approval, versioned images, rollback plan."},{"id":"ci-1","topicId":"cicd-concepts","front":"What is CI?","back":"Frequently merging code to a shared main, each integration built and tested automatically."},{"id":"ci-2","topicId":"cicd-concepts","front":"Continuous delivery vs deployment?","back":"Delivery: always releasable, manual release; deployment: every passing change goes to production automatically."},{"id":"ci-3","topicId":"cicd-concepts","front":"Three advantages of CI?","back":"Early bug detection, fewer integration conflicts, faster reliable releases."},{"id":"ci-4","topicId":"cicd-concepts","front":"Pitfalls of adopting CI/CD?","back":"Slow pipelines, flaky tests, culture/skills, tool complexity, environment mismatch, late security."},{"id":"ut-1","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"What is a unit test?","back":"An automated test of the smallest testable part (method/class) in isolation."},{"id":"ut-2","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Arrange-Act-Assert?","back":"Set up inputs, call the method, check result.","hook":"AAA"},{"id":"ut-3","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Principles of good unit tests?","back":"Isolated, repeatable, fast, independent."},{"id":"ut-4","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Benefits of unit testing?","back":"Early bug detection, better design, safe refactoring, faster cycles, CI regression protection."},{"id":"ut-5","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"@BeforeEach?","back":"Runs before every test method to set up a fresh fixture."},{"id":"ut-6","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"assertEquals for doubles?","back":"assertEquals(expected, actual, delta) because of floating point error."},{"id":"ut-7","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"How to assert an exception?","back":"assertThrows(Ex.class, () -> code)."},{"id":"ut-8","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Mockito: stub and verify?","back":"when(mock.m()).thenReturn(x); verify(mock).m()."},{"id":"ut-9","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Three test cases for add(int,int)?","back":"Positive operands, negative/mixed operands, zero and boundary (MAX_VALUE overflow)."},{"id":"ut-10","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Test pyramid levels?","back":"Many unit tests, fewer component/API tests, few end-to-end tests."},{"id":"ut-11","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Good test name style?","back":"States behaviour and condition, e.g. rejectsAddressShorterThanFifteenCharacters."},{"id":"ut-12","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"Which boundary values for length 15 to 77?","back":"14 (reject), 15 (accept), 77 (accept), 78 (reject)."},{"id":"cc-1","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"SNUMOC checklist?","back":"Side effects, Null handling, Unclear names, Mutability, Overloaded one-liners, Coupling/untestable.","hook":"Some Nasty Unseen Mistakes Often Creep in"},{"id":"cc-2","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why is forEach adding to an outside list bad?","back":"Side effect in a stream: not thread safe with parallelStream, not declarative; use map + collect."},{"id":"cc-3","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Optional.of(x) vs ofNullable?","back":"of throws NPE on null; ofNullable returns empty."},{"id":"cc-4","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why is Optional.get() without check bad?","back":"Throws NoSuchElementException; use orElse, orElseThrow, map."},{"id":"cc-5","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why return empty collection not null?","back":"Callers need no null check; avoids NPE."},{"id":"cc-6","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Does final make a list immutable?","back":"No, only the reference; contents can change. Use List.copyOf/unmodifiableList."},{"id":"cc-7","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why is LocalDate.now() inside logic a flaw?","back":"Not repeatable/testable; inject a Clock."},{"id":"cc-8","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Hidden side effect in getter?","back":"A method named get/calculate that mutates state surprises callers; separate query from command."},{"id":"cc-9","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why is double bad for money?","back":"Floating point rounding; use BigDecimal or integer cents."},{"id":"cc-10","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"How to answer a \"what is wrong\" point?","back":"State the problem, why it matters, the fix (rewrite)."},{"id":"cc-11","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Static mutable field problem?","back":"Shared state between tests/threads: order-dependent and untestable."},{"id":"cc-12","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","front":"Why is new Service() inside a method bad?","back":"Hard-wired dependency prevents mocking; use constructor injection."},{"id":"tdd-1","topicId":"tdd","front":"TDD cycle?","back":"Red (failing test), Green (minimum code), Refactor."},{"id":"tdd-2","topicId":"tdd","front":"Why must the test fail first?","back":"To prove it checks the requirement; it must fail for the expected reason."},{"id":"tdd-3","topicId":"tdd","front":"What is allowed in Refactor?","back":"Improve structure without changing behaviour; tests stay green."},{"id":"tdd-4","topicId":"tdd","front":"Benefits of TDD?","back":"Every code has a test, testable design, small steps, safe refactoring, living spec."},{"id":"tdd-5","topicId":"tdd","front":"Challenges of TDD?","back":"Discipline, slower start, legacy code, over-mocking, needs clean architecture."},{"id":"tdd-6","topicId":"tdd","front":"Given-When-Then?","back":"Given a state, when an action occurs, then an expected result follows."},{"id":"tdd-7","topicId":"tdd","front":"Next cycle after green average?","back":"New behaviour such as empty list: write a new failing test."},{"id":"jc-1","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Line vs branch coverage?","back":"Line: % of lines executed. Branch: both outcomes of each decision executed."},{"id":"jc-2","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"What does JaCoCo measure?","back":"Which bytecode instructions/lines/branches the tests execute."},{"id":"jc-3","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"How does JaCoCo work?","back":"A JVM agent instruments bytecode and records execution; data in target/jacoco.exec."},{"id":"jc-4","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"prepare-agent vs report goal?","back":"prepare-agent sets argLine before tests; report writes HTML after tests."},{"id":"jc-5","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Where is the JaCoCo report?","back":"target/site/jacoco/index.html."},{"id":"jc-6","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Command to produce the report?","back":"mvn clean test (report bound to test phase)."},{"id":"jc-7","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"JaCoCo counters?","back":"Instructions, lines, branches, complexity (and methods/classes)."},{"id":"jc-8","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Does 100% coverage mean correct?","back":"No: coverage measures execution not assertion quality."},{"id":"jc-9","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Yellow line in report?","back":"Partly covered: a branch was missed."},{"id":"jc-10","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"How to raise coverage in CI without slowing it?","back":"Fast isolated unit tests for risky uncovered branches, jacoco:check gate on critical code, heavy tests nightly."},{"id":"jc-11","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Coverage tools for JS and Python?","back":"Istanbul/nyc and Coverage.py."},{"id":"jc-12","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Questions to ask about a coverage gap?","back":"Which requirement? Would a miss matter? Tested elsewhere? Test gap or debt?"},{"id":"td-1","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"Stub vs mock?","back":"Stub returns canned answers; mock also verifies interactions."},{"id":"td-2","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"Spy vs fake?","back":"Spy wraps a real object; fake is a simple working substitute (in-memory repo)."},{"id":"td-3","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"What is over-mocking?","back":"Mocking so much that tests pass despite real bugs and become brittle."},{"id":"td-4","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"What is a flaky test?","back":"Passes or fails intermittently without code change."},{"id":"td-5","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"Causes of flaky tests?","back":"External services, timing, randomness, concurrency, order dependence, wall-clock time."},{"id":"td-6","topicId":"test-doubles-flaky","front":"Fix for time-dependent tests?","back":"Inject java.time.Clock and use Clock.fixed in tests."},{"id":"sq-1","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"Cyclomatic complexity formula?","back":"CC = E - N + 2P (edges, nodes, connected components)."},{"id":"sq-2","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"CC shortcut?","back":"Decision points + 1."},{"id":"sq-3","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"CC bands?","back":"1-10 low, 11-20 moderate, 21-50 high, over 50 very high."},{"id":"sq-4","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"QA vs testing?","back":"QA is proactive process improvement; testing reactively finds defects in the product."},{"id":"sq-5","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"Static vs dynamic testing?","back":"Static examines code without running (SonarQube); dynamic executes it."},{"id":"sq-6","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"What is a code smell?","back":"A symptom of deeper problems: long method, duplication, large class, excessive comments."},{"id":"sq-7","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","front":"What is shift-left?","back":"Do testing and QA earlier in the lifecycle."},{"id":"sre-1","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"SLI?","back":"Service Level Indicator: a measured metric (availability, latency, error rate)."},{"id":"sre-2","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"SLO?","back":"Service Level Objective: target for an SLI, e.g. 99.9% uptime."},{"id":"sre-3","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"SLA?","back":"Service Level Agreement: contract with penalties if the agreed level is missed."},{"id":"sre-4","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Availability SLI formula?","back":"(Total time - downtime) / total time x 100."},{"id":"sre-5","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Error budget formula?","back":"100% - SLO, converted to time or requests over the window."},{"id":"sre-6","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Allowed downtime for 99.9% over 30 days?","back":"43.2 minutes (0.001 x 43,200)."},{"id":"sre-7","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Allowed downtime for 99.95% over 30 days?","back":"21.6 minutes."},{"id":"sre-8","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Allowed downtime for 99.99% over 30 days?","back":"4.32 minutes."},{"id":"sre-9","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Allowed downtime for 99.9% per year?","back":"8.76 hours (525.6 min)."},{"id":"sre-10","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Minutes in 30 days?","back":"43,200 (720 hours)."},{"id":"sre-11","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Request-based SLI?","back":"good requests / valid requests x 100."},{"id":"sre-12","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Burn rate?","back":"Fraction of budget consumed divided by fraction of window elapsed; 1 = on budget."},{"id":"sre-13","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Time to budget exhaustion?","back":"Window / burn rate."},{"id":"sre-14","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"What happens when error budget is spent?","back":"Pause risky releases; focus on reliability until restored."},{"id":"sre-15","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Lecture example: 1.44 h down in 720 h?","back":"SLI 99.8%; SLO 99.9% allows 0.72 h so it fails."},{"id":"sre-16","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Series availability of two services?","back":"Multiply: 99.9% x 99.5% = 99.40%."},{"id":"sre-17","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"What is toil?","back":"Repetitive manual automatable operations work; SRE minimises it."},{"id":"sre-18","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Why SLO stricter than SLA?","back":"Gives a safety margin before contractual penalties apply."},{"id":"sre-19","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"What is a blameless postmortem?","back":"Incident review of what, why (root cause) and improvements, without blame."},{"id":"mt-1","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","front":"MTTD?","back":"Mean time to detect: incident start to detection."},{"id":"mt-2","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","front":"MTTR (as in the papers)?","back":"Mean time to repair/recover: detection to restoration."},{"id":"mt-3","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","front":"Exam assumption for MTBF?","back":"MTBF = MTTD + MTTR for each incident (as stated in the question)."},{"id":"mt-4","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","front":"How to reduce MTTD/MTTR?","back":"Automated alerting and correlation, observability, runbooks, automated rollback/patching."},{"id":"mt-5","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","front":"Average of 6 h, 1.5 h, 10 h?","back":"17.5 / 3 = 5.83 h (5 h 50 min)."},{"id":"api-1","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"What is API-first design?","back":"Agree the API contract before implementing the behaviour."},{"id":"api-2","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"API-first workflow?","back":"Define contract, mock, test, implement."},{"id":"api-3","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"Benefits of API-first?","back":"Consistency, less integration risk, parallel work, safe evolution, better developer experience."},{"id":"api-4","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"OpenAPI vs Swagger?","back":"OpenAPI is the specification; Swagger is the tooling (editor, UI, codegen)."},{"id":"api-5","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"REST method meanings?","back":"GET read, POST create, PUT replace/update, PATCH partial update, DELETE remove."},{"id":"api-6","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"Name resources with?","back":"Nouns (plural); the HTTP method is the verb."},{"id":"api-7","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"Status codes 400, 401, 403, 404, 500?","back":"Bad request, authentication required, forbidden, not found, server error."},{"id":"api-8","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"API versioning approaches?","back":"URI (/v1/users) or custom header; deprecate gracefully."},{"id":"api-9","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"API security four points?","back":"Authentication, rate limiting, input validation, HTTPS."},{"id":"api-10","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"OAuth2 vs JWT vs API key?","back":"OAuth2 = delegated authorisation; JWT = signed self-contained token; API key = simple client identifier."},{"id":"api-11","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"Swagger tools?","back":"Editor, UI, Codegen, SwaggerHub."},{"id":"api-12","topicId":"api-design-openapi","front":"201 vs 204?","back":"201 Created for POST; 204 No Content for successful DELETE/no body."},{"id":"mo-1","topicId":"monitoring-observability","front":"Monitoring vs observability?","back":"Monitoring alerts on known thresholds; observability explains why using logs, metrics, traces."},{"id":"mo-2","topicId":"monitoring-observability","front":"Prometheus model?","back":"Pull-based scraping, time series with labels, PromQL, Alertmanager."},{"id":"mo-3","topicId":"monitoring-observability","front":"What does up == 1 mean?","back":"The latest scrape succeeded, not that the app is healthy."},{"id":"mo-4","topicId":"monitoring-observability","front":"Grafana role?","back":"Dashboards and panels querying data sources such as Prometheus."},{"id":"mo-5","topicId":"monitoring-observability","front":"Playbook vs runbook?","back":"Playbook: organisational goals, escalation and communication; runbook: technical steps for an alert."},{"id":"sca-1","topicId":"sca-security","front":"What is SCA?","back":"Software Composition Analysis: finds known vulnerabilities in third-party and transitive dependencies."},{"id":"sca-2","topicId":"sca-security","front":"SAST vs SCA?","back":"SAST checks our own code; SCA checks components we inherit."},{"id":"sca-3","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Direct vs transitive dependency?","back":"Direct = declared by us; transitive = pulled in by another dependency."},{"id":"sca-4","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Dependabot features?","back":"Security alerts, security updates (auto PRs), version updates."},{"id":"sca-5","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Parts of a security alert?","back":"Package, GHSA id, CVE id, severity, affected and patched versions, dependency type, manifest path."},{"id":"sca-6","topicId":"sca-security","front":"CVE vs CWE?","back":"CVE = specific known vulnerability id; CWE = category of weakness."},{"id":"sca-7","topicId":"sca-security","front":"How to prioritise findings?","back":"Severity, exposure, business impact, availability of a fix.","hook":"SEBF"},{"id":"sca-8","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Is a scanner finding a vulnerability?","back":"No: it is evidence; verify, check reachability, assess consequence, then decide."},{"id":"sca-9","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Safe remediation workflow?","back":"Branch, bump version, test, PR review, CI scans, staged deploy with rollback, verify."},{"id":"sca-10","topicId":"sca-security","front":"Name SCA tools.","back":"Dependabot, Trivy, OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk, npm audit."},{"id":"sd-1","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"SAST in one line?","back":"Analyses source/bytecode without running it; early; needs code."},{"id":"sd-2","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"DAST in one line?","back":"Attacks the running application from outside; later; no source needed."},{"id":"sd-3","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"SAST and DAST tool examples?","back":"SAST: SonarQube, Semgrep, CodeQL. DAST: OWASP ZAP, Burp."},{"id":"sd-4","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"Why add SAST/DAST to CI/CD?","back":"Find vulnerabilities early and automatically, cheaper fixes, consistent evidence."},{"id":"sd-5","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"ZAP passive vs active scan?","back":"Passive observes traffic only; active sends attack-like requests (authorised targets only)."},{"id":"sd-6","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"ZAP parts?","back":"History, Sites tree, passive scan, active scan."},{"id":"sd-7","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"Why does ZAP produce an empty report?","back":"App not running/reachable, endpoints not discovered, auth missing, report not saved or uploaded."},{"id":"sd-8","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"Speed up SonarQube without lowering quality?","back":"Analyse only changed code on PRs, full scan nightly/on merge, caching, exclude generated code, parallelise."},{"id":"sd-9","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"Trade-off of incremental analysis?","back":"Legacy issues are not rechecked until a full scan."},{"id":"sd-10","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","front":"What can DAST not see?","back":"Unreachable paths, logic flaws, code never exercised by the scanner."},{"id":"sf-1","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Vulnerability (RFC 2828)?","back":"A flaw or weakness in design, implementation or operation that could be exploited to violate the security policy."},{"id":"sf-2","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Countermeasure?","back":"Action, device, procedure or technique that reduces a threat, vulnerability or attack."},{"id":"sf-3","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Zero-day vulnerability?","back":"A flaw unknown to the vendor with no patch, so zero days to fix before exploitation."},{"id":"sf-4","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Zero-day life cycle?","back":"Introduced, discovered, exploited, disclosed, patched.","hook":"IDEDP"},{"id":"sf-5","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Buffer overflow?","back":"Writing more data than a buffer holds, corrupting adjacent memory such as the return address."},{"id":"sf-6","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Two buffer overflow countermeasures?","back":"Bounds-checked functions/validation; stack canaries, ASLR, DEP/NX (or memory-safe languages)."},{"id":"sf-7","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"STRIDE?","back":"Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege."},{"id":"sf-8","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"CIA triad?","back":"Confidentiality, integrity, availability."},{"id":"sf-9","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"SQL injection fix?","back":"Parameterised queries (PreparedStatement), not string concatenation."},{"id":"sf-10","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"What is XSS?","back":"Attacker input returned to a browser and executed as script; fix with output encoding and CSP."},{"id":"sf-11","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"What is CSRF?","back":"Tricking a logged-in browser into sending an unwanted state-changing request; fix with tokens/SameSite."},{"id":"sf-12","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Threat vs vulnerability vs exploit?","back":"Threat = potential harm; vulnerability = weakness; exploit = code that uses the weakness."},{"id":"sf-13","topicId":"security-fundamentals","front":"Why is strcpy unsafe?","back":"It copies with no length check, so long input overflows the destination."},{"id":"ds-1","topicId":"devsecops","front":"What is shift-left security?","back":"Moving security checks earlier in the lifecycle to catch vulnerabilities before deployment."},{"id":"ds-2","topicId":"devsecops","front":"DevSecOps benefits?","back":"Faster cheaper fixes, continuous security, better compliance, shared responsibility."},{"id":"ds-3","topicId":"devsecops","front":"DevSecOps best practices?","back":"Security as code, automated testing, continuous monitoring, IaC, training."},{"id":"dp-1","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"What is refactoring?","back":"Restructuring code without changing external behaviour."},{"id":"dp-2","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Why does refactoring improve reusability?","back":"Removes duplication, extracts abstractions, lowers coupling, splits responsibilities."},{"id":"dp-3","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Open/Closed Principle?","back":"Open for extension, closed for modification."},{"id":"dp-4","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Factory pattern?","back":"Centralises object creation behind a method so callers do not use new on concrete classes."},{"id":"dp-5","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Remove if(role) chains using?","back":"Polymorphism with an abstract User and subclasses."},{"id":"dp-6","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Pattern for many payment methods?","back":"Strategy."},{"id":"dp-7","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Pattern for email/SMS/push notifications?","back":"Observer (or Strategy/Adapter behind an interface)."},{"id":"dp-8","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"How can a factory obey OCP?","back":"Use a registry map of creators so new roles register without editing createUser."},{"id":"dp-9","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Style vs pattern?","back":"Architectural style organises a whole system; design pattern solves a local design problem."},{"id":"dp-10","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","front":"Cohesion and coupling goal?","back":"High cohesion, loose coupling."},{"id":"ad-1","topicId":"agile-vs-devops-sre","front":"Agile vs DevOps focus?","back":"Agile: iterative development and feedback. DevOps: automated delivery and dev-ops collaboration."},{"id":"ad-2","topicId":"agile-vs-devops-sre","front":"DevOps core practices?","back":"CI/CD, Infrastructure as Code, monitoring and logging, collaboration."},{"id":"ad-3","topicId":"agile-vs-devops-sre","front":"SRE vs DevOps?","back":"SRE adds SLOs, error budgets and toil reduction; DevOps is the broader collaboration culture."},{"id":"ar-1","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"Monolith benefits?","back":"Simple development, easy deployment, easy debugging."},{"id":"ar-2","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"Monolith challenges?","back":"Hard to scale parts, maintenance, tight coupling, slow risky deployment."},{"id":"ar-3","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"Microservices benefits?","back":"Independent scaling, technology flexibility, fault isolation."},{"id":"ar-4","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"ADR components?","back":"Title, Context, Decision, Status, Consequences."},{"id":"ar-5","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"Four architecture views?","back":"Logical, process, physical, development."},{"id":"ar-6","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","front":"What is cohesion?","back":"How well-defined a class purpose is; single responsibility."},{"id":"sl-1","topicId":"serverless-faas","front":"What is FaaS?","back":"Deploy small stateless functions triggered by events; provider manages servers."},{"id":"sl-2","topicId":"serverless-faas","front":"Serverless principles?","back":"Event-driven, automatic scaling, no server management, on-demand billing."},{"id":"sl-3","topicId":"serverless-faas","front":"What is a cold start?","back":"Delay when a function needs a fresh execution environment."},{"id":"sl-4","topicId":"serverless-faas","front":"Cold start mitigations?","back":"Light runtime, small package, provisioned concurrency, warm-up invocations."},{"id":"sw-1","topicId":"sdlc-software-basics","front":"Textbook definition of software?","back":"Instructions, data structures, and descriptive documentation."},{"id":"sw-2","topicId":"sdlc-software-basics","front":"Why does software not wear out?","back":"It is logical, with no physical material to degrade."},{"id":"sw-3","topicId":"sdlc-software-basics","front":"Ariane 5 lesson?","back":"64-bit value forced into 16 bits caused overflow; reused software must be re-validated."},{"id":"sw-4","topicId":"sdlc-software-basics","front":"What is a framework?","back":"Generic reusable code that user code can override or specialise."},{"id":"lg-1","topicId":"legacy-topics","front":"Surjective function in Z?","back":"Every element of the codomain is the image of some element: ran f = codomain."},{"id":"lg-2","topicId":"legacy-topics","front":"Z: Delta vs Xi schema?","back":"Delta S: state changes; Xi S: state unchanged."},{"id":"lg-3","topicId":"legacy-topics","front":"Type 1 vs type 2 hypervisor?","back":"Type 1 runs on bare metal; type 2 runs on a host OS (VirtualBox)."},{"id":"lg-4","topicId":"legacy-topics","front":"CAP theorem?","back":"Cannot have all of consistency, availability and partition tolerance in a distributed store."},{"id":"lg-5","topicId":"legacy-topics","front":"Reflected vs persistent XSS?","back":"Reflected: payload in request echoed back. Persistent: payload stored and served to others."},{"id":"bb-1","topicId":"build-basics","front":"What does a build tool automate?","back":"Compiling, dependency resolution, testing and packaging in a repeatable way."},{"id":"bb-2","topicId":"build-basics","front":"Maven vs Gradle?","back":"Maven: XML pom, fixed lifecycle. Gradle: Groovy/Kotlin script, flexible tasks."},{"id":"df-s1","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Does a successful docker build prove the app works?","back":"No. It only proves Docker could execute the instructions. Run, ps, logs, port mapping, HTTP request to verify."},{"id":"df-s2","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"What does the final \".\" in docker build -t my-app . mean?","back":"Use the current directory as the build context."},{"id":"df-s3","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"COPY target/app.jar app.jar: what are the two sides?","back":"Left = path inside the build context (host); right = location inside the image (relative to WORKDIR)."},{"id":"df-s4","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why can COPY fail even with correct syntax?","back":"The source is not inside the build context (built from the wrong directory, JAR not built, or excluded by .dockerignore)."},{"id":"df-s5","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Is .dockerignore a substitute for secret management?","back":"No. It reduces context size and the chance of leaking .env, but secrets must be supplied at runtime."},{"id":"df-s6","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Which question does each Dockerfile instruction answer?","back":"FROM: which runtime? WORKDIR: where does the app live? COPY: which artefact? EXPOSE: which internal port? ENTRYPOINT: which process starts?","hook":"F-W-C-E-E"},{"id":"df-s7","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"RUN versus ENTRYPOINT/CMD?","back":"RUN executes at image build time; ENTRYPOINT/CMD define what runs when the container starts."},{"id":"df-s8","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Option A vs Option B for building the JAR?","back":"A: build outside Docker (host/CI) then COPY the JAR. B: multi-stage build inside Docker, copy only the JAR to a runtime stage."},{"id":"df-s9","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Is -DskipTests a universal Docker rule?","back":"No, it is a pipeline decision. Ask: where in our delivery process is correctness verified?"},{"id":"df-s10","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Layer-cache rule for Dockerfile order?","back":"Put relatively stable inputs (pom.xml, dependencies) before frequently changing ones (src). COPY . . before RUN mvn package invalidates the cache."},{"id":"df-s11","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Dockerfile workflow in five words?","back":"Inspect, derive, build, run, verify."},{"id":"df-s12","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Typical startup evidence for Spring Boot / Node / Flask?","back":"Spring Boot: pom.xml, JAR, java -jar. Node: package.json, npm start or node. Flask: requirements.txt, python or app server command."},{"id":"df-s13","topicId":"dockerfile","front":"Why is the base-image tag an engineering dependency?","back":"It defines the execution environment (runtime version). Do not copy a FROM line from Stack Overflow or use :latest."},{"id":"dt-s1","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Name the six categories a Docker problem falls into.","back":"BUILD, IMAGE, STARTUP, CONFIGURATION, NETWORK/PORT MAPPING, APPLICATION. Classify before rewriting source.","hook":"B-I-S-C-N-A"},{"id":"dt-s2","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Container exits immediately: first commands and causes?","back":"docker ps -a, docker logs. Causes: wrong startup command, missing artefact, incompatible runtime, missing configuration, app startup failure."},{"id":"dt-s3","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"Container running but app unreachable: what to check?","back":"Internal listening port, -p HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT mapping, application logs, whether the service is actually listening."},{"id":"dt-s4","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","front":"The verification chain after docker run?","back":"Image exists, container remains running (docker ps), docker logs, port mapping, HTTP request, application responds."},{"id":"jc-s1","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Three ideas of JaCoCo in the lecture?","back":"Instrumentation (bytecode tracks execution), reporting (instruction, line, branch, complexity), interpretation (high % does not prove requirement checked).","hook":"I-R-I"},{"id":"jc-s2","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Four questions to ask about a coverage gap?","back":"Which requirement/failure mode? Would a miss break important business behaviour? Already tested elsewhere? Missing test, maintainability issue, or not worth action?"},{"id":"jc-s3","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Missing test vs code smell vs technical debt?","back":"Missing test: uncovered documented rule. Smell: duplication, long method, coupling. Technical debt: design trade-off with ongoing cost."},{"id":"jc-s4","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Why can instruction coverage rise while branch coverage stays the same?","back":"Added tests may run more instructions but not the missed branch outcomes (slide: 86% to 96% instruction, branch still 90%, 2 of 20 missed)."},{"id":"jc-s5","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"Exam Registry score rule used in the JaCoCo practical?","back":"Valid scores are the inclusive range 0-100; outside it must be rejected."},{"id":"jc-s6","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","front":"JaCoCo versions in the practicals?","back":"Exam Registry: JUnit Jupiter 5, JaCoCo 0.8.11. PetClinic: JUnit 4-style tests, JaCoCo 0.8.2 (./mvnw --batch-mode clean verify)."},{"id":"sre-s1","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"What is \"engineering bankruptcy\"?","back":"Toil takes an unmanageable share of capacity, leaving none to reduce toil or improve the business."},{"id":"sre-s2","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","front":"Slide example: 720 h month, 1.44 h downtime, SLO 99.9%?","back":"SLI = 99.8%. Allowed downtime 0.72 h (43.2 min). 1.44 h exceeds it, so the SLO is not met."},{"id":"ut-s1","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"What is a domain primitive (EmailAddress)?","back":"A value object for an important domain concept that validates itself on construction, so invalid values cannot exist."},{"id":"ut-s2","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","front":"EmailAddress boundary lengths in the slides?","back":"Accept exactly 15 and exactly 77 chars; reject 14 chars, 65-char local part, multiple @, wrong domain, subdomain, leading/trailing dot, more than one dot."},{"id":"rq-s1","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Name the two lanes from request to engineering evidence.","back":"Requirements maturity: raw request, clarified requirement, ready for engineering. Evidence: Issue, Pull Request, implementation, test, evidence (history, checks, traceability)."},{"id":"rq-s2","topicId":"req-change-management","front":"Can an urgent change be not ready?","back":"Yes. Priority (urgency) and readiness are different concerns."}],"methods":[{"id":"write-good-requirements","topicId":"req-attributes","title":"Write or critique requirements using the attributes","when":"Asked for attributes of good requirements, to improve a vague requirement, or to write requirements for a scenario","mnemonic":"U-C-N-V-F-C-T and WHAT not HOW","steps":["1. State assumptions first (2-3 bullets) if the scenario is vague.","2. List 6+ attributes, each with a one-line meaning (unambiguous, complete, necessary, verifiable, feasible, consistent, traceable, solution-neutral).","3. Identify the stakeholders affected.","4. Split FR and NFR; make every NFR measurable (number + condition).","5. Rewrite each vague line as \"The system shall ...\" or a user story with conditions of satisfaction.","6. Check each rewritten line against the attributes: who tests it, how, is it traceable to a stakeholder need?"],"example":{"problem":"Veterinary clinic: \"The booking system should be fast and user friendly and remember pets.\" Improve it.","solution":["Assumptions: reception staff are the main users; peak load is 20 concurrent users; pet records already exist.","Problems: \"fast\" and \"user friendly\" are ambiguous and unverifiable; \"remember pets\" is incomplete and mixes several needs.","FR-1: A receptionist shall be able to book an appointment by selecting an existing pet, a vet and a free 15-minute slot.","FR-2: The system shall prevent two appointments for the same vet and slot (consistent, verifiable by test).","NFR-1: 95% of booking requests shall complete within 2 seconds with 20 concurrent users (measurable).","NFR-2: A new receptionist shall complete a booking in under 3 minutes after 10 minutes of training (usability, testable).","Story: As a receptionist I want to book a visit for a pet so that owners get a confirmed time. Conditions: 1.1 slot shown as taken afterwards; 1.2 owner gets an SMS (D)."]}},{"id":"agile-scenario-answer","topicId":"agile-manifesto","title":"Answer an Agile Manifesto / principles scenario question","when":"Q1a: describe the Manifesto, choose a principle to solve a scenario problem, propose solutions or steps","mnemonic":"Values, Principle, Apply, Evidence (VPAE)","steps":["1. One sentence: what Agile and the Manifesto are (iterative, customer feedback, adapt).","2. State the 4 values (X over Y) if asked to describe the manifesto.","3. Pick the principle that matches the problem (changing requirements = 2; collaboration = 4; frequent value = 1 or 3; quality = 9; team = 5 or 11; improvement = 12).","4. Quote it in your own words in one line.","5. Give concrete actions in the scenario: backlog, sprint length, demos, tests, CI, stand-ups, product owner.","6. Close with the outcome (what improves)."],"example":{"problem":"A veterinary clinic extends its software with online booking. Owners and vets keep changing what they want, causing rework. Use ONE principle and give TWO solutions (6 marks).","solution":["Principle 2: welcome changing requirements, even late in development; Agile harnesses change for the customer's advantage.","Solution 1: keep a prioritised product backlog owned by the head vet (product owner); re-order it at every sprint planning so new requests are slotted into the next 2-week sprint rather than disrupting the current one.","Solution 2: end each sprint with a demo of working software (e.g. the booking form) to vets and reception, collecting feedback that becomes new backlog items; combine with automated tests and CI so changes are cheap and safe.","Outcome: change is absorbed in small increments, wrong assumptions are found within 2 weeks, and rework falls."]}},{"id":"change-request-answer","topicId":"req-change-management","title":"Handle a change request (requirements and change management answer)","when":"Q1: a customer asks for a change; describe how you capture, assess, approve and implement it, stating assumptions","mnemonic":"A-S-C-I-D-I-V-T: Assume, Stakeholders, Clarify, Impact, Decide, Implement, Verify, Trace","steps":["1. Assumptions: who approves, existing system, constraints.","2. Stakeholders and their concerns.","3. Clarify: restate the request as a requirement, user story, acceptance criteria and open questions; check attributes of good requirements.","4. Impact analysis: affected requirements, components, tests, effort, risk, compatibility (additive or breaking).","5. Decision by the change authority; record priority and readiness separately; record rationale.","6. Implement via an issue, feature branch and pull request with review.","7. Verify with tests (including regression); update docs.","8. Trace: link issue, PR, tests and release; keep history."],"example":{"problem":"The vet clinic asks: \"staff must see only one pet's visits instead of the whole list\" (change request CR-02). Describe how you manage it.","solution":["Assumptions: the head vet is the change authority; the current visit list endpoint must keep working; pet ids are numeric.","Stakeholders: reception (daily use), vets (history), owners (indirect), developers.","Requirement: callers can retrieve visits for a specified pet while full-list behaviour remains. Criteria: petId given returns only that pet's visits; no petId returns the full list; unknown pet returns 404 (open question to confirm).","Impact: one controller parameter, service method, repository query, 4 tests, API docs; additive and non-breaking; estimate 1 day, low risk.","Decision: approved, priority High, status Ready once the unknown-pet question is answered (urgent is not the same as ready).","Implement: GitHub issue, branch feature/visits-by-pet, PR with review. Verify: unit and API tests, CI green. Trace: issue linked to PR and tests; release notes updated."]}},{"id":"git-command-sequence","topicId":"git-commands","title":"Write the Git commands for a task and explain each","when":"Asked for the sequence of git commands (e.g. commit a feature, merge it) with reasoning","mnemonic":"SACP-M: Status, Add, Commit, Push, then Merge","steps":["1. Check where you are: `git status` (and `git branch`).","2. Stage: `git add <files>` or `git add .`.","3. Commit with a quoted message: `git commit -m \"...\"`.","4. Integrate others' work: `git pull` (or `git pull --rebase`) and fix conflicts.","5. Publish: `git push origin <branch>` (`-u` the first time).","6. To merge: `git checkout main`, `git pull`, `git merge <feature>`, `git push`.","7. After each command add a one-line reason (marks are for the reasoning)."],"example":{"problem":"Write the commands to: check status, add all, commit \"Implemented new feature\", push to remote feature-branch, merge feature-branch into main.","solution":["`git status`: see changed/untracked files and current branch.","`git add .`: stage all changes for the commit.","`git commit -m \"Implemented new feature\"`: save the staged snapshot to the local repository.","`git push origin feature-branch`: upload the commits to the remote feature branch.","`git checkout main`: switch to the branch that receives the merge.","`git pull origin main`: make sure main is up to date.","`git merge feature-branch`: bring the feature commits into main.","`git push origin main`: publish the merged main."]}},{"id":"git-diagnose-command","topicId":"git-commands","title":"Given a command or error: identify the problem and fix it","when":"\"Given this command / output, identify the problem and how to solve it\"","mnemonic":"READ-NAME-FIX: read the message, name the cause, give the exact fix and prevention","steps":["1. Read the exact command and output; note branch, remote, flags and quoting.","2. Name the cause in one sentence (wrong branch, nothing staged, remote ahead, no upstream, conflict, quoting).","3. Give the exact corrective commands in order.","4. Say how to verify (`git status`, `git log --oneline`).","5. Add prevention (pull before push, short branches, branch protection)."],"example":{"problem":"A developer on branch feature-x runs `git merge feature-x` then `git push` and the push fails with \"rejected: non-fast-forward, fetch first\". The feature never reached main. Explain and fix.","solution":["Problem 1: `git merge feature-x` while ON feature-x merges the branch into itself (\"Already up to date\"), so main is untouched. Merge goes INTO the current branch.","Problem 2: the push is rejected because the remote branch has commits the local branch lacks.","Fix: `git pull --rebase origin feature-x` (resolve conflicts if any), `git push origin feature-x`.","Then: `git checkout main`, `git pull origin main`, `git merge feature-x`, `git push origin main` (or open a pull request).","Verify: `git log --oneline --graph` shows the feature commits on main.","Do not use `git push --force`: it can erase teammates' commits."]}},{"id":"resolve-merge-conflict","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","title":"Resolve a merge conflict step by step","when":"Merge issue and how to resolve; given conflict markers in a file","mnemonic":"PEACE: Pull, Edit markers, Add, Commit, Examine","steps":["1. Run the merge/pull; read \"CONFLICT (content)\" and `git status` (both modified).","2. Open each conflicted file; understand both sides (HEAD = yours, below ======= = incoming).","3. Decide the correct final code (ours, theirs or combined); delete all markers.","4. Compile and run tests.","5. `git add <file>` for each resolved file.","6. `git commit` to complete the merge, then `git push`.","7. Abort option: `git merge --abort`."],"example":{"problem":"Two developers edited Pen.java. The file contains: <<<<<<< HEAD / private float length; / ======= / private short length; / >>>>>>> feature/pen-size. Resolve it.","solution":["Both sides change the type of `length`: a semantic (type 2) conflict.","Ask which the requirements need: pen lengths are decimal (5.5 cm), so choose `float` (keep HEAD); `short` would truncate.","Edit the file to contain only `private float length;` and update any method from the other branch (e.g. `setLen(float l)`).","Run `mvn test`; fix compile errors from the other branch's `short` usage.","Commands: `git add Pen.java`, `git commit -m \"Resolve length type conflict (float)\"`, `git push origin feature/pen-size`.","Prevention: agree data types in the issue/PR before editing shared classes; pull main daily."]}},{"id":"write-pom","topicId":"pom-xml","title":"Write a pom.xml from a description","when":"Asked to write or complete a pom for a Java project with given dependencies/plugins","mnemonic":"M-G-A-V-P-D-B","steps":["1. `<project>` with modelVersion 4.0.0.","2. Coordinates: groupId, artifactId, version, packaging.","3. Properties: Java version (maven.compiler.source/target or java.version) and source encoding.","4. Dependencies: each with GAV and scope (test for JUnit/Mockito).","5. Build plugins: surefire for tests, jacoco (prepare-agent + report), plus packaging plugin if asked.","6. State the commands: `mvn clean package`, `mvn test`, the output path in target/."],"example":{"problem":"Write a pom for vet-clinic 1.0.0 (jar, group ie.clinic), Java 17, JUnit 5 and Mockito for tests, JaCoCo report.","solution":["`<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>`","`<groupId>ie.clinic</groupId> <artifactId>vet-clinic</artifactId> <version>1.0.0</version> <packaging>jar</packaging>`","`<properties><maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source><maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target></properties>`","`<dependency>` junit-jupiter 5.10.2, `<scope>test</scope>`","`<dependency>` mockito-core 5.11.0, `<scope>test</scope>`","`<plugin>` jacoco-maven-plugin 0.8.11 with executions `prepare-agent` and `report` (phase test).","Run: `mvn clean test` builds, runs tests, writes target/site/jacoco/index.html; `mvn package` writes target/vet-clinic-1.0.0.jar.","Full file is in the \"pom.xml anatomy\" topic."]}},{"id":"write-spring-boot-pom","topicId":"spring-boot","title":"Write a Spring Boot pom and explain the project structure","when":"Q2 Spring Boot version of the pom question","mnemonic":"Parent, Starters, Plugin","steps":["1. `<parent>` spring-boot-starter-parent with a version (no relativePath issues: `<relativePath/>`).","2. Your own groupId/artifactId/version.","3. `<properties><java.version>17</java.version>`.","4. Starters without versions: web, data-jpa, actuator, test (scope test).","5. `spring-boot-maven-plugin` in build.","6. Explain layout: main class with @SpringBootApplication in the root package; controller, service, repository, model packages; application.properties.","7. Run with `mvn spring-boot:run` or `java -jar target/*.jar`."],"example":{"problem":"Create the pom and explain the structure for a vet-api REST service using JPA and monitoring.","solution":["`<parent>` org.springframework.boot : spring-boot-starter-parent : 3.2.5 (supplies versions and plugin defaults).","Dependencies: spring-boot-starter-web (REST + Tomcat), spring-boot-starter-data-jpa (database), spring-boot-starter-actuator (health/metrics), spring-boot-starter-test (test scope).","Plugin: spring-boot-maven-plugin builds an executable JAR.","Structure: VetApiApplication (@SpringBootApplication) in ie.clinic; VisitController (@RestController) calls VisitService (@Service) calls VisitRepository (extends JpaRepository); Visit is an @Entity.","Config: server.port=8080 and management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info in application.properties.","Run: `mvn spring-boot:run`; Dockerfile later copies target/vet-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar."]}},{"id":"write-dockerfile","topicId":"dockerfile","title":"Construct and write a Dockerfile (steps and instructions)","when":"Q3: write a Dockerfile for a given application, or describe the steps to construct one","mnemonic":"F-W-C-R-E-E: FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN, EXPOSE, ENTRYPOINT","steps":["1. Inspect: what build system (pom.xml / package.json) and what artefact does it produce?","2. Pick the runtime base image matching the version (Java 17 gives eclipse-temurin:17-jre); avoid latest.","3. FROM then WORKDIR /app.","4. COPY the artefact (or, for multi-stage, copy pom.xml, resolve dependencies, copy src, RUN build).","5. EXPOSE the application port; read config from environment variables, no secrets in ENV.","6. ENTRYPOINT in exec form with the start command.","7. Add .dockerignore; then `docker build -t name .` and verify with run, ps, logs, curl.","8. Explain each line in one phrase."],"example":{"problem":"Write a Dockerfile for the Spring Boot vet-api (Java 17, Maven, listens on 8080) that builds inside Docker, and give the build/run commands.","solution":["`FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build`: stage 1 image with JDK and Maven to build.","`WORKDIR /workspace`: build directory in the image.","`COPY pom.xml .` then `RUN mvn dependency:go-offline`: cache dependencies in their own layer.","`COPY src ./src` then `RUN mvn clean package -DskipTests`: compile and package the JAR (tests run in CI).","`FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre`: stage 2 small runtime image, JRE only.","`WORKDIR /app` and `COPY --from=build /workspace/target/vet-api.jar app.jar`: take just the JAR.","`EXPOSE 8080`: document the port.","`ENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]`: command run at container start.","Commands: `docker build -t vet-api .`, `docker run -d --name vet-api -p 8080:8080 -e DB_PASSWORD=\"$DB_PASSWORD\" vet-api`, `docker ps`, `docker logs vet-api`, `curl http://localhost:8080/actuator/health`.",".dockerignore: target/, .git, .idea, *.log, .env."]}},{"id":"docker-troubleshoot","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","title":"Troubleshoot a Docker build/run/deploy failure","when":"Part 2 of the Docker question: a container does not start/run, or a pull/push fails; give commands and fix","mnemonic":"PLIE: ps -a, logs, inspect, exec","steps":["1. Classify: build time, start time, network/port, registry (pull/push).","2. `docker ps -a`: is it running, or Exited with which code?","3. `docker logs <name>` (add `--tail 50`): read the actual error.","4. `docker inspect <name>` for ports, env, mounts, OOMKilled; `docker exec -it <name> /bin/sh` to look inside.","5. Check the port mapping HOST:CONTAINER and that the app binds to 0.0.0.0.","6. For registry errors check tag exists (`docker manifest inspect`), login and permissions.","7. Fix the root cause (Dockerfile, env, command, tag), rebuild/redeploy, verify with ps, logs and curl."],"example":{"problem":"Deployment log: \"docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:latest: manifest unknown\". The build job pushed only the commit SHA tag. Diagnose, identify the cause and redeploy.","solution":["Diagnose: read the workflow: build pushes `$IMAGE:${{ github.sha }}` only; deploy pulls `:latest`.","Confirm: `docker manifest inspect ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:<sha>` works; `docker manifest inspect ...:latest` fails; `git log -1` shows the SHA built.","Cause: the `latest` tag was never pushed, so the registry has no such manifest. The build passes because pushing the SHA tag succeeds; deploy fails because it asks for a different tag.","Rectify: change the build to `docker build -t $IMAGE:$SHA -t $IMAGE:latest .` and push both tags (commit with `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`).","Redeploy on staging: `docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/payment-api:latest`, `docker compose up -d payment-api`, `docker ps`, `docker logs payment-api --tail 50`, `curl -f http://localhost:3000/health`.","Prevent: deploy the immutable SHA tag, keep `latest` as a convenience only."]}},{"id":"explain-workflow-yaml","topicId":"github-actions","title":"Explain a GitHub Actions workflow (what does this job do?)","when":"Given a YAML, explain what it does (e.g. 7 marks) and spot problems","mnemonic":"T-J-S-P: Trigger, Job/runner, Steps in order, Problems","steps":["1. Trigger (`on`): which events and branches.","2. Jobs: names, `runs-on`, `needs` (order), `if` conditions.","3. Walk each step in order: `uses` (what action) / `run` (what command does) and the result.","4. State what the whole job achieves end to end (build, test, image, publish).","5. Point out risks: no docker login, tag mismatch, latest only, no PR tests, old versions, duplicate test runs, no cache or scan.","6. Offer one improvement for each risk."],"example":{"problem":"Explain: on push to main; job build on ubuntu-latest: checkout@v2; setup-java@v1 JDK 11; `mvn clean package`; `mvn verify`; `docker build -t my-app .`; `docker push myregistry/my-app:latest`.","solution":["Trigger: every push to main only; pull requests are not tested.","One job `build` on a fresh Ubuntu VM.","Checkout: fetches the code. Setup JDK 11: installs Java for Maven.","`mvn clean package`: delete target, compile, run unit tests, create the JAR.","`mvn verify`: later lifecycle phases (integration tests, quality checks, JaCoCo report if configured); unit tests run again.","`docker build -t my-app .`: builds the image from the Dockerfile.","`docker push myregistry/my-app:latest`: pushes to the registry.","Problems: no `docker login` so the push fails; local image is named my-app but the pushed name is myregistry/my-app (needs `docker tag` or a matching -t); only the latest tag (no versioning or rollback); no PR trigger; duplicate test run; outdated action versions; no scans or caching."]}},{"id":"write-workflow","topicId":"github-actions","title":"Write a CI/CD workflow YAML (test on PR, build and push on main, deploy)","when":"Design/write a GitHub Actions pipeline for a given stack and requirements","mnemonic":"N-O-J-R-S then needs/if/secrets","steps":["1. `name` and `on`: `pull_request` plus `push: branches: [main]`.","2. Job test: checkout, set up language (with cache), install, test (and scan).","3. Job image: `needs: test`, `if` only on push to main, login with secrets, build with SHA tag (and latest), push.","4. Job deploy-staging: `needs: image`, deploy using the SHA tag, smoke test.","5. Use `secrets.*`, never plain credentials; set `permissions` when using GHCR.","6. Explain each job and key step in one line."],"example":{"problem":"Node.js microservice: run tests on PRs, build and push an image after merge to main, deploy to staging. List and explain the key steps.","solution":["`on: pull_request` and `push: branches: [main]`: test PRs, publish only on main.","Job `test`: `actions/checkout@v4`, `actions/setup-node@v4` (node 20, cache npm), `npm ci`, `npm test`.","Job `image`: `needs: test`, `if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'`; log in to GHCR using a secret; `docker build -t ghcr.io/org/svc:${{ github.sha }} .`; `docker push` the same tag.","Job `deploy-staging`: `needs: image`; ssh or compose deploy pulling that exact SHA tag; run a health-check curl.","Why: PR tests stop bad code merging; image built once and promoted; SHA tag is traceable and supports rollback; staging validates before production.","Full YAML in the \"GitHub Actions\" topic."]}},{"id":"fix-build-push-step","topicId":"github-actions","title":"Rewrite a build-and-push step with SHA and latest tags","when":"Image built with one tag but deploy needs another; version tagging rule","mnemonic":"One build, two -t, two pushes","steps":["1. Identify which tags are pushed and which are pulled.","2. Build once with several `-t` options.","3. Push each tag (or `docker push --all-tags`).","4. Make sure login precedes the push.","5. Recommend deploying the SHA tag; keep latest for convenience."],"example":{"problem":"Rewrite the Build and push image step so that both the commit SHA tag and latest are available.","solution":["`- name: Build and push image`","`  run: |`","`    docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest .`","`    docker push $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }}`","`    docker push $IMAGE:latest`","Explanation: both -t flags tag the same image; each push publishes a manifest, so deploy's pull of :latest succeeds."]}},{"id":"pipeline-risk-improvements","topicId":"cicd-concepts","title":"Identify pipeline risks and propose improvements","when":"Given a flawed pipeline or business problems, propose 4 improvements (pre-merge control, staging control, rollback, versioning)","mnemonic":"P-S-R-V: Pre-merge, Staging, Rollback, Versioning","steps":["1. List each symptom and map it to a missing control.","2. Pre-merge: tests and scans on every PR, required reviews, branch protection.","3. Staging/deploy: staging identical to production, smoke tests, approval gate, canary or blue-green.","4. Rollback: keep previous immutable image, redeploy previous tag, health-check auto rollback, feature flags.","5. Versioning: tag by commit SHA or semantic version, avoid mutable latest only.","6. Tie each improvement to the scenario problem it solves."],"example":{"problem":"Junior pipeline: tests only on main, every commit to main deploys to production, no rollback. Identify risks and propose improvements.","solution":["Risks: bugs found only after merge; broken code reaches customers; downtime with no way back; no review or staging.","Pre-merge control: run unit tests and SAST/SCA on every pull request; protect main with required checks and review.","Staging control: auto-deploy to staging, run smoke/DAST tests, then manual approval (protected environment) for production, using canary rollout.","Rollback: keep the previous versioned image and redeploy it (`docker pull app:<prev-sha>`), or `git revert` and re-run the pipeline; health-check failure triggers automatic rollback.","Versioning rule: tag images with the commit SHA (and semantic version on release); never deploy a bare `latest`."]}},{"id":"write-junit-tests","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","title":"Write JUnit 5 tests for a method","when":"Q4c: write/design unit tests (e.g. Calculator, discount, validator)","mnemonic":"NBEZ: Normal, Boundary, Error, Zero/empty; each as Arrange-Act-Assert","steps":["1. Read the contract: inputs, outputs, rules, exceptions.","2. List cases: normal, boundary (min, max, just outside), invalid (null, negative), empty/zero, special (overflow).","3. One @Test per behaviour with a descriptive name.","4. Arrange the object and inputs; Act by calling once; Assert with the right assertion (delta for doubles, assertThrows for errors).","5. Use @BeforeEach for shared setup; @ParameterizedTest for many inputs.","6. Mock external dependencies; do not mock the unit under test.","7. Say what each test proves in one line."],"example":{"problem":"Write tests for `double discount(int ageYears, double price)` of a vet clinic: under 2 years or over 10 years of age get 20% off; price below 0 throws IllegalArgumentException.","solution":["`@Test void youngPetGetsTwentyPercentOff() { assertEquals(80.0, calc.discount(1, 100.0), 0.001); }` (normal rule)","`@Test void seniorPetGetsTwentyPercentOff() { assertEquals(80.0, calc.discount(11, 100.0), 0.001); }`","`@Test void adultPetPaysFullPrice() { assertEquals(100.0, calc.discount(5, 100.0), 0.001); }`","Boundaries: `discount(2, 100.0)` expects 100.0 (age 2 is not under 2); `discount(10, 100.0)` expects 100.0 (not over 10); `discount(1, ...)` and `discount(11, ...)` already cover the other side.","`@Test void zeroPriceIsZero() { assertEquals(0.0, calc.discount(1, 0.0), 0.001); }`","`@Test void negativePriceThrows() { assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> calc.discount(5, -1.0)); }`","Parameterised alternative: `@ParameterizedTest @CsvSource({\"1,100,80\", \"2,100,100\", \"10,100,100\", \"11,100,80\"})`."]}},{"id":"write-mockito-test","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","title":"Write a unit test with a mocked dependency","when":"The class under test calls a repository, email service or API","mnemonic":"Mock the edges, verify the effects","steps":["1. Identify the dependencies injected into the class (constructor injection).","2. Declare them as @Mock and the class as @InjectMocks with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class).","3. Arrange: stub with `when(...).thenReturn(...)`.","4. Act: call the method.","5. Assert on the returned result (not on the stub).","6. Verify interactions that matter: `verify(mock).method(...)`, `never()`, `times(n)`.","7. Add a rejection test and check state is unchanged."],"example":{"problem":"Test `ReminderService.sendReminders(List<Appointment>)` which uses an EmailSender and a Clock; only appointments within 24 hours are emailed.","solution":["Arrange: `Clock clock = Clock.fixed(Instant.parse(\"2026-01-05T09:00:00Z\"), ZoneOffset.UTC); EmailSender sender = mock(EmailSender.class); ReminderService svc = new ReminderService(sender, clock);`","Data: appointment A at 2026-01-05T12:00 (within 24 h) and B at 2026-01-08T12:00 (outside).","Act: `int sent = svc.sendReminders(List.of(A, B));`","Assert: `assertEquals(1, sent);`","Verify: `verify(sender, times(1)).send(eq(A.ownerEmail()), anyString()); verify(sender, never()).send(eq(B.ownerEmail()), anyString());`","Why: the fixed clock makes the test repeatable; the mock isolates email; assertions check the observable result and the interaction."]}},{"id":"critique-code","topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","title":"Find what is wrong with this code (design critique)","when":"Q4c part: \"given this code, identify the problem in this implementation\" (not syntax), 5 marks","mnemonic":"SNUMOC: Side effects, Null, Unclear names, Mutability, One-liner overload, Coupling","steps":["1. Say in one line what the code is meant to do (intent).","2. Scan SNUMOC: side effects (mutation in getters/streams), null/Optional misuse, naming and magic values, mutable leaks and static state, overloaded one-liners, hard-wired time/IO/new.","3. For each flaw write: PROBLEM, WHY (bug risk, surprise, untestable), FIX.","4. Give a short corrected snippet for the most important flaw.","5. Mention how the fix makes unit testing possible.","6. Aim for 4-5 distinct points; one mark each."],"example":{"problem":"public Optional<Owner> find(String id) { return Optional.of(repo.get(id)); }  /  public List<String> emails() { List<String> out = new ArrayList<>(); owners.stream().filter(o -> o.getEmail() != null).forEach(o -> out.add(o.getEmail().toLowerCase())); lastRun = new Date(); return out; }","solution":["Intent: look up an owner, and list all owner emails in lower case.","Flaw 1 (null/Optional): `Optional.of(...)` throws NPE when `repo.get` returns null, so absence is never expressed. Fix: `Optional.ofNullable(repo.get(id))`.","Flaw 2 (stream side effect): `forEach` mutates an external list; breaks with parallel streams and is not declarative. Fix: `owners.stream().map(Owner::getEmail).filter(Objects::nonNull).map(String::toLowerCase).collect(toList())`.","Flaw 3 (hidden side effect): a method called `emails()` silently updates `lastRun`; calling it to display data changes state. Fix: separate the command or remove the field.","Flaw 4 (untestable): `new Date()` hard-codes the clock so the result cannot be asserted deterministically. Fix: inject a `Clock`.","Flaw 5 (mutability): the returned list type is mutable and `owners` may be exposed; return `List.copyOf` / unmodifiable. Also name `find` does not say what it finds by."]}},{"id":"tdd-worked-cycle","topicId":"tdd","title":"Demonstrate TDD (red, green, refactor) for a requirement","when":"Asked to show or explain TDD for a small feature with code","mnemonic":"RED - GREEN - REFACTOR - next RED","steps":["1. Write the requirement as one concrete example (input and expected output).","2. RED: write the test; add a stub so it compiles; run and show it fails on the assertion.","3. GREEN: write the least code that passes; run; all tests green.","4. REFACTOR: tidy names/duplication without changing behaviour; tests stay green.","5. Repeat for the next behaviour (edge case, error case).","6. Finish by listing benefits (design, regression safety, living documentation)."],"example":{"problem":"Requirement: DoseCalculator returns 10 mg per kg of body weight for a dog; weight must be positive.","solution":["RED 1: `assertEquals(150.0, new DoseCalculator().dose(15.0), 0.001);` with stub `double dose(double kg) { return -1; }` fails: expected 150.0 but was -1.0.","GREEN 1: `double dose(double kg) { return kg * 10; }` passes.","REFACTOR: extract `private static final double MG_PER_KG = 10;` and use it; run tests: still green.","RED 2: `assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> calc.dose(0));` fails because 0 returns 0.","GREEN 2: add `if (kg <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"weight must be positive\");` passes.","REFACTOR: nothing needed. Result: two tests document the rules and protect future changes."]}},{"id":"coverage-calculation","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","title":"Calculate line and branch coverage for a method and tests","when":"Given code and a test set, compute line/branch coverage or choose tests for 100%","mnemonic":"Count lines run / executable lines; count outcomes taken / (2 x decisions)","steps":["1. List executable lines (exclude braces, comments, method header unless told).","2. List decisions (if, loop condition, case); branches = 2 per decision.","3. For each test, trace which lines and which branch outcomes run.","4. Union across tests.","5. Line coverage = executed lines / executable lines; branch coverage = outcomes taken / total outcomes.","6. To reach 100% pick inputs that take each outcome (true and false)."],"example":{"problem":"int classify(int age): lines: L2 if (age < 0), L3 throw, L5 if (age < 18), L6 return 0, L8 return 1. Tests: classify(10) only. Then add tests to reach 100%.","solution":["Executable lines 5 (L2, L3, L5, L6, L8); decisions 2 so 4 branches.","classify(10): runs L2, L5, L6 = 3/5 = 60% lines; branches: L2 false, L5 true = 2/4 = 50%.","Add classify(-1): runs L2, L3 (union L2, L3, L5, L6 = 4/5 = 80%); branch L2 true added = 3/4 = 75%.","Add classify(30): runs L8 (all 5 lines = 100%); branch L5 false added = 4/4 = 100%.","Three tests (-1, 10, 30) give 100% line and 100% branch coverage. Note 100% does not prove the assertions are right."]}},{"id":"coverage-strategy-answer","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","title":"Explain how to find and raise low coverage (JaCoCo)","when":"\"Explain how you would use a coverage tool to find low-coverage areas and propose a strategy to increase coverage without slowing the pipeline\"","mnemonic":"Measure, Locate, Judge, Test, Gate","steps":["1. Measure: add JaCoCo (prepare-agent + report), run `mvn clean test`, open target/site/jacoco/index.html (or Istanbul/nyc, Coverage.py).","2. Locate: drill from summary to package, class, method, source; look for red lines and yellow branches in the critical module.","3. Judge: for each gap ask which requirement/risk it represents and whether it is already covered.","4. Test: write fast isolated unit tests (mocks, parameterised tests) for important uncovered branches and boundaries.","5. Gate: `jacoco:check` rule on the critical package; run in CI on PRs; upload the report.","6. Keep the pipeline fast: unit tests only on PR; slow integration tests nightly; parallel execution; dependency cache."],"example":{"problem":"The payment module has low coverage. Explain how you would find and fix it (5 marks).","solution":["Run the build with the coverage agent; open the report and sort the payment package by missed branches.","Find e.g. `refund()` with red lines and `applyDiscount()` with half-covered branches.","Decide which are business-critical (refund limits, rounding, currency).","Add parameterised unit tests for each missed branch and boundary, mocking the gateway so they run in milliseconds.","Add a JaCoCo check requiring 80% branch coverage on the payment package so it cannot regress; heavier gateway integration tests run nightly so PR feedback stays fast."]}},{"id":"calc-availability-sli","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"Calculate an availability SLI and check it against the SLO","when":"Given total time and downtime (or incident list) and an SLO: compute SLI and say if met","mnemonic":"Same units, subtract, divide, x100, compare","steps":["1. Convert the window to one unit (30 days = 720 h = 43,200 min).","2. Sum all downtime in that unit.","3. SLI % = (total - downtime) / total x 100.","4. Compare SLI with the SLO: met if SLI >= SLO.","5. State the verdict and the margin, then the action."],"example":{"problem":"Vet booking API: 30-day month, incidents of 20 min and 30 min. SLO 99.9%. Is the SLO met?","solution":["Total = 30 x 24 x 60 = 43,200 min. Downtime = 20 + 30 = 50 min.","SLI = (43,200 - 50) / 43,200 x 100 = 43,150 / 43,200 x 100 = 99.884%.","99.884% < 99.9%: SLO NOT met.","Margin: allowed 43.2 min, actual 50 min, overspent by 6.8 min (115.7% of budget).","Action: freeze risky releases and prioritise reliability work."]}},{"id":"calc-error-budget","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"Calculate the error budget and allowed downtime","when":"Given an SLO and window: allowed downtime, budget used and remaining","mnemonic":"(100 - SLO)% x window; used / allowed","steps":["1. Error budget % = 100 - SLO.","2. Convert the window to minutes.","3. Allowed downtime = budget fraction x window minutes.","4. Used % = actual downtime / allowed x 100.","5. Remaining = allowed - actual.","6. Decide: if remaining is positive keep shipping carefully; if exhausted freeze."],"example":{"problem":"SLO 99.95% over a 90-day quarter. Incidents so far total 45 minutes. Budget, used, remaining?","solution":["Budget % = 0.05%. Window = 90 x 1,440 = 129,600 min.","Allowed downtime = 0.0005 x 129,600 = 64.8 min.","Used = 45 / 64.8 = 69.4%. Remaining = 64.8 - 45 = 19.8 min.","Verdict: SLO still met but 30.6% of budget left; be cautious with risky deployments.","In requests: with 20,000,000 requests the budget is 0.0005 x 20,000,000 = 10,000 failed requests."]}},{"id":"calc-burn-rate","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"Calculate burn rate and time to exhaustion","when":"Partway through the window: how fast is the budget being consumed?","mnemonic":"Burn = % budget used / % window elapsed","steps":["1. Compute the budget for the whole window (minutes).","2. Fraction used = downtime so far / budget.","3. Fraction elapsed = time elapsed / window.","4. Burn rate = fraction used / fraction elapsed (1 = on target, above 1 = too fast).","5. Time to exhaustion = window / burn rate (from the start of the window).","6. Time left = that value minus time elapsed; recommend action."],"example":{"problem":"Weekly window (7 days), SLO 99.9%. After 2 days there has been 8 minutes of downtime.","solution":["Window = 7 x 1,440 = 10,080 min. Budget = 0.001 x 10,080 = 10.08 min.","Fraction used = 8 / 10.08 = 0.794 (79.4%). Fraction elapsed = 2 / 7 = 0.286.","Burn rate = 0.794 / 0.286 = 2.78.","Exhausted after 7 / 2.78 = 2.52 days from the window start, so about 0.52 days (12.5 hours) from now.","Action: stop feature releases, investigate the failure cause, and protect the remaining 2.08 min."]}},{"id":"calc-composite-availability","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"Composite availability of dependent services","when":"A request needs several services (serial) or has redundancy (parallel)","mnemonic":"Serial multiply; parallel 1 - (1 - a)(1 - b)","steps":["1. Convert percentages to decimals.","2. Serial (all needed): multiply availabilities.","3. Parallel (any one works): 1 - product of (1 - a).","4. Convert back to % and to allowed downtime (1 - A) x window.","5. Compare with the SLO you promised."],"example":{"problem":"Checkout needs API 99.95%, database 99.9% and payment gateway 99.8% (all in series). Can you promise 99.9% over 30 days?","solution":["A = 0.9995 x 0.999 x 0.998 = 0.9965 (99.65%).","Expected downtime = (1 - 0.9965) x 43,200 = about 151 min per 30 days.","99.65% < 99.9% so you cannot promise 99.9%: the weakest dependency and the chain limit you.","Improvement: add redundancy to the gateway: two independent 99.8% gateways in parallel give 1 - 0.002^2 = 99.9996%, so A = 0.9995 x 0.999 x 0.999996 = 99.85%, still below 99.9% (the database is the next weak link).","Conclusion: promise about 99.8% or improve the database and API too."]}},{"id":"slo-compliance-report","topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","title":"Compliance check across several services or months","when":"A table of services/months with downtime and SLOs: decide which meet SLO, which breach SLA","mnemonic":"Per row: allowed, actual, verdict","steps":["1. For each row compute allowed downtime from its SLO and window.","2. Compare actual with allowed: met or not.","3. Compute SLI % for each.","4. Identify rows that also breach the SLA (contract) and the penalty.","5. Rank by budget consumed and recommend action per row."],"example":{"problem":"30-day month. Auth: SLO 99.95%, down 18 min. Booking: SLO 99.9%, down 40 min. Notifications: SLO 99%, down 300 min. SLA for all is one level lower than the SLO.","solution":["Auth: allowed 21.6 min; actual 18 -> SLI = (43,200 - 18)/43,200 = 99.958%; met; 83.3% budget used.","Booking: allowed 43.2 min; actual 40 -> SLI = 99.907%; met; 92.6% used (very little left).","Notifications: allowed 432 min; actual 300 -> SLI = 99.306%; met; 69.4% used.","No SLO is breached, hence no SLA breach. Highest risk: Booking at 92.6% budget used: stop risky changes on it; Auth next at 83.3%.","If Booking had been down 60 min: SLI = 99.861%, SLO missed (budget 139% used); SLA at 99.5%? still met, but the error-budget policy freezes releases."]}},{"id":"mttd-mttr-average","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","title":"Compute MTTD, MTTR and averages from incident data","when":"Given start/detect/restore times or a table of MTTD/MTTR and asked for averages and best incident","mnemonic":"Subtract timestamps in minutes, then sum and divide","steps":["1. Convert all times to minutes.","2. MTTD = detected - started; MTTR = restored - detected (use the paper's definition).","3. Total downtime per incident = MTTD + MTTR.","4. Average = sum / number of incidents.","5. Best incident = smallest MTTD, MTTR and total; explain why (automation, monitoring).","6. Suggest 2 improvements."],"example":{"problem":"Incident 1: start 09:00, detected 09:40, restored 10:30. Incident 2: 13:15, 13:20, 13:50. Incident 3: 18:00, 19:30, 21:00. Calculate average MTTD and MTTR.","solution":["Inc 1: MTTD 40, MTTR 50 (total 90). Inc 2: MTTD 5, MTTR 30 (total 35). Inc 3: MTTD 90, MTTR 90 (total 180).","Average MTTD = (40 + 5 + 90) / 3 = 45 min.","Average MTTR = (50 + 30 + 90) / 3 = 56.7 min.","Best: incident 2 (5 min to detect, 35 min total outage).","Improvements: automated alerting with on-call paging to cut MTTD; runbooks and automated rollback to cut MTTR."]}},{"id":"write-openapi-spec","topicId":"api-design-openapi","title":"Design an API and write an OpenAPI fragment","when":"Q4b: design endpoints for a scenario, explain API-first, choose methods and status codes","mnemonic":"Resources (nouns), Methods, Codes, Schema, Security","steps":["1. Identify resources (nouns, plural).","2. Map operations to methods: GET list/read, POST create, PUT/PATCH update, DELETE remove.","3. Define parameters (path for identity, query for filters) and request bodies.","4. List responses with status codes (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409) and schema.","5. Decide versioning (/v1) and whether a change is additive or breaking.","6. Add security: authentication (OAuth2/JWT), rate limiting, validation, HTTPS.","7. Write the OpenAPI YAML skeleton (openapi, info, paths, components)."],"example":{"problem":"Design the API for the clinic to list a pet's visits and to book a new visit without breaking existing clients that call GET /visits.","solution":["`GET /v1/visits` returns all visits (existing behaviour kept).","`GET /v1/visits?petId=8` adds an optional filter: additive change, not breaking. 200 with array; 400 for non-numeric petId; 404 if the pet does not exist.","`POST /v1/visits` with body {petId, date, notes}: 201 Created with the visit and Location header; 400 invalid; 409 if the slot is taken.","`GET /v1/visits/{id}`: 200 or 404.","Security: OAuth2 bearer JWT, rate limit per API key, validate types and ranges, HTTPS only.","API-first: write this as openapi.yaml, publish mocks so the front end builds in parallel, generate docs with Swagger UI, then implement."]}},{"id":"prioritise-vulnerabilities","topicId":"sca-security","title":"Prioritise dependency findings and plan remediation","when":"A scan table (severity, exposure, fix available) and \"which TWO should be fixed, and how?\"","mnemonic":"SEBF then branch-bump-test-review-deploy","steps":["1. For each finding note severity, exposure (public/internal/build-time), business impact, fix availability and effort.","2. Rank: critical or high with public/auth exposure and an available fix first.","3. Justify each chosen and each deferred item in one line.","4. Remediation: branch, bump the dependency, update lockfile, run tests, PR review, CI with scans, staging then production with rollback.","5. Verify the alert closes; record the decision."],"example":{"problem":"Findings: A express 4.17.1 High public API fix yes; B lodash High internal batch fix yes; C React build dep Medium needs major upgrade; D jsonwebtoken 8.5.1 Critical customer sessions fix yes. Which two and how?","solution":["Fix D first: critical, protects customer sessions (account takeover risk), fix available.","Fix A second: high severity on the public internet-facing API, fix available.","Defer B (high but internal; schedule) and C (medium, build-time only, major upgrade effort and regression risk).","Workflow for D: `git checkout -b fix/jsonwebtoken`; `npm install jsonwebtoken@<patched>`; commit package.json and lockfile; run tests incl. token verify/expiry/tamper tests; open a PR; CI runs tests, SAST and SCA; reviewer approves; deploy to staging, smoke test, canary to production with the previous image tag kept for rollback."]}},{"id":"compare-sast-dast","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","title":"Compare SAST and DAST (4-attribute template)","when":"Explain the key differences by when, source access, issue types and tools","mnemonic":"W-S-T-T: When, Source, Types, Tools","steps":["1. When in the lifecycle: SAST early (coding/commit/PR); DAST later (running app in test/staging).","2. Source access: SAST needs source/bytecode (white-box); DAST does not (black-box).","3. Issue types: SAST insecure code patterns, injection flows, secrets; DAST runtime issues: headers, cookies, exposed endpoints, auth/session, some injection/XSS.","4. Tools: SonarQube/Semgrep/CodeQL vs OWASP ZAP/Burp.","5. Limits and why combine (plus SCA)."],"example":{"problem":"Explain the key differences between SAST and DAST (5 marks).","solution":["When: SAST is run during development/CI before deployment; DAST needs a deployed running app so it runs in staging/after build.","Source: SAST analyses source code or bytecode so access is required; DAST tests from outside over HTTP and needs no code.","Detects: SAST finds insecure patterns and data flows (SQL injection source-to-sink, hard-coded secrets); DAST finds runtime and configuration weaknesses (missing headers, insecure cookies, exposed consoles, authentication flaws).","Tools: SonarQube (SAST), OWASP ZAP (DAST).","Together they cover different surfaces; neither proves the app is secure; add SCA for dependencies."]}},{"id":"zap-sonar-troubleshoot","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","title":"Fix a failing ZAP step or a slow SonarQube step","when":"Pipeline problem: ZAP job fails/empty report, or SonarQube slows PR feedback; give two causes/fixes with trade-offs","mnemonic":"Cause, Fix, Trade-off (CFT) x2","steps":["1. ZAP: is the target running and reachable (health wait, Docker network, URL)?","2. ZAP: are endpoints discovered/authenticated (OpenAPI import, context)?","3. ZAP: do warnings fail the build, is there a timeout, is the report written and uploaded?","4. Sonar: analyse changed code (PR analysis), run the full scan nightly or on merge.","5. Sonar: cache, exclude generated code, parallelise, scale the runner.","6. For each, state the benefit and the trade-off; do not reduce rule quality."],"example":{"problem":"ZAP in the payment-api staging pipeline fails or produces an empty report. Two causes and fixes (5 marks).","solution":["Cause 1: the scan starts before the service is ready, or targets localhost from inside the ZAP container (which is ZAP itself). Fix: wait on the /health endpoint, put both containers on one Docker network and target the service name.","Cause 2: the report is not written or kept: no -r/-J output, no volume, artefact not uploaded, or the step fails on warnings before the upload. Fix: configure report options, mount a writable directory, upload the artefact with `if: always()`, and set thresholds so warnings do not fail the job.","Also: import the OpenAPI spec so POST endpoints are discovered; use a baseline passive scan on PRs and a full active scan nightly."]}},{"id":"refactor-with-patterns","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","title":"Refactor if/else code using polymorphism and patterns","when":"Given code branching on role/type/method, refactor with abstract class, Factory, Strategy, OCP","mnemonic":"Branch on type = polymorphism; create by type = Factory; swap algorithm = Strategy","steps":["1. Spot the smell: repeated if (role.equals ...) or switch, duplicated methods per type.","2. Extract an abstract class/interface with the varying method.","3. Move each branch body into a subclass implementing it.","4. Make the controller depend on the abstraction only.","5. Move creation to a Factory; make it open for extension (registry map).","6. Name the pattern and the principle (OCP, SRP) and the benefit."],"example":{"problem":"PaymentService has if (method.equals(\"card\")) ... else if (\"wallet\") ... else if (\"bank\") .... New methods are added monthly. Refactor.","solution":["Problem: every new method edits tested code (violates OCP) and the class does many things.","Interface: `interface PaymentStrategy { void pay(long cents); }`.","Implementations: `CardPayment`, `WalletPayment`, `BankTransferPayment` each hold their own logic.","Factory/registry: `Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies` (Spring injects all beans) selected by key.","Service: `strategies.get(method).pay(cents)`; unknown key throws IllegalArgumentException.","Benefit: add a method by adding one class; each strategy is unit-testable; the service is closed for modification."]}},{"id":"cyclomatic-complexity-calc","topicId":"sqa-metrics-complexity","title":"Calculate cyclomatic complexity","when":"Given a control flow graph (E, N, P) or code with decisions","mnemonic":"E - N + 2P, or decisions + 1","steps":["1. If a graph: count edges E and nodes N; P = 1 for one method.","2. CC = E - N + 2P.","3. If code: count decision points (if, else if, for, while, case, catch, && , ||) and add 1.","4. Map to the band: 1-10 low, 11-20 moderate, 21-50 high, over 50 very high.","5. State testing implication: at least CC test cases for basis-path coverage."],"example":{"problem":"Graph with E = 8, N = 7 (one method). Also count: for (x : a) { if (x > 0 && x < 10) c++; }","solution":["CC = E - N + 2P = 8 - 7 + 2(1) = 3: low complexity.","Code: decisions: for (1), if (1), && (1) = 3, so CC = 3 + 1 = 4.","Both are in the 1-10 band: easy to understand and test; need about 4 test paths for the second."]}},{"id":"buffer-overflow-answer","topicId":"security-fundamentals","title":"Answer a buffer overflow / vulnerability code question","when":"Given C code (strcpy) or asked to define buffer overflow and countermeasures","mnemonic":"Define, Show, Exploit, Counter x2","steps":["1. Define: data written beyond the buffer boundary corrupts adjacent memory.","2. Point at the exact line (no bounds check, fixed buffer size).","3. Exploit: long input overwrites the return address (or variables), redirects execution to attacker code, crash or privilege escalation.","4. Countermeasure 1 (code): bounds-checked functions and length validation, or safe languages.","5. Countermeasure 2 (platform): stack canaries, ASLR, DEP/NX.","6. Optionally show the fixed code."],"example":{"problem":"void copyCustomerName(char *input) { char name[8]; strcpy(name, input); printf(\"%s\\n\", name); } Identify the vulnerability, exploit and two countermeasures.","solution":["Vulnerability: stack buffer overflow; `strcpy` copies until the null byte without checking the 8-byte size.","Exploit: an input longer than 7 characters overwrites adjacent stack memory including the saved return address; the attacker places shellcode or a return-to-libc address there so the function returns into attacker-controlled code.","Countermeasure 1: use `strncpy(name, input, sizeof(name) - 1)` with explicit termination or `snprintf`, and validate input length.","Countermeasure 2: compile with stack protector (canaries), enable ASLR and non-executable stack (DEP/NX); use code review and static analysis, or a memory-safe language."]}},{"id":"essay-compare-template","topicId":"agile-vs-devops-sre","title":"Answer template: compare A and B / explain and discuss","when":"Any \"compare\", \"distinguish\", \"discuss benefits and trade-offs\" question","mnemonic":"D-T-S-V: Define each, Table the differences, Scenario example, Verdict","steps":["1. One-line definition of each.","2. 3-4 contrasting points across the same dimensions (table-like, parallel sentences).","3. One example from the scenario.","4. A verdict or when to choose which."],"example":{"problem":"Compare monolithic and microservice architecture for the clinic system (4 marks).","solution":["Monolith: one codebase deployed as one unit; microservices: independent services with own data.","Scaling: monolith scales whole app; microservices scale only booking or billing.","Fault isolation and technology: failure in a monolith can stop everything; services isolated and may use different stacks.","Cost: monolith simpler to build, deploy, debug; microservices add distributed complexity.","Verdict: start monolithic for a small clinic; split when scaling or team boundaries demand it."]}},{"id":"classify-docker-failure","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","title":"Classify and fix a Docker failure","when":"Given a symptom (build error, exited container, unreachable app) and asked to diagnose and fix","mnemonic":"B-I-S-C-N-A: Build, Image, Startup, Configuration, Network, Application","steps":["1. Decide which stage failed: docker build (BUILD) or docker run.","2. If it runs: docker ps. Not listed? docker ps -a, then docker logs <name>.","3. Exited: check startup command, missing artefact, runtime version, missing config (env var), app crash.","4. Running but unreachable: check internal port, -p HOST:CONTAINER mapping, app logs, is it listening.","5. Name the category (BUILD, IMAGE, STARTUP, CONFIGURATION, NETWORK/PORT MAPPING or APPLICATION) before touching source code.","6. Apply the smallest fix, rebuild, rerun and verify with an HTTP request."],"example":{"problem":"docker run -d -p 8080:8080 my-app; docker ps shows nothing. Logs: \"Error: Unable to access jarfile app.jar\". Diagnose and fix.","solution":["docker ps -a shows STATUS Exited (1): the container started and stopped, so it is a STARTUP problem.","docker logs my-app: ENTRYPOINT [\"java\",\"-jar\",\"app.jar\"] cannot find the JAR in /app.","Cause (missing artefact): the JAR was never built, or it is named my-app-1.0.jar so COPY target/app.jar app.jar did not match.","Fix: ./mvnw clean package, ls target to get the real name, correct the COPY line, docker build -t my-app ., docker run again.","Verify: docker ps, docker logs my-app, curl http://localhost:8080/ responds."]}},{"id":"jacoco-gap-decision","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","title":"Interpret a JaCoCo gap and decide what to do","when":"Given a coverage percentage or report and asked whether to add tests","mnemonic":"Locate, Question, Decide, Re-run","steps":["1. Run the tests first (mvn clean test) and fix failures; open target/site/jacoco/index.html.","2. Drill down summary, package, class, method, source; find ONE gap (red or yellow).","3. Ask the four questions: requirement/failure mode, business impact, already tested elsewhere, test gap vs smell vs debt.","4. Choose: add a test for an agreed rule, refactor, or no change (a justified no-change is valid).","5. Re-run and compare the SAME counter at the same level before and after; record what did not change."],"example":{"problem":"Exam Registry reports 85% instruction and 85% branch coverage. recordScore leaves one branch missed. What do you do?","solution":["The percentage alone does not say whether it is good; locate the gap: the recordScore branch for a score outside 0-100.","Requirement: valid scores are in the inclusive range 0-100; outside must be rejected. Impact: invalid grades could be stored, so it matters.","Add a test: -1 and 101 are rejected (and 0 and 100 accepted as boundaries).","Re-run and compare branch coverage before and after. If instruction coverage rose (e.g. 86% to 96%) but branches stay at 18/20 = 90%, say so honestly and investigate the other missed branch rather than inventing rules."]}}],"practice":[{"id":"2019-q1a","topicId":"git-commands","source":"2019-20 Q1(a)","marks":4,"question":"When making a commit to an online repository from your laptop, what is the sequence of git commands that have to be executed? Explain the reasoning behind each step. Assume the repository has already been cloned.","outline":["git status: see what changed","git add: stage the chosen changes","git commit -m \"message\": record snapshot locally","git pull: integrate remote changes first and resolve conflicts","git push: publish the commit to the online repository","One reason sentence per step"]},{"id":"2019-q1b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q1(b)","marks":8,"question":"Take two software architectural styles discussed in lectures and discuss key aspects where they might have traits in common and where they would differ.","outline":["Name two styles (e.g. monolithic and microservices, or layered and client-server)","Shared traits: structure of components, separation of concerns, NFR goals","Differences: deployment, scaling, coupling, fault isolation, data management","Short example for each","Conclusion on when to choose which"]},{"id":"2019-q1c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q1(c)","marks":3,"question":"Distinguish between IaaS, PaaS and SaaS and give typical customer needs served by each.","outline":["IaaS: rented VMs/storage/network; customer manages OS and apps; needs raw compute","PaaS: platform to deploy code without managing servers; developers","SaaS: finished application used via browser; end users, e.g. email/CRM"]},{"id":"2019-q1d","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2019-20 Q1(d)","marks":5,"question":"Distinguish between an architectural style and a design pattern. Use examples.","outline":["Style = system-wide organisation (layered, microservices)","Pattern = reusable solution to a local design problem (Factory, Observer)","Scope and level differ","Example of each","Patterns are used inside a style"]},{"id":"2019-q2a","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2019-20 Q2(a)","marks":8,"question":"Take two design patterns. Using a UML diagram illustrate how these patterns are coded, discuss how they are implemented and outline benefits.","outline":["Pick two (e.g. Factory, Observer) and name roles","Class diagram: interface/abstract class, concrete classes, relationships","Implementation sketch (Java)","Benefits: loose coupling, extensibility, reuse, shared vocabulary"]},{"id":"2019-q2b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q2(b)","marks":2,"question":"What do we mean when we say a component provides a service?","outline":["Component exposes a well-defined interface/contract","Clients use the functionality without knowing implementation","Example"]},{"id":"2019-q2c","topicId":"docker-concepts","source":"2019-20 Q2(c)","marks":6,"question":"Distinguish between: type 1 and type 2 hypervisor; host and guest machine; virtual appliances; Docker.","outline":["Type 1 on bare metal, type 2 on host OS","Host = physical machine, guest = VM","Virtual appliance = pre-built VM image","Docker = containers sharing host kernel, lighter than VMs"]},{"id":"2019-q2d","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q2(d)","marks":4,"question":"List and explain 3 potential errors that might happen when a proxy is used in place of a callback object.","outline":["Network failure or latency on remote calls","Stale or inconsistent state","Identity/equality and type issues","Security/serialisation problems (any 3 explained)"]},{"id":"2019-q3a","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2019-20 Q3(a)","marks":6,"question":"Describe the core components of a modern continuous software development system and how they interact and are inter-linked.","outline":["Version control (Git) triggers","CI server builds and tests automatically","Artefact repository / registry","Deployment automation (containers)","Monitoring feedback loop","How each links to the next"]},{"id":"2019-q3b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q3(b)","marks":5,"question":"Identify the key differences between a monolithic kernel and a microkernel.","outline":["Monolithic: all OS services in kernel space, fast, less modular","Microkernel: minimal kernel, services in user space, modular and safer","IPC overhead vs performance","Fault isolation and extensibility"]},{"id":"2019-q3c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q3(c)","marks":3,"question":"In the context of plugin architectures, what does POSA define as the key actors involved?","outline":["Core/host system","Plugin interface/extension point","Plugins (extensions) and a registry/loader"]},{"id":"2019-q3d","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2019-20 Q3(d)","marks":6,"question":"Discuss which design pattern might be most effectively used in a plugin environment to improve efficiency and explain how it works.","outline":["Name a pattern (e.g. Factory/Proxy lazy loading/Flyweight)","Explain mechanism","Efficiency benefit","Small diagram or code"]},{"id":"2019-q4a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q4(a)","marks":4,"question":"What is the CAP theorem? How do ACID and BASE transactions relate to it?","outline":["Consistency, availability, partition tolerance: at most two","ACID favours consistency","BASE favours availability with eventual consistency"]},{"id":"2019-q4b","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2019-20 Q4(b)","marks":6,"question":"Illustrate with a diagram a design pattern covered in the course. What is the strength of using design patterns?","outline":["Diagram of a pattern with roles","Brief explanation","Strengths: reuse, communication, proven, maintainable"]},{"id":"2019-q4c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2019-20 Q4(c)","marks":5,"question":"Using diagrams distinguish between NAT, Bridged adapter and NAT network in VirtualBox, outlining characteristics and disadvantages.","outline":["NAT: guest behind host, outbound only, not reachable","Bridged: own IP on LAN, exposed, needs LAN address","NAT network: guests share a private network and can talk to each other, still not inbound reachable","A disadvantage of each"]},{"id":"2019-q4d","topicId":"architecture-monolith-microservices","source":"2019-20 Q4(d)","marks":5,"question":"In the layered architecture, what is the purpose of an open layer? List two other layers and their purpose.","outline":["Open layer may be bypassed to avoid pointless pass-through","Closed layers force strict ordering","Two layers e.g. presentation (UI) and persistence (data access) with purposes"]},{"id":"2021-q1a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q1(a)","marks":5,"question":"What is a surjective function (in Z)? Give an example in Z notation.","outline":["Every element of the codomain is the image of some element: ran f = Y","Notation X -» Y","Example e.g. {1 |-> a, 2 |-> b, 3 |-> a}"]},{"id":"2021-q1b1","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q1(b1)","marks":5,"question":"Train booking state: booked : P PERSON, #booked <= trainCapacity. Give a suitable initial state schema.","outline":["TrainBookingInit with TrainBooking primed","booked' = empty set","Satisfies the invariant"]},{"id":"2021-q1b2","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q1(b2)","marks":10,"question":"Write Z specifications for someone books a ticket and someone cancels a booking (no error handling).","outline":["Book: Delta TrainBooking, p? : PERSON, p? not in booked, #booked < trainCapacity, booked' = booked U {p?}","Cancel: Delta TrainBooking, p? : PERSON, p? in booked, booked' = booked \\ {p?}"]},{"id":"2021-q2a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q2(a)","marks":5,"question":"Explain in your own words the precise meaning of SupermarketItems: carried : P ITEM; level : ITEM +-> N; dom level = carried.","outline":["carried = set of items stocked","level maps item to current quantity","Domain of level equals carried: every carried item has a level and nothing else does"]},{"id":"2021-q2b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q2(b)","marks":5,"question":"Provide a suitable initial state schema for the supermarket.","outline":["carried' = empty set, level' = empty function","Satisfies dom level = carried"]},{"id":"2021-q2c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q2(c)","marks":10,"question":"Z specification for buying a given quantity of a given item.","outline":["Delta SupermarketItems, item? : ITEM, qty? : N","item? in carried","level' = level (+) {item? |-> level(item?) - qty?}","carried' = carried","Precondition qty? <= level(item?) (error handling not required)"]},{"id":"2021-q3a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q3(a)","marks":7,"question":"Create an initial state schema for the football (soccer) rules: 2 teams, 11 field players each, 7 substitutes, max 3 substitutions, yellow/red cards, dismissal.","outline":["State: field players, substitutes, dismissed, yellow card count, red card count, substitutions used per team","Init: 11 field players and 7 substitutes per team, empty dismissed set, counts zero","Invariants: max 3 subs, yellow <= 2, red <= 1"]},{"id":"2021-q3b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q3(b)","marks":13,"question":"Write Z specifications to show a player a red card, a yellow card, and query the number of field players of a team.","outline":["RedCard: player on field, red count +1, player removed from field (no substitution)","YellowCard: yellow count +1; if it becomes 2 the player is dismissed","Query: Xi schema, output n! = #(fieldPlayers(team?))"]},{"id":"2021-q4a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q4(a)","marks":7,"question":"What type of cookies, content/structure and attributes for the athletics club site? Explain lifecycle with code snippets.","outline":["Session cookie with random id, not user data","Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, Path, Domain, Max-Age/Expires","Lifecycle: Set-Cookie on login, sent each request, expiry, logout","Snippet of Set-Cookie header"]},{"id":"2021-q4b","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2021-22 Q4(b)","marks":10,"question":"Show with concrete examples how the website could be attacked via reflected and persistent XSS, and the outcome.","outline":["Reflected: malicious link with script in a search parameter echoed back","Persistent: script in a forum post served to every reader","Outcomes: cookie/session theft, defacement, phishing, actions as victim"]},{"id":"2021-q4c","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2021-22 Q4(c)","marks":3,"question":"Briefly discuss how the website could be hardened against XSS.","outline":["Output encoding for context","Input validation","Content Security Policy","HttpOnly cookies"]},{"id":"2021-q5a","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2021-22 Q5(a)","marks":10,"question":"Describe the inner workings of an SQL injection attack and how it could retrieve or manipulate user credentials.","outline":["Concatenated query: data becomes SQL","Example ' OR '1'='1 bypasses login","UNION SELECT to dump user table","UPDATE/DROP to manipulate"]},{"id":"2021-q5b","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2021-22 Q5(b)","marks":3,"question":"How could such attacks be prevented?","outline":["Parameterised queries / prepared statements","Input validation (allowlist)","Least-privilege database account, error hiding"]},{"id":"2021-q5c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q5(c)","marks":3,"question":"Benefits of password hashing and characteristics of a good hash function.","outline":["Stored hashes limit damage when credentials leak","Deterministic, one-way (pre-image resistant), collision resistant, avalanche, salted slow hash"]},{"id":"2021-q5d","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2021-22 Q5(d)","marks":4,"question":"How can dictionary attacks be used to recover hashed passwords?","outline":["Hash a list of common passwords and compare to stolen hashes","Precomputed tables, rainbow tables","Defences: salt, slow hash, rate limits, strong passwords"]},{"id":"2023s1-q1a","topicId":"agile-manifesto","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q1(a)","marks":4,"question":"Describe the Agile Manifesto and list TWO of its core principles.","outline":["Manifesto: values individuals, working software, customer collaboration, responding to change","Two principles e.g. deliver working software frequently, welcome changing requirements"]},{"id":"2023s1-q1b","topicId":"agile-manifesto","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q1(b)","marks":6,"question":"Soccermatic: rapidly changing requirements and evolving data sources. Propose a strategy following one of the 12 principles: identify the principle and propose TWO solutions.","outline":["Principle 2: welcome changing requirements","Solution 1: re-prioritised backlog each sprint","Solution 2: short sprints with demos/feedback or automated testing and CI"]},{"id":"2023s1-q1c","topicId":"agile-manifesto","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q1(c)","marks":5,"question":"Using \"collaboration between business stakeholders and developers throughout the project\", suggest FIVE steps to resolve prediction accuracy issues.","outline":["Embed product owner/stakeholder","Define measurable accuracy acceptance criteria","Joint backlog refinement","Stakeholders in sprint reviews","Retrospective and feedback loop into backlog"]},{"id":"2023s1-q2a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q2(a)","marks":3,"question":"List THREE advantages of using formal specifications.","outline":["Unambiguous precise description","Enables reasoning/proof and early error detection","Basis for testing and better understanding"]},{"id":"2023s1-q2b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q2(b)","marks":2,"question":"Describe the initial state schema for Cursor.","outline":["line in 1..numLines, column in 1..numColumns","Init: line' = 1, column' = 1 (top left)"]},{"id":"2023s1-q2c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q2(c)","marks":4,"question":"Define DownKeyNormal for the cursor not on the bottom line.","outline":["Delta Cursor","Precondition line < numLines","line' = line + 1, column' = column"]},{"id":"2023s1-q2d","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q2(d)","marks":4,"question":"Define DownKeyAtBottom for the cursor on the bottom line.","outline":["Xi Cursor (state unchanged)","Precondition line = numLines"]},{"id":"2023s1-q2e","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q2(e)","marks":2,"question":"Define the full behaviour.","outline":["DownKey = DownKeyNormal or DownKeyAtBottom (schema disjunction)"]},{"id":"2023s1-q3a","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q3(a)","marks":2,"question":"Define \"vulnerability\" (RFC 2828).","outline":["Flaw or weakness in design, implementation or operation and management","Could be exploited to violate the security policy"]},{"id":"2023s1-q3b","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q3(b)","marks":2,"question":"Define \"countermeasures\".","outline":["Action, device, procedure or technique","Reduces a threat, vulnerability or attack by eliminating/preventing it, minimising harm or detecting and reporting it"]},{"id":"2023s1-q3c","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q3(c)","marks":2,"question":"What is a zero-day vulnerability?","outline":["Flaw unknown to vendor/no patch available","Exploitable before it can be fixed (zero days)"]},{"id":"2023s1-q3d","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q3(d)","marks":5,"question":"Describe a typical life cycle of a zero-day vulnerability.","outline":["Introduced in code","Discovered (attacker/researcher)","Exploited (zero-day attack)","Disclosed/vendor aware","Patch released and deployed"]},{"id":"2023s1-q3e","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q3(e)","marks":4,"question":"Define buffer overflow and how it can be exploited by attackers.","outline":["Writing past the buffer boundary","Unsafe C functions with no bounds check","Overwrite return address / inject code","Consequences: code execution, DoS, privilege escalation"]},{"id":"2023s1-q4a","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q4(a)","marks":3,"question":"List THREE advantages of a typical CI process.","outline":["Early bug detection","Fewer integration problems","Faster reliable releases / automation"]},{"id":"2023s1-q4b","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q4(b)","marks":6,"question":"Discuss THREE potential pitfalls when adopting CI/CD.","outline":["E.g. slow pipelines, flaky tests, cultural resistance, tool complexity, security gaps","Each with explanation and mitigation"]},{"id":"2023s1-q4c","topicId":"unit-testing-junit","source":"2023-24 Sem 1 Q4(c)","marks":6,"question":"Discuss THREE test cases for Calculator.add(int a, int b) with sample code.","outline":["Positive operands","Negative / mixed operands","Zero and boundary (MAX_VALUE overflow)","JUnit code with assertEquals for each"]},{"id":"2023a-q1a","topicId":"agile-manifesto","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q1(a)","marks":6,"question":"Identify and explain THREE core principles of Agile that XYZ Corporation should follow for the CRM project.","outline":["Three principles, each explained","Tied to the CRM (customer collaboration, working software, welcome change)"]},{"id":"2023a-q1b","topicId":"agile-manifesto","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q1(b)","marks":9,"question":"Describe how the team can implement these principles in daily activities and sprint planning to ensure customer collaboration, response to change and delivering working software, with CRM examples.","outline":["Customer collaboration: product owner, sprint reviews, user stories","Response to change: backlog reprioritisation, short sprints","Working software: increments each sprint, CI/CD, demos","CRM-specific examples for each"]},{"id":"2023a-q2a","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q2(a)","marks":3,"question":"List THREE advantages of using formal specifications.","outline":["Precision, reasoning/verification, early defect detection"]},{"id":"2023a-q2b","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q2(b)","marks":2,"question":"Describe the initial state schema for Cursor.","outline":["line = 1, column = 1"]},{"id":"2023a-q2c","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q2(c)","marks":4,"question":"Define DownKeyNormal.","outline":["Delta Cursor, line < numLines, line' = line + 1, column unchanged"]},{"id":"2023a-q2d","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q2(d)","marks":4,"question":"Define DownKeyAtBottom.","outline":["Xi Cursor, line = numLines"]},{"id":"2023a-q2e","topicId":"legacy-topics","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q2(e)","marks":2,"question":"Define the full behaviour of the schema.","outline":["Disjunction of the two schemas"]},{"id":"2023a-q3a","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q3(a)","marks":4,"question":"Discuss TWO benefits of Java build tools compared to manual build processes.","outline":["Repeatable automated builds","Dependency management","Standard structure / CI integration"]},{"id":"2023a-q3b","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q3(b)","marks":4,"question":"Describe TWO core features of Apache Maven.","outline":["POM and convention over configuration","Dependency management with repositories","Lifecycle and plugins"]},{"id":"2023a-q3c","topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q3(c)","marks":7,"question":"Describe the typical steps in the Java build process using a build tool such as Maven.","outline":["validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, deploy","Dependency resolution first","Plugins/goals bound to phases","Reports and artefact output in target/"]},{"id":"2023a-q4a","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q4(a)","marks":3,"question":"Explain the primary goals and TWO benefits of implementing CI/CD.","outline":["Goal: frequent reliable automated delivery","Benefits: fast feedback, fewer errors, quicker releases"]},{"id":"2023a-q4b","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q4(b)","marks":8,"question":"Discuss THREE challenges Tech Solutions may face implementing CI/CD and suggest strategies.","outline":["Three challenges (culture, slow tests, environments, security)","Strategy for each"]},{"id":"2023a-q4c","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2023-24 Autumn Q4(c)","marks":4,"question":"Define buffer overflow and how it can be exploited.","outline":["Define, unsafe C functions, overwrite return address, consequences"]},{"id":"2024-q1a","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2024-25 Q1(a)","marks":8,"question":"FoodieGo: manual testing and approvals delay releases; integration conflicts; updates cause downtime with no robust test environment; security checks too late. Propose FOUR specific CI/CD pipeline improvements.","outline":["Automated tests on every PR/commit (unit, integration, regression)","CI with short-lived branches and PR checks to cut conflicts","Staging environment matching production, smoke tests, canary/blue-green and automated rollback","Shift-left security: SAST, SCA, secret scan, DAST on staging","Each tied to the scenario problem"]},{"id":"2024-q1b","topicId":"git-commands","source":"2024-25 Q1(b)","marks":5,"question":"Write the Git commands to: check status, add all changes, commit with message \"Implemented new feature.\", push to remote feature-branch, merge feature-branch into main.","outline":["git status","git add .","git commit -m \"Implemented new feature.\"","git push origin feature-branch","git checkout main, git pull, git merge feature-branch (then push)"]},{"id":"2024-q1c","topicId":"github-actions","source":"2024-25 Q1(c)","marks":7,"question":"Explain what the build job does based on the given GitHub Actions YAML (checkout, JDK 11, mvn clean package, mvn verify, docker build, docker push).","outline":["Trigger: push to main","Runner ubuntu-latest","Checkout, setup JDK 11","mvn clean package: compile, test, JAR","mvn verify: further checks/coverage","docker build and docker push to registry","Issues: no login, tag mismatch, latest only"]},{"id":"2024-q2a","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2024-25 Q2(a)","marks":4,"question":"Explain the difference between SAST and DAST.","outline":["SAST static, source, early","DAST dynamic, running app, black-box","Different vulnerability types","Tools"]},{"id":"2024-q2b","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2024-25 Q2(b)","marks":2,"question":"Why is it important to integrate SAST and DAST in a CI/CD pipeline?","outline":["Find vulnerabilities early and automatically","Cheaper fixes, continuous compliance"]},{"id":"2024-q2c","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","source":"2024-25 Q2(c)","marks":2,"question":"What is the role of code coverage in unit testing?","outline":["Measures how much code the tests execute","Shows untested areas; not proof of correctness"]},{"id":"2024-q2d","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2024-25 Q2(d)","marks":4,"question":"SonarQube analysis slows the pipeline. Propose TWO improvements without reducing quality, with justification and trade-offs.","outline":["Incremental/PR analysis of changed code","Nightly full scan / parallel / cache / exclude generated","Why it works and trade-off for each"]},{"id":"2024-q2e","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2024-25 Q2(e)","marks":4,"question":"OWASP ZAP integration fails and reports are hard to find. Identify TWO common issues and practical solutions.","outline":["Target not running/reachable, network","Warnings fail the step, report not saved/uploaded","Fix for each"]},{"id":"2024-q2f","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","source":"2024-25 Q2(f)","marks":4,"question":"Low test coverage in critical Java code. Explain how to use a coverage tool to find gaps and propose a strategy to increase coverage without slowing the pipeline.","outline":["JaCoCo report, drill to class/method, red/yellow","Target critical business logic","Fast isolated unit tests, parameterised, mocks","Coverage gate, heavy tests nightly"]},{"id":"2024-q3a","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(a)","marks":2,"question":"Define \"vulnerability\" (RFC 2828).","outline":["Flaw/weakness in design, implementation, operation that could be exploited to violate security policy"]},{"id":"2024-q3b","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(b)","marks":2,"question":"Define \"countermeasures\".","outline":["Action/device/procedure/technique that reduces a threat, vulnerability or attack"]},{"id":"2024-q3c","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(c)","marks":2,"question":"What is a zero-day vulnerability?","outline":["Unknown to vendor, no patch, exploitable immediately"]},{"id":"2024-q3d","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(d)","marks":5,"question":"Describe the 5 stages of the life cycle of a zero-day vulnerability.","outline":["Introduced, discovered, exploited, disclosed/vendor aware, patched and deployed"]},{"id":"2024-q3e","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(e)","marks":5,"question":"Define and explain buffer overflow and how it can be exploited.","outline":["Definition","Unsafe C functions","Overwritten return address / shellcode","Consequences"]},{"id":"2024-q3f","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2024-25 Q3(f)","marks":4,"question":"Discuss TWO countermeasures for buffer overflow.","outline":["Bounds checking/safe functions","Canaries, ASLR, DEP/NX","Explain how each works"]},{"id":"2024-q4a","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2024-25 Q4(a)","marks":4,"question":"Explain the importance of design patterns in software development.","outline":["Reusable proven solutions","Common vocabulary","Maintainability, extensibility, reduced coupling"]},{"id":"2024-q4b","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2024-25 Q4(b)","marks":4,"question":"Why is refactoring important for improving code reusability?","outline":["Remove duplication","Extract abstractions","Lower coupling, higher cohesion","Tests keep behaviour safe"]},{"id":"2024-q4c","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2024-25 Q4(c)","marks":4,"question":"Refactor authenticateCustomer/authenticateManager in UserController to use the abstract User class and polymorphism.","outline":["Single authenticate(User user) calling user.authenticate()","Customer and Manager override authenticate","No role-specific methods in controller"]},{"id":"2024-q4d","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2024-25 Q4(d)","marks":4,"question":"Refactor createCustomerAccount/createManagerAccount to avoid duplication using a Factory pattern.","outline":["One createAccount(role, ...) in controller","Delegates to UserFactory.createUser","Controller depends on User abstraction"]},{"id":"2024-q4e","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2024-25 Q4(e)","marks":4,"question":"Refactor UserFactory so adding Admin does not require modifying createUser (Open/Closed).","outline":["Registry map role -> creator","register(\"admin\", Admin::new)","createUser looks up and calls the creator","Unknown role throws"]},{"id":"2025s1-q1a","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q1(a)","marks":5,"question":"GitHub Actions deployment failed: docker pull ghcr.io/skybankai/app:latest returned manifest unknown. Using only git and basic docker commands, show how to diagnose, identify the cause and rectify and redeploy.","outline":["Diagnose: git log, check workflow tags, docker manifest inspect, docker images","Cause: latest tag never pushed (only SHA tag)","Rectify: tag and push latest (or deploy SHA)","Redeploy: docker pull, docker compose up -d, docker ps, docker logs"]},{"id":"2025s1-q1b","topicId":"github-actions","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q1(b)","marks":5,"question":"Design a simplified GitHub Actions CI/CD pipeline for a Node.js microservice: tests on PRs, build and push Docker image after merge to main, deploy to staging. List and explain the key steps.","outline":["Triggers: pull_request and push to main","Test job: checkout, setup-node, npm ci, npm test","Image job needs test, if main, login, build, tag with SHA, push","Deploy staging job pulling the tag, smoke test","Secrets used for credentials"]},{"id":"2025s1-q1c","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q1(c)","marks":5,"question":"Pipeline: tests only on main, every commit to main deploys directly to production, no rollback strategy. Identify risks and propose improvements.","outline":["Risks: untested code merges, direct prod, downtime","PR tests and branch protection","Staging, approval, canary","Rollback with versioned images","Versioning rule"]},{"id":"2025s1-q1d","topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q1(d)","marks":5,"question":"Explain how merge conflicts arise in collaborative GitHub workflows and describe a practical process to prevent and resolve them.","outline":["Arise: same lines changed on different branches, long-lived branches","Prevent: short branches, pull/rebase often, small PRs, ownership, CI","Resolve: fetch, open files, edit markers, test, add, commit, push"]},{"id":"2025s1-q2a","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q2(a)","marks":5,"question":"Explain differences between SAST and DAST: when applied, vulnerability types, need for source code, tool example for each.","outline":["SAST early, DAST needs running app","SAST insecure code/flows; DAST runtime/config","SAST needs code, DAST does not","SonarQube / OWASP ZAP"]},{"id":"2025s1-q2b","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q2(b)","marks":5,"question":"OWASP ZAP in pipeline fails often and generates no meaningful reports. Identify two likely technical causes and a resolution for each.","outline":["App not up / network","Endpoints not discovered/auth","Warnings fail job or report not saved","Fix for each"]},{"id":"2025s1-q2c","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q2(c)","marks":5,"question":"SonarQube slows CI feedback. Propose two changes to improve performance and explain effect and trade-offs.","outline":["Incremental PR analysis","Nightly full scan, parallel, cache, exclusions","Trade-offs"]},{"id":"2025s1-q2d","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q2(d)","marks":5,"question":"Low coverage in the payment module. Explain how a coverage tool identifies low-coverage areas and describe one strategy to increase coverage efficiently without slowing the pipeline.","outline":["Run JaCoCo/Istanbul/Coverage.py, read report","Prioritise payment logic","Fast unit tests with mocks, coverage gate, heavy tests nightly"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3a","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(a)","marks":1,"question":"Define \"vulnerability\".","outline":["Weakness in design/implementation/operation exploitable to violate security policy"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3b","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(b)","marks":1,"question":"Define \"countermeasures\".","outline":["Action/technique that reduces a threat, vulnerability or attack"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3c","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(c)","marks":2,"question":"What is zero-day vulnerability?","outline":["Flaw unknown to vendor, no patch, exploited before fix"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3d1","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(d) i","marks":4,"question":"Incidents: #1 MTTD 4 h, MTTR 2 h, downtime 6 h; #2 30 min, 1 h, 1.5 h; #3 6 h, 4 h, 10 h. Calculate the average MTBF assuming MTBF = MTTD + MTTR for each incident.","outline":["Convert: 30 min = 0.5 h","MTBF values 6, 1.5, 10","Average = 17.5 / 3 = 5.83 h (5 h 50 min)"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3d2","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(d) ii","marks":2,"question":"Identify which incident demonstrates the best operational performance and why.","outline":["Incident 2: lowest MTTD, MTTR and downtime","Automated alerting active"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3d3","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(d) iii","marks":4,"question":"Recommend two practical improvements to reduce MTTD or MTTR.","outline":["Automated alert correlation and paging","Runbooks, automated rollback/patching, observability","Explain impact on metrics"]},{"id":"2025s1-q3e","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q3(e)","marks":6,"question":"Define and explain buffer overflow and how it can be exploited. Discuss TWO countermeasures.","outline":["Definition and exploit mechanism","Two countermeasures with explanation"]},{"id":"2025s1-q4a","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q4(a)","marks":4,"question":"Why is refactoring important for improving code reusability?","outline":["Remove duplication, abstractions, lower coupling, testability"]},{"id":"2025s1-q4b","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q4(b)","marks":6,"question":"Identify two concrete design problems in the SkyBankAI system; for each describe the problem, name the pattern and explain the improvement.","outline":["Role conditionals: Factory + polymorphism","Payment conditionals: Strategy","Hard-coded notifications: Observer/Adapter","Benefit statements"]},{"id":"2025s1-q4c1","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q4(c) i","marks":2,"question":"Refactor UserController using an abstract User class or interface.","outline":["abstract class User with authenticate()/create abstract","Controller uses User"]},{"id":"2025s1-q4c2","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q4(c) ii","marks":4,"question":"Role-specific subclasses Customer and Manager.","outline":["class Customer extends User with override","class Manager extends User with override","Constructors with extraInfo"]},{"id":"2025s1-q4c3","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Sem 1 Q4(c) iii","marks":4,"question":"A UserFactory to handle user instantiation.","outline":["createUser(role, username, password, extraInfo)","Returns User; unknown role throws","Controller delegates to factory; extensible"]},{"id":"2025a-q1a","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q1(a)","marks":5,"question":"Using the deployment log and workflow fragment, identify the exact technical cause of the failed deployment and explain why the build can succeed while deployment fails.","outline":["Build pushes only SHA tag","Deploy pulls :latest which was never pushed","Registry returns manifest unknown","Build only needs the SHA push to succeed; stages are independent"]},{"id":"2025a-q1b","topicId":"github-actions","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q1(b)","marks":5,"question":"Rewrite only the Build and push image step so the image is available using both the commit SHA tag and the latest tag.","outline":["docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest .","docker push $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }}","docker push $IMAGE:latest","Brief explanation"]},{"id":"2025a-q1c","topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q1(c)","marks":5,"question":"Give a practical sequence of basic Docker commands on the staging server to pull the corrected image, restart the service and verify it is running.","outline":["docker pull image:tag","docker compose up -d payment-api (or stop/rm/run)","docker ps","docker logs","curl health endpoint"]},{"id":"2025a-q1d","topicId":"cicd-concepts","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q1(d)","marks":5,"question":"Recommend four practical workflow improvements: one pre-merge control, one staging/deployment control, one rollback approach, one tagging/versioning rule.","outline":["Pre-merge: PR tests and review, branch protection","Staging: deploy to staging, smoke tests, approval, canary","Rollback: redeploy previous SHA tag/git revert","Tagging: SHA and semantic version, avoid bare latest"]},{"id":"2025a-q2a","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q2(a)","marks":5,"question":"Differences between SAST and DAST: lifecycle timing, source access, issue types, tool example.","outline":["Timing","Source access","Issue types","SonarQube / ZAP"]},{"id":"2025a-q2b","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q2(b)","marks":5,"question":"ZAP job scanning staging payment-api fails or produces an empty report. Two likely technical causes and fixes.","outline":["Service not ready/unreachable; use health wait and Docker network","Report not saved/uploaded or warnings fail the step; configure output/artefact/thresholds","Spidering misses endpoints; import OpenAPI"]},{"id":"2025a-q2c","topicId":"sast-dast-zap-sonar","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q2(c)","marks":5,"question":"SonarQube slows PR feedback. Propose two ways to reduce the impact; for each give the improvement, benefit and a trade-off.","outline":["PR/incremental analysis; full scan nightly","Caching, exclusions, parallel, bigger runner","Benefit and trade-off each"]},{"id":"2025a-q2d","topicId":"jacoco-coverage","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q2(d)","marks":5,"question":"How a coverage tool such as JaCoCo, Istanbul/nyc or Coverage.py helps identify low-coverage areas, then one practical strategy to increase useful coverage without slowing CI.","outline":["Generate and read the report; missed lines/branches","Focus on payment logic","Fast unit/parameterised tests; gate; nightly heavy tests"]},{"id":"2025a-q3a","topicId":"sca-security","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q3(a)","marks":5,"question":"Which TWO findings should SkyBankAI fix? Justify with severity, exposure, business impact and fix availability.","outline":["D jsonwebtoken Critical customer sessions fix yes","A express High public API fix yes","B internal, C medium build-time requires major upgrade: defer","Justify with the four criteria"]},{"id":"2025a-q3b","topicId":"sca-security","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q3(b)","marks":5,"question":"For the highest-priority finding describe a safe remediation workflow using Git, package management and CI/CD: made, tested, reviewed, deployed.","outline":["Branch, bump patched version, lockfile","Tests incl. regression","PR review, CI scans","Staged deploy with rollback"]},{"id":"2025a-q3c","topicId":"mttd-mttr-mtbf","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q3(c)","marks":5,"question":"Incidents (start/detected/restored): 09:00/09:40/10:30; 13:15/13:20/13:50; 18:00/19:30/21:00. Calculate MTTD and MTTR each, the averages, and the best incident.","outline":["MTTD 40, 5, 90 min; MTTR 50, 30, 90 min","Average MTTD 45 min, average MTTR 56.7 min","Best: incident 2"]},{"id":"2025a-q3d","topicId":"security-fundamentals","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q3(d)","marks":5,"question":"C code: char name[8]; strcpy(name, input). Identify the vulnerability, how an attacker could exploit it, and TWO countermeasures.","outline":["Buffer overflow via strcpy no bounds check","Overwrite return address, code execution","strncpy/snprintf with length check","Canaries/ASLR/DEP or safe language"]},{"id":"2025a-q4a","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q4(a)","marks":4,"question":"Why is refactoring important for improving code reusability?","outline":["Remove duplication, abstractions, lower coupling"]},{"id":"2025a-q4b","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q4(b)","marks":6,"question":"Identify two concrete design problems in the system, describe, name the pattern and explain the improvement.","outline":["Factory/polymorphism for roles","Strategy for payments","Observer for notifications"]},{"id":"2025a-q4c","topicId":"design-patterns-refactoring","source":"2025-26 Autumn Q4(c)","marks":10,"question":"Refactor the UserController using an abstract User class or interface (2), role-specific subclasses Customer and Manager (4) and a UserFactory (4).","outline":["abstract User with abstract authenticate/create","Customer and Manager subclasses","UserFactory createUser with registry or switch","Controller delegates; Partner/Auditor added without editing controller"]}],"mocks":[{"id":"mock1","title":"Mock Paper 1: PawCare Veterinary Clinic","duration":120,"instructions":"Answer any 3 of 4 questions; all 20 marks. Where necessary, state any assumptions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"PawCare Veterinary Clinic runs a desktop appointment system used by two receptionists and three vets. The clinic is extending its existing software with (1) online booking for pet owners, (2) SMS reminders and (3) prescription tracking. A team of four developers uses Scrum with two-week sprints. The head vet acts as product owner.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"Describe the Agile Manifesto: state its four values, and explain how any TWO of them apply to the PawCare extension project.","outline":["Manifesto (2001): better ways of developing software; values on the left are valued more than the items on the right","Individuals and interactions over processes and tools; working software over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration over contract negotiation; responding to change over following a plan","Customer collaboration applied: head vet as product owner reviews each sprint demo; receptionists try the booking form","Working software applied: each sprint ends with a deployable increment, e.g. online booking form live for one vet","Marks: 2 for four values, 1 + 1 for the two applications"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"Owners and vets keep requesting late changes (accept card payments, several pets per booking, SMS in Irish). Choose ONE of the 12 Agile principles that addresses this, state it, and propose THREE concrete practices the PawCare team should adopt following it.","outline":["Principle 2: welcome changing requirements, even late in development; agile processes harness change for the customer's competitive advantage (1 + 1 marks)","Practice 1: prioritised product backlog owned by the head vet, re-ordered at each sprint planning; new requests become stories for a later sprint (1)","Practice 2: two-week sprints ending with a demo and retrospective so changes are absorbed in small steps and feedback arrives early (1)","Practice 3: automated tests and CI so changing booking rules is cheap and safe; keep stories small (INVEST) (1)","Link to PawCare examples (card payments as a new story in sprint 4) (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"req-attributes","text":"The product owner writes: \"The online booking should be easy and fast and notify people.\" (i) Identify problems with this requirement against at least FOUR attributes of good requirements. (ii) Rewrite it as TWO functional requirements and ONE non-functional requirement. State your assumptions.","outline":["Assumptions: owners book via a web page; SMS provider exists; peak 20 concurrent users","Unambiguous: \"easy\", \"fast\", \"people\" are vague (1)","Verifiable: no measurable criteria so it cannot be tested (1)","Complete: who notifies whom, when, with what content missing; mixes three needs, not concise (1)","Necessary/feasible/traceable: no stakeholder source; cannot be traced to tests (1 for any 4th attribute)","FR-1: An owner shall be able to book an appointment by selecting a registered pet, a vet and a free 15-minute slot (0.5)","FR-2: The system shall send an SMS confirmation to the owner within 1 minute of booking (0.5)","NFR-1: 95% of booking submissions shall complete in under 2 seconds with 20 concurrent users (1)"]},{"label":"d","marks":4,"topicId":"req-change-management","text":"A vet submits the change request: \"Show only one pet's visits instead of the full visit list.\" Outline how PawCare should manage this change from request to release. State your assumptions.","outline":["Assumptions: head vet is the change authority; existing visit list must keep working; pet ids numeric","Raise and record as a GitHub issue keeping the original wording; clarify into requirement + acceptance criteria (filter returns only that pet; no filter returns full list) + open questions (unknown pet) (1)","Impact analysis: controller, service, query, tests, docs; additive and non-breaking; effort and risk (1)","Decision by change authority with priority; readiness separate from priority (1)","Implement on a feature branch via pull request, test incl. regression, update traceability (issue-PR-tests) and release notes (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"The PawCare back end is a Java 17 service built with Maven. The team is converting it to Spring Boot and is also standardising its Git workflow.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":5,"topicId":"maven-structure-lifecycle","text":"Draw (as a list) the Maven standard directory layout for the project pawcare-api, saying what each folder contains. Explain what `mvn clean package` does phase by phase and where the JAR appears.","outline":["pom.xml at root (1)","src/main/java (code), src/main/resources (properties), src/test/java (JUnit tests), src/test/resources, target/ (output) (2)","clean lifecycle deletes target/; then validate, compile, test (Surefire runs tests), package (jar) (1.5)","JAR at target/pawcare-api-<version>.jar; dependencies fetched from ~/.m2 then Maven Central; convention over configuration (0.5)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"spring-boot","text":"Write the key parts of the pom.xml for the Spring Boot service pawcare-api (group ie.pawcare, version 1.2.0, Java 17) using the starter parent, a web starter, JPA starter, test starter and the Spring Boot Maven plugin. Briefly explain why the starters have no version, and what @SpringBootApplication and @RestController do.","outline":["<parent> spring-boot-starter-parent 3.2.x (1)","groupId ie.pawcare, artifactId pawcare-api, version 1.2.0, <java.version>17</java.version> (1)","Dependencies spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, spring-boot-starter-test with <scope>test</scope> (1)","spring-boot-maven-plugin in <build><plugins> for executable JAR (0.5)","No versions: starter parent dependency management supplies them (0.5)","@SpringBootApplication = @Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan on main class; @RestController returns JSON bodies from handler methods (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"git-commands","text":"List and explain TEN Git commands a PawCare developer would use, in order, to add the \"prescription reminder\" feature: from getting the code to the change being merged into main through a pull request.","outline":["git clone <url>: copy repository locally (1)","git checkout -b feature/prescription-reminder: create and switch to feature branch (1)","git status: see changed files (1)","git add . (or named files): stage changes (1)","git commit -m \"Add prescription reminder\": snapshot locally (1)","git pull --rebase origin main (or git fetch + merge): bring in teammates' changes, resolve conflicts (1)","git push -u origin feature/prescription-reminder: publish branch, set upstream (1)","Open pull request on GitHub: review and CI checks (1)","git checkout main, git pull, git merge feature/prescription-reminder (or merge the PR) and git push origin main (1)","git branch -d feature/prescription-reminder, git log --oneline / git diff as extras (1)","Each command must come with its explanation; accept equivalent commands (switch for checkout)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"scenario":"PawCare will ship pawcare-api (Spring Boot 3, Java 17) as a Docker image. The JAR is built with Maven to target/pawcare-api-1.2.0.jar. The service listens on port 8081 and reads the database address from the environment variable DB_URL.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"dockerfile","text":"Describe the steps you would follow to construct a Dockerfile for this service, then write the Dockerfile (single stage, JAR already built) with a one-line explanation per instruction. Also state what you would put in .dockerignore.","outline":["Steps: inspect project (pom.xml = Maven/Java), identify artefact target/pawcare-api-1.2.0.jar, choose Java 17 runtime image, decide files, config (DB_URL env var) and port 8081, start command, write, build and verify (2)","FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre (runtime only, pinned version, not latest) (1)","WORKDIR /app (1)","COPY target/pawcare-api-1.2.0.jar app.jar (1)","EXPOSE 8081 (documents the port; -p publishes) (1)","ENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"] (1)","No ENV with secrets: pass DB_URL at run time with -e (0.5)",".dockerignore: target/ (if building inside), .git, .idea, *.log, .env (0.5)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","text":"Diagnose each failure and give the fix. (i) `docker ps` shows nothing; `docker ps -a` shows Exited (1); `docker logs` says \"Error: Unable to access jarfile app.jar\" (the Dockerfile has `COPY target/pawcare-api-1.2.0.jar /app/pawcare.jar` and `ENTRYPOINT [\"java\",\"-jar\",\"app.jar\"]`). (ii) The container is Up, started with `docker run -d -p 8080:8081 pawcare`, but `curl localhost:8081` is refused. (iii) Logs show UnsupportedClassVersionError: class file version 61.0, this runtime only recognises up to 55.0.","outline":["(i) Cause: the file was copied as pawcare.jar but the entrypoint runs app.jar; fix COPY target (use `COPY ... app.jar`) or change ENTRYPOINT; rebuild, run; first commands docker ps -a, docker logs (2)","(ii) Mapping is HOST:CONTAINER = 8080:8081, so the app is on localhost:8080; use `curl localhost:8080` or run with -p 8081:8081; verify in docker ps PORTS column (2)","(iii) Built with Java 17 (61.0) but run on a Java 11 image (55.0); change base image to eclipse-temurin:17-jre and rebuild (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","text":"Give the Docker commands to: build the image as pawcare-api:1.2.0; run it detached as `pawcare` mapping port 8081 and supplying DB_URL; check it is running and healthy; view recent logs; open a shell inside; then replace it with version 1.2.1 and roll back if it fails.","outline":["docker build -t pawcare-api:1.2.0 . (1)","docker run -d --name pawcare -p 8081:8081 -e DB_URL=\"$DB_URL\" pawcare-api:1.2.0 (1)","docker ps; curl http://localhost:8081/actuator/health (1)","docker logs pawcare --tail 50 (0.5)","docker exec -it pawcare /bin/sh (0.5)","Upgrade: docker stop pawcare; docker rm pawcare; docker run ... pawcare-api:1.2.1 (1)","Rollback: stop/remove the failing container and run pawcare-api:1.2.0 again (versioned tags make it possible) (1)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"scenario":"PawCare is hardening and testing its prescription and booking services.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":3,"topicId":"sca-security","text":"A software composition analysis scan of pawcare-api reports a Critical vulnerability in a transitive dependency (jackson-databind) reachable from the public booking endpoint, with a fixed version available. Explain what SCA is, why a transitive dependency matters, and the steps to remediate safely.","outline":["SCA scans third-party and transitive dependencies for known CVEs (e.g. Dependabot, Trivy) (1)","Transitive: not declared directly, pulled by another dependency, still shipped and exploitable; check with mvn dependency:tree (1)","Remediate: branch, bump or exclude/override version in pom, run tests, PR review, CI re-scan, staged deploy with rollback (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"api-design-openapi","text":"Design the REST API for visits: list all visits, list the visits of one pet without breaking existing clients of `GET /visits`, and create a visit. Give methods, paths, status codes, and say whether your change to the list endpoint is additive or breaking and why. Write a short OpenAPI fragment for the pet filter.","outline":["GET /v1/visits (200 list); GET /v1/visits?petId=8 optional query filter (200, 400 malformed, 404 unknown pet) (1.5)","POST /v1/visits body {petId, date, notes}: 201 Created, 400 invalid, 409 slot taken (1)","Additive: new optional parameter keeps existing requests and responses; making petId mandatory would be breaking (0.5)","OpenAPI: paths: /visits: get: parameters: - name: petId, in: query, required: false, schema: type: integer; responses 200/400/404 (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","text":"Part 1 (5 marks): Identify what is wrong with this implementation (ignore syntax). Part 2 (3 marks): write TWO JUnit 5 tests for the corrected method.\n\npublic class PrescriptionService {\n  public static List<Prescription> cache = new ArrayList<>();\n  public Optional<Prescription> latest(Pet pet) {\n    return Optional.of(pet.getPrescriptions().stream()\n      .filter(p -> p.getExpiry().isAfter(LocalDate.now()))\n      .peek(p -> p.setViewed(true))\n      .sorted((a, b) -> a.getIssued().compareTo(b.getIssued()))\n      .reduce((a, b) -> b).orElse(null));\n  }\n}","outline":["Optional.of(...orElse(null)) throws NullPointerException when there is no active prescription; use Optional return of the stream result directly (1)","peek(p -> p.setViewed(true)) hides a mutation inside a read; may not run as expected and changes state just by querying (1)","LocalDate.now() hard-coded: not repeatable/testable; inject a Clock or pass the date (1)","public static mutable cache: global shared state, unused, breaks test independence (1)","Sorting the whole stream to take the last element is obscure and wasteful; use max(Comparator.comparing(Prescription::getIssued)); also possible null getPrescriptions (1)","Fix: `public Optional<Prescription> latestActive(Pet pet, LocalDate today) { return pet.getPrescriptions().stream().filter(p -> p.getExpiry().isAfter(today)).max(Comparator.comparing(Prescription::getIssued)); }`","Test 1 (1.5): `latestActiveReturnsNewestUnexpired`: Arrange prescriptions issued 1 Jan (expires 1 Dec), 1 Mar (expires 1 Dec), today 1 Jun; assertEquals(march, service.latestActive(pet, today).get())","Test 2 (1.5): `latestActiveEmptyWhenAllExpired`: all expiries before today; assertTrue(service.latestActive(pet, today).isEmpty())"]},{"label":"d","marks":5,"topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","text":"The PawCare online booking API has an SLO of 99.9% availability over a 30-day month. Incidents this month lasted 14 minutes, 9 minutes and 22 minutes. (i) Calculate the allowed downtime and the SLI, and say whether the SLO was met. (ii) Part way through the next month, after 15 days, 20 minutes of downtime have occurred: calculate the burn rate and interpret it. (iii) The customer SLA is 99.5%: how much downtime does it allow per 30 days?","outline":["Window 30 x 24 x 60 = 43,200 min; allowed = 0.001 x 43,200 = 43.2 min (1)","Downtime = 14 + 9 + 22 = 45 min; SLI = (43,200 - 45)/43,200 x 100 = 99.896% (1)","SLO not met (99.896% < 99.9%); budget 104.2% used, overspent by 1.8 min; freeze risky releases (0.5)","Burn rate = (20/43.2) / (15/30) = 0.463/0.5 = 0.93: slightly under 1, on track; at this rate budget lasts 30/0.93 = 32.4 days (1)","SLA 99.5%: 0.005 x 43,200 = 216 min allowed (1)","No SLA breach (45 < 216) although SLO missed (0.5)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock2","title":"Mock Paper 2: UrbanBikes Bike-Sharing","duration":120,"instructions":"Answer any 3 of 4 questions; all 20 marks. Where necessary, state any assumptions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"UrbanBikes operates 300 bike stations across a city. Customers unlock bikes with a mobile app and are charged per minute. The company is extending its existing rental system with an e-bike battery display, a loyalty scheme and station-capacity forecasting. Two Scrum teams (8 developers, 2 testers, 1 product owner) work on it.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"Explain Agile principles 3 (deliver working software frequently), 6 (face-to-face conversation) and 12 (regular reflection), and give one UrbanBikes example of applying EACH.","outline":["3: working software every couple of weeks, preferring the shorter scale; example: release the battery indicator to a pilot of 5 stations after sprint 1 (1.3)","6: face-to-face is the most effective communication; example: daily stand-up and pairing between mobile and back-end developers instead of long email threads (1.3)","12: team reflects at regular intervals and adjusts; example: sprint retrospective shows deployments are slow, so the team automates the pipeline next sprint (1.4)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"Station operators complain the forecasts are not what they need. You choose the principle \"business people and developers must work together daily throughout the project\". Suggest FIVE specific steps the team should take.","outline":["Embed an operations representative as product owner delegate, available daily (1)","Run joint backlog refinement to write forecast stories with measurable acceptance criteria (e.g. within 10% of actual demand at 90% of stations) (1)","Invite operators to every sprint review to try the forecast tool with real data (1)","Capture their feedback as new backlog items, prioritised in planning (1)","Retrospective to adjust data sources/models and re-measure the criterion next sprint (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"req-attributes","text":"For each statement name the attribute of a good requirement that is violated and rewrite it. (i) \"The app shall be user friendly.\" (ii) \"Bikes shall be unlocked using a Bluetooth 4.2 module written in Kotlin.\" (iii) \"The system shall record every ride and shall not store personal data about riders.\" (iv) \"Charging shall be accurate.\" State any assumptions.","outline":["(i) Unverifiable/ambiguous; rewrite: a first-time user shall unlock a bike within 60 seconds with no training in 9 of 10 trials (1.5)","(ii) Solution-specific (HOW not WHAT); rewrite: the app shall unlock a bike within 5 seconds of confirming the scan (1.5)","(iii) Inconsistent (contradictory: rides are linked to riders); rewrite: ride records shall be stored under a pseudonymous id and personal data retained per GDPR policy (1.5)","(iv) Unmeasurable/incomplete; rewrite: the charge shall equal the per-minute tariff times ride minutes rounded up, with 0 mismatches in 1,000 test rides (1.5)","Assumption stated, e.g. tariff and GDPR policy exist (credit within parts)"]},{"label":"d","marks":5,"topicId":"req-change-management","text":"Mid-sprint a city official requests: \"Show the battery level of e-bikes in the app before unlocking.\" Describe how UrbanBikes should handle this as a change request, including impact analysis, who decides, traceability and the tooling (GitHub Issues/Projects or Jira). State your assumptions.","outline":["Assumptions: product owner is change authority; battery data already exposed by bike firmware API (1)","Record as issue with original request; clarify requirement, acceptance criteria (level shown as percentage, refreshed within 30 s), open questions (offline bikes?) (1)","Impact analysis: firmware API, back end endpoint, app screens, tests, schedule; additive not breaking (1)","Decision by product owner; set priority separately from readiness (ready only after open questions resolved); may defer to next sprint to protect the sprint goal (1)","Implement via branch, PR and review; tests; trace issue-PR-test; board status (Raw, Clarified, Ready) (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"The UrbanBikes rental-api is a Spring Boot service. A new developer committed the following pom.xml and has also been struggling with Git.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"pom-xml","text":"Identify SIX problems in this pom and correct them.\n<project>\n  <modelVersion>3.0.0</modelVersion>\n  <groupId>com.urbanbikes</groupId>\n  <artifactId>rental-api</artifactId>\n  <packaging>war</packaging>\n  <dependencies>\n    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId></dependency>\n    <dependency><groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId><artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId><version>5.10.2</version></dependency>\n    <dependency><groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId><artifactId>lombok</artifactId></dependency>\n  </dependencies>\n</project>","outline":["modelVersion must be 4.0.0 (1)","Project <version> is missing (1)","No spring-boot-starter-parent (or dependencyManagement), so starter-web and lombok have no resolvable version (1)","Packaging war for an embedded-server app; should be jar (1)","junit-jupiter has no <scope>test</scope> so tests ship in the artefact (1)","No spring-boot-maven-plugin for an executable JAR; also no java.version property (1)","Corrected skeleton: parent 3.2.5, version 1.0.0, packaging jar, test scope, plugin"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"spring-boot","text":"Describe the layered structure of a Spring Boot application such as rental-api. Name the annotation used at each layer and give one reason constructor injection is preferred.","outline":["Main class with @SpringBootApplication in the root package (0.5)","Controller layer @RestController with @RequestMapping/@GetMapping/@PostMapping (1)","Service layer @Service: business rules such as fee calculation (1)","Repository layer: interface extending JpaRepository / @Repository; model with @Entity (1)","Constructor injection: final fields, explicit dependencies, easy to unit test with mocks without Spring (0.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"git-commands","text":"For each situation identify the problem and give the corrected commands. (i) `git commit -m Fixed station bug` fails with errors. (ii) `git push` on branch fix-bike says \"fatal: The current branch fix-bike has no upstream branch\". (iii) After `git pull` reports CONFLICT in StationService.java, the developer ran `git commit -am \"done\"` and the build now fails. (iv) `git push origin main` is rejected as non-fast-forward and the developer is about to run `git push --force`. (v) `git add .` was run and `.env` (with API keys) was committed.","outline":["(i) Message not quoted so git treats words as pathspecs; use git commit -m \"Fixed station bug\" (2)","(ii) New branch has no remote counterpart; git push -u origin fix-bike (2)","(iii) Conflict markers committed into source; open the file, choose/combine code, delete <<<<<<< ======= >>>>>>>, run tests, git add, git commit, push (2)","(iv) Remote has commits not present locally; do not force; git pull --rebase origin main (resolve conflicts) then git push; force push can erase teammates' work (2)","(v) Secret in history: git rm --cached .env, add .env to .gitignore, commit, rotate/revoke the keys immediately; rewrite history only if necessary (2)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"scenario":"The UrbanBikes team added this GitHub Actions workflow for rental-api (Java 17, Maven, Dockerfile in the repo root):\n\nname: ci\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: mvn package -DskipTests\n      - run: docker build -t ghcr.io/urbanbikes/api:1.0 .\n      - run: docker push ghcr.io/urbanbikes/api:1.0\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ssh deploy@prod \"docker pull ghcr.io/urbanbikes/api:latest && docker compose up -d\"","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":7,"topicId":"github-actions","text":"Explain what this workflow does and identify at least FIVE problems with it.","outline":["Trigger: only pushes to main; build job on Ubuntu runner: checkout, mvn package with tests skipped, build image tag 1.0, push; separate deploy job ssh-es to production, pulls latest and restarts compose (2)","No pull-request trigger/tests; -DskipTests means no tests ever run (1)","No docker login before push, so the push fails/denied (1)","Fixed tag 1.0 overwritten each build: no version/SHA traceability or rollback; deploy pulls :latest which is never pushed so manifest unknown (1)","deploy has no needs: build, so it runs in parallel/before the image exists (1)","Deploys straight to production with no staging, approval or rollback; no JDK pin, no cache, no scans (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":7,"topicId":"github-actions","text":"Write an improved workflow: run tests on pull requests; on merge to main build and push the image tagged with the commit SHA (and latest); then deploy to staging using the SHA tag. Explain the key steps.","outline":["on: pull_request and push branches [main] (1)","Job test: checkout@v4, setup-java@v4 temurin 17 with cache maven, mvn --batch-mode clean verify (1.5)","Job image: needs: test; if: github.ref == refs/heads/main; permissions packages: write; login ghcr with secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN (1.5)","docker build -t $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} -t $IMAGE:latest . and push both tags (1)","Job deploy-staging: needs: image; ssh deploy@staging \"docker pull $IMAGE:${{ github.sha }} && docker compose up -d\"; health check (1)","Explanation: PR tests stop bad merges, SHA is immutable and traceable, staging before production (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","text":"After the fix, the push step fails with \"denied: permission_denied: write_package\". (i) State two likely causes and fixes. (ii) Give Docker commands to confirm the image and tag exist in the registry and to roll production back to the previous SHA tag. (iii) Recommend one tagging rule.","outline":["Causes: token lacks write:packages permission (set permissions: packages: write or use PAT with that scope); not logged in or wrong username/registry; package belongs to another owner/org settings (2)","Confirm: docker manifest inspect ghcr.io/urbanbikes/api:<sha>; docker pull of that tag from a clean machine (1.5)","Rollback: docker pull ghcr.io/urbanbikes/api:<previous-sha>; update the compose image tag or run docker compose up -d with it; docker ps; docker logs; curl health (1.5)","Tagging rule: tag every image with the commit SHA (plus semantic version on release); never deploy a bare latest (1)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"scenario":"UrbanBikes is improving test quality and reliability of the rental service.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"jacoco-coverage","text":"A JaCoCo report shows: RentalService instructions 92%, branches 50%; PricingCalculator 100%, 100%; StationRepository instructions 40%. (i) Which class would you examine first and why? (ii) Explain what line and branch coverage mean and why 92% instruction coverage can sit beside 50% branch coverage. (iii) Where is the report generated from Maven?","outline":["Examine RentalService first (core business logic, half the branches missed) then StationRepository (low but maybe simple) (1)","Line coverage = % of lines executed; branch coverage = both true and false outcomes of each decision executed (1)","Every line can run via one path while the other outcome of each if never runs: e.g. one test enters every if (1)","mvn clean test (report goal bound to test) writes target/site/jacoco/index.html; coverage shows execution not assertion quality (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"api-design-openapi","text":"Design the REST endpoints for rentals: start a rental, end a rental, list a customer's rentals. Give methods, URIs (nouns), status codes and one security measure. State why the API-first approach helps the mobile and back-end teams.","outline":["POST /v1/rentals (201 Created, 409 if bike already rented, 404 unknown bike) (1)","PATCH or POST /v1/rentals/{id}/end (200, 404, 409) / PUT status (1)","GET /v1/customers/{id}/rentals (200, 401, 403 for another customer's data) (1)","Security: OAuth2/JWT, HTTPS, rate limiting, validation (0.5)","API-first: agree OpenAPI contract first, mocks let the app and back end work in parallel, fewer integration surprises (0.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"tdd","text":"Part 1 (3 marks): Using TDD, show the red-green-refactor steps for the requirement: \"The first 30 minutes of a rental are free; after that the fee is EUR 0.50 for each 15 minutes started.\" Give at least three tests with expected values.\nPart 2 (5 marks): Identify what is wrong with this implementation (ignore syntax).\n\npublic class RentalService {\n  private final List<Rental> rentals = new ArrayList<>();\n  public List<Rental> getRentals() { return rentals; }\n  public double fee(Rental r) {\n    long mins = Duration.between(r.getStart(), LocalDateTime.now()).toMinutes();\n    return mins > 30 ? (mins - 30) / 15 * 0.5 : 0;\n  }\n  public String report() {\n    return rentals.stream().map(r -> r.getUser() + \":\" + fee(r)).collect(Collectors.joining(\",\"));\n  }\n}","outline":["RED: write test fee(30 min) = 0.0 and fee(31) = 0.50, fee(45) = 0.50, fee(46) = 1.00, fee(60) = 1.00 with a stub returning -1 so it fails on the assertion (1)","GREEN: minimal code: if (mins <= 30) return 0; return Math.ceil((mins - 30) / 15.0) * 0.5; run tests pass (1)","REFACTOR: extract constants FREE_MINUTES=30, BLOCK_MINUTES=15, RATE; tests stay green; next cycle: negative minutes throws (1)","Flaw: getRentals returns the internal mutable list: callers can alter state (1)","Flaw: LocalDateTime.now() hard-coded: not repeatable/testable; pass duration or inject a Clock (1)","Flaw: integer division (mins - 30) / 15 truncates, so 31 minutes costs 0 instead of 0.50 (should round up per \"started\" block) (1)","Flaw: magic numbers 30, 15, 0.5, and double for money (use BigDecimal/cents); report mixes formatting with calculation, calls the time-dependent fee for every row so output is non-repeatable, and r.getUser() may be null (1)","Fix snippet: `long fee(Duration d) {...}` pure function; test as above (1)"]},{"label":"d","marks":4,"topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","text":"The rental service handled 12,500,000 requests in a 30-day month; 9,000 failed. The SLO is 99.9% request success. (i) Calculate the SLI. (ii) How many failed requests does the error budget allow, and how much remains? (iii) Is the SLO met and what should the team do?","outline":["SLI = (12,500,000 - 9,000)/12,500,000 x 100 = 99.928% (1)","Budget = 0.001 x 12,500,000 = 12,500 failed requests (1)","Remaining = 12,500 - 9,000 = 3,500 (72% of the budget consumed) (1)","SLO met (99.928% >= 99.9%); budget is healthy but 72% used, so continue releases with care and watch the burn rate (1)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock3","title":"Mock Paper 3: MediSupply Pharmacy Delivery","duration":120,"instructions":"Answer any 3 of 4 questions; all 20 marks. Where necessary, state any assumptions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"MediSupply delivers prescription medicines from pharmacies to patients. It is a regulated business: every change to prescription handling must be auditable. A team of 9 is adding refill reminders and a courier tracking page to the existing platform.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"State the four values of the Agile Manifesto. Which TWO are most at risk in a regulated setting such as MediSupply, and how can the team protect them?","outline":["Individuals and interactions over processes and tools; working software over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration over contract negotiation; responding to change over following a plan (1.5)","At risk: working software over comprehensive documentation (auditors need records) and responding to change over following a plan (approvals needed) (1)","Protect: generate documentation from the work (issues, PRs, test reports, OpenAPI) rather than writing separate documents (0.75)","Protect: lightweight change-approval step within the sprint, risk-based approvals, automated audit trail (0.75)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"agile-manifesto","text":"Principle 12 says the team reflects regularly on how to become more effective. Describe how MediSupply should run a retrospective after a sprint in which a refill-reminder bug reached production. Give four concrete actions.","outline":["Timeboxed retrospective at sprint end with whole team, blameless (1)","Gather facts: timeline, how the bug escaped, which tests were missing (1)","Identify root causes, e.g. no test for expired prescription path, review rushed (1)","Agree 2-3 actions with owners and dates (add regression test, add coverage gate, checklist item) and review them next retrospective (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"req-attributes","text":"Write THREE good requirements for the refill-reminder feature, each in a different category (functional, performance, security/regulatory). For each, name the attributes of a good requirement it satisfies and state your assumptions.","outline":["Assumptions: SMS gateway exists; reminders are opt-in; GDPR applies (1)","Functional: \"The system shall send an SMS reminder to an opted-in patient 3 days before the prescription refill due date\" - unambiguous, verifiable, necessary (1.5)","Performance: \"Reminders for 50,000 patients shall be queued within 10 minutes of the nightly job starting\" - measurable, feasible (1.5)","Security/regulatory: \"Reminder messages shall not contain medicine names and every send shall be logged with a timestamp for 7 years\" - consistent, traceable, testable (1.5)","Check each: atomic, not solution-specific, prioritised, traceable to a stakeholder need (0.5)"]},{"label":"d","marks":6,"topicId":"req-change-management","text":"The health regulator announces that from next quarter, prescriptions for controlled drugs may be refilled at most twice (currently three times). Describe the change management process MediSupply should follow, including impact analysis, prioritisation versus readiness, traceability and approval. State assumptions.","outline":["Assumptions: compliance officer is change authority; deadline is fixed by law (1)","Raise change request with source (regulator notice), rationale, and acceptance criteria (maximum of 2 refills for controlled drugs only) (1)","Impact analysis: rules engine, refill API, patient app messages, existing in-flight prescriptions, test data, audit reports; effort and risk (1.5)","Prioritisation: high priority because of the deadline and legal exposure, but readiness is separate: not ready until the in-flight prescription rule is clarified (1)","Approval by compliance officer and product owner; implement on a branch with PR review; tests; traceability issue to PR to tests to release notes (1)","Communicate to pharmacies and patients; update the requirement baseline and version history (0.5)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"MediSupply has a small plain-Java library, prescription-rules, that is built with Maven (not Spring Boot). Two developers have also edited the same file on different branches.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"pom-xml","text":"Write a pom.xml for prescription-rules (groupId com.medisupply, version 1.0.0, Java 17) with JUnit 5 and Mockito as test dependencies and the JaCoCo plugin configured to produce a report and fail the build if branch coverage is below 80%.","outline":["project root with modelVersion 4.0.0, groupId, artifactId prescription-rules, version 1.0.0, packaging jar (1)","properties: maven.compiler.release 17 and UTF-8 encoding (1)","dependencies junit-jupiter 5.10.2 and mockito-core 5.11.0 with scope test (1)","maven-surefire-plugin 3.2.5 so JUnit 5 tests run (1)","jacoco-maven-plugin 0.8.12 with executions: prepare-agent, report (phase test) (1)","check execution with rule element BUNDLE, limit counter BRANCH, value COVEREDRATIO, minimum 0.80 (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"spring-boot","text":"The team considers moving the refill API to Spring Boot. Explain what Spring Boot adds compared with plain Maven and Java, referring to starters, auto-configuration and the embedded server.","outline":["Starter dependencies: one dependency (spring-boot-starter-web) brings a compatible set of libraries; versions managed by the starter parent (1)","Auto-configuration: configures beans (DispatcherServlet, JSON mapper, datasource) from the classpath and application.properties (1)","Embedded server: Tomcat inside the JAR so java -jar runs the app, no separate server install (1)","Plus spring-boot-maven-plugin for an executable JAR; actuator health endpoints; @SpringBootApplication entry point (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"git-merge-conflicts","text":"Two developers changed PrescriptionRules.java on different branches. After `git merge feature/refill-limit` the file contains:\n\n<<<<<<< HEAD\nprivate static final int MAX_REFILLS = 3;\npublic boolean isApproved(Prescription p) { return p.isSigned(); }\n=======\nprivate static final int MAX_REFILLS = 5;\npublic boolean isApproved(Prescription p) { return p.isSigned() && !p.isExpired(); }\n>>>>>>> feature/refill-limit\n\n(i) Explain each marker and what Git is telling you. (ii) Resolve the conflict, stating your reasoning. (iii) Give the Git commands to finish the merge. (iv) Describe two ways to reduce such conflicts in future.","outline":["<<<<<<< HEAD to ======= is the current branch version; ======= to >>>>>>> is the incoming branch version; Git cannot decide automatically because the same lines changed differently (2)","Resolution: ask which MAX_REFILLS is correct (regulatory value; assume 3 for controlled drugs or confirm with the owner); keep the incoming isApproved with the expiry check because it is stricter and does not clash logically; delete all markers (3)","Resulting code: `private static final int MAX_REFILLS = 3;` and `return p.isSigned() && !p.isExpired();` (1)","Commands: git status; edit the file; mvn test; git add PrescriptionRules.java; git commit (message of the merge); git push (2)","Abort alternative: git merge --abort (0.5)","Prevention: short-lived branches, merge main often, small commits, agree ownership of files, review PRs quickly, use CODEOWNERS or pair on shared files (2)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"scenario":"MediSupply is containerising its Node.js tracking service (server.js, package.json, package-lock.json, listening on port 3000, needs DATABASE_URL and a writable /app/logs directory).","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":7,"topicId":"dockerfile","text":"Write a Dockerfile for the tracking service and explain each instruction. Follow good practice for image size, build cache and security.","outline":["FROM node:20-alpine: small, pinned base image (1)","WORKDIR /app (0.5)","COPY package*.json ./ then RUN npm ci --omit=dev: dependencies layer cached until package files change (1.5)","COPY . . after install so source edits do not invalidate the dependency layer; .dockerignore excludes node_modules, .git, .env (1.5)","ENV NODE_ENV=production; EXPOSE 3000 documents the port (1)","USER node: do not run as root (1)","CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] exec form; secrets passed at run time, not baked in (0.5)"]},{"label":"b","marks":7,"topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","text":"The container starts then keeps restarting. docker logs shows: \"Error: Cannot find module 'express'\". Later, after a fix, it shows \"Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000\". Explain the likely causes and the fixes, and list the diagnostic commands you would use.","outline":["Diagnose: docker ps -a (status Restarting), docker logs --tail 50 <name>, docker inspect, docker exec -it <name> sh (if it stays up) (1.5)","Cannot find module: dependencies not installed in the image: npm ci missing or node_modules excluded and never installed, or COPY order wrong or .dockerignore excludes package.json; fix Dockerfile, rebuild with docker build --no-cache (2)","EADDRINUSE: another process or container already uses host port 3000; list with docker ps and netstat; stop the old container or map a different host port: -p 8080:3000 (2)","Also check missing DATABASE_URL env (app exits on start): supply with -e or --env-file; check restart policy (1)","Verify after fix: docker ps shows Up, curl localhost:8080/health (0.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"docker-commands-troubleshooting","text":"Give the Docker commands to: (i) build and tag the image as 1.4.0 for registry ghcr.io/medisupply/tracking; (ii) push it; (iii) pull and run it detached on a server, mapping host port 8080 to 3000, passing DATABASE_URL, mounting a host directory /srv/logs to /app/logs, restarting on failure; (iv) remove stopped containers and unused images.","outline":["docker build -t ghcr.io/medisupply/tracking:1.4.0 . (1)","docker login ghcr.io then docker push ghcr.io/medisupply/tracking:1.4.0 (1)","docker pull ghcr.io/medisupply/tracking:1.4.0 (0.5)","docker run -d --name tracking -p 8080:3000 -e DATABASE_URL=... -v /srv/logs:/app/logs --restart on-failure ghcr.io/medisupply/tracking:1.4.0 (2)","docker container prune; docker image prune (or docker system prune) (1)","Verify: docker ps, docker logs tracking (0.5)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"scenario":"MediSupply is reviewing security, API design, code quality and reliability of its platform.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"sca-security","text":"A Software Composition Analysis scan reports: spring-web 5.3.17 (Critical, internet-facing); commons-text 1.9 (High, only used in an internal batch job); log4j-core 2.14.1 (Critical, used by the API); junit 4.12 (Low, test scope). (i) Explain what SCA does. (ii) Rank the findings for remediation and justify. (iii) Name two ways to automate this in the pipeline.","outline":["SCA inventories third-party dependencies (SBOM), matches versions to known CVEs, reports severity and fixed versions, and checks licences (1)","Priority: 1 log4j-core and spring-web (Critical, exploitable and reachable on the API), then commons-text (High but internal), junit last (Low, test only) (1.5)","Prioritise using severity, exposure, exploitability and whether a fix exists (SEBF-style reasoning) (0.5)","Automation: Dependabot or Renovate pull requests; OWASP Dependency-Check or Snyk step in GitHub Actions that fails the build on Critical (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":3,"topicId":"api-design-openapi","text":"For the endpoint POST /v1/prescriptions/{id}/refills give suitable status codes for success, bad input, not found, and unauthorised. A proposal makes the field `pharmacyId` mandatory in the request. Is this a breaking change and how should it be released?","outline":["201 Created (or 202 if processed asynchronously), 400 invalid input, 404 prescription not found, 401 unauthenticated / 403 not permitted (1)","Adding a mandatory request field is breaking: existing clients without it will start receiving 400 errors (1)","Release as v2 or make it optional with a default first, deprecate, announce, then enforce; document in the OpenAPI contract (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"code-critique-flawed-code","text":"Part 1 (5 marks): Critique this class (ignore syntax errors).\n\npublic class RefillService {\n  private PrescriptionRepository repo = new SqlPrescriptionRepository();\n  public void refill(String id) {\n    Prescription p = repo.find(id);\n    if (p.getRefills() < 3) {\n      p.setRefills(p.getRefills() + 1);\n      p.setLastRefill(LocalDate.now());\n      repo.save(p);\n    }\n    System.out.println(\"Refilled \" + p.getPatientName() + \" \" + p.getMedicine());\n  }\n}\n\nPart 2 (3 marks): Write a Mockito-based JUnit 5 test for the case where the prescription has already reached its refill limit.","outline":["Hard-wired new SqlPrescriptionRepository(): tight coupling, cannot mock; use constructor injection (1)","p may be null if the id is unknown: NullPointerException; handle not found (1)","Magic number 3, and silent no-op at the limit: the caller is never told the refill failed; throw a clear exception or return a result (1)","LocalDate.now() makes the behaviour time-dependent and hard to test; inject a Clock (1)","System.out.println logs patient name and medicine (personal health data, privacy breach) and is not proper logging; mixes concerns (1)","Test: `@Mock PrescriptionRepository repo;` when(repo.find(\"p1\")).thenReturn(prescriptionWith3Refills); new RefillService(repo) (1)","Act and assert: assertThrows(RefillLimitException.class, () -> service.refill(\"p1\")) (1)","verify(repo, never()).save(any()) and the refill count is unchanged (1)"]},{"label":"d","marks":5,"topicId":"sli-slo-sla-error-budget","text":"The order-API has an SLO of 99.95% availability over a 28-day window. Three outages occurred: 6 minutes, 11 minutes and 4 minutes. (i) Calculate the error budget in minutes. (ii) Calculate the SLI. (iii) Was the SLO met, and what percentage of the budget was consumed? (iv) The customer SLA promises 99.5%: how much downtime does that allow in 28 days and is the SLA breached?","outline":["Window = 28 x 24 x 60 = 40,320 minutes; budget = 0.0005 x 40,320 = 20.16 minutes (1.5)","Downtime = 6 + 11 + 4 = 21 minutes; SLI = (40,320 - 21)/40,320 = 99.948% (1)","SLO not met: 99.948% is below 99.95%; budget used = 21/20.16 = 104.2%, overspent by 0.84 minutes (1)","SLA 99.5% allows 0.005 x 40,320 = 201.6 minutes; 21 minutes is well within, so the SLA is not breached though the internal SLO is (1)","Action: freeze risky releases and prioritise reliability work until budget recovers (0.5)"]}]}]}]};
